BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:khoj-mozilla-counterstructures-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Khoj x Mozilla Foundation: Counterstructures Residency 20
 26 (Delhi)
DESCRIPTION:Counterstructures is Mozilla Foundation's cultural R&D residenc
 y\, in which creative technologists prototype alternatives to dominant tec
 h systems: cultural artifacts\, installations and tools that question and 
 reframe them. Each edition operationalises the findings of a regional part
 icipatory assembly into thematic tracks\; the Delhi edition\, hosted by Kh
 oj International Artists' Association\, works from Mozilla's assemblies wi
 th G5A in Delhi and Mumbai and runs on two tracks. RETAINING CULTURAL AUTH
 ORITY asks what happens when oral\, embodied or community-held knowledge i
 s converted into datasets and machine-readable forms\, which cultural mate
 rials become visible to AI systems and which do not\, and how communities 
 can retain authority over what is digitised\, interpreted\, reused or deli
 berately kept private. REMEMBERING AND THINKING WITH MACHINES asks where t
 hought resides when people think with machines\, at what stage human judgm
 ent enters the system\, and what cultural or professional knowledge is los
 t when judgment is standardised through automation. Proposals may respond 
 to one or both tracks and can take many forms\, but must critically counte
 r the questions the tracks raise rather than simply illustrate them. WHAT 
 RESIDENTS DO: prototype an artifact\, installation\, system\, artwork or t
 ool for public exhibition\; take part in listening\, reading\, workshops a
 nd facilitated critique\, testing ideas with peers and community members\;
  write a 750-word report on the project's development and insights for the
  Counterstructures report and online archive\; and present the work public
 ly. WHO IT IS FOR: creative technologists and multidisciplinary artists us
 ing technology as a medium for cultural inquiry\, bringing critical perspe
 ctives and research-driven practice. Experience with AI can be early-stage
  or advanced\, but SOME PROFICIENCY WITH CODING IS REQUIRED. ELIGIBILITY: 
 this Delhi edition is open to Indian nationals only. Earlier editions ran 
 with tiat in San Francisco (responding to Hollywood's 8 Rules for AI) and 
 with Rhizome in New York\, whose residents showed at the New Museum. APPLY
  through the Mozilla Foundation form at https://mozillafoundation.tfaforms
 .net/f/khojresidency\; the guiding principles for the call are set out in 
 the form. Deadline 13 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Khoj International 
 Artists' Association in collaboration with Mozilla Foundation\nLocation: N
 ew Delhi\, India. Weekly in-person sessions at Khoj. INDIAN NATIONALS ONLY
 .\nAward: Mozilla's Creative Futures: Counterstructures programme publishe
 s USD 2\,500 per resident (USD 2\,000 stipend plus a USD 500 materials bud
 get)\, along with weekly in-person work sessions\, guest lectures from pra
 ctitioners across art\, technology and community design\, and publication 
 of the finished project in the collective documentation report. CAVEAT: ne
 ither the Khoj call page nor the Mozilla application form restates a figur
 e for this six-week Delhi edition\, and the published package covers the t
 en-week format\, so confirm the amount with Khoj before applying. No appli
 cation fee.\nDuration: Six weeks\, 1 October to 12 November 2026\, running
  weekly in Delhi\, with final projects presented publicly 13 to 15 Novembe
 r 2026.\n\nMore: https://khojstudios.org/opportunity/open-call-counterstru
 ctures-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://khojstudios.org/opportunity/open-call-counterstructures-residen
 cy/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New Delhi\, India. Weekly in-person sessions at Khoj. INDIAN NATIO
 NALS ONLY.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:algorave-istanbul-open-call-artists@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Algorave Istanbul: Open Call for New Artists and Performe
 rs
DESCRIPTION:UNPAID COMMUNITY CALL. Algorave Istanbul\, the local node of th
 e international algorave scene (live-coded music and visuals performed for
  an audience)\, is looking for new artists to create\, experiment and perf
 orm with. The call names live coding\, sound\, visuals\, audiovisual perfo
 rmance\, and generative or reactive systems\, and says explicitly that it 
 is open to formats the organisers have not thought of yet. WHO CAN APPLY: 
 artists based in Istanbul. There is no stated experience requirement and n
 o selection process described. Applications close 30 September 2026. WHAT 
 IS OFFERED: performance opportunities and collaboration within the collect
 ive. No fee\, stipend or expenses are mentioned anywhere in the call\, so 
 treat this as unpaid unless the organisers say otherwise. APPLY through th
 e Google Form\, or email algoraveistanbul@gmail.com. The call is published
  in Turkish and English.\n\nOrganisation: Algorave Istanbul\nLocation: Ist
 anbul\, Turkiye. Applicants must be based in Istanbul.\nAward: UNPAID\, an
 d no figure of any kind is published. The call states no fee\, honorarium\
 , stipend or expenses\; it is an invitation to join a local live-coding co
 mmunity and perform with it. Listed here as a deliberate exception to the 
 desk's paid-only rule because it is one of very few live-coding openings i
 n the region. No application fee.\nDuration: Open-ended involvement with t
 he collective\; no fixed term or number of performances stated.\n\nMore: h
 ttps://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdboafDes-9bslmHhaHwO3jpEN4YgS1bxe
 3R3wUlYfzvBlNsA/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdboafDes-9bslmHhaHwO3jpEN4YgS
 1bxe3R3wUlYfzvBlNsA/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Istanbul\, Turkiye. Applicants must be based in Istanbul.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ctm-resynthesising-traditional-at-the-extreme-2027@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: CTM Festival: Resynthesising the Traditional 2027 Artisti
 c Lab (At the Extreme)
DESCRIPTION:Resynthesising the Traditional is CTM Festival's long-term init
 iative co-curated by Stas Sharifulla\, taking resynthesis (the computer-mu
 sic method of analysing a sound to extract its components) as a metaphor f
 or critically engaging with tradition and heritage\, against conservative 
 views of culture as frozen and untouchable. The artistic lab at its heart 
 is an open toolbox for artists connecting to the traditional outside exoti
 cisation\, cultural essentialism and uneven power dynamics. THEME\, AT THE
  EXTREME: this edition invites artists and practice-based researchers whos
 e work examines the ethical\, aesthetic and political implications of trad
 itions entangled with extremity: intensity\, transgression\, transformatio
 n\, marginality\, outsiderness\, endurance\, conflict and excess. The call
  explicitly looks past the usual noise\, violence and loud-popular-music t
 ropes and past masculine codings of the extreme\, toward current queer and
  feminist reframings\, treating extremity as a relational condition\, chos
 en or imposed\, produced between majoritarian centres and their minoritari
 an outsides. Guiding questions include what it means to follow a tradition
  to its extreme consequences\, who is allowed to push tradition to its lim
 its and whose practices get framed as inauthentic or dangerous\, what it m
 eans to practise tradition under war\, displacement\, repression or ecolog
 ical crisis\, and how traditions might offer ways of inhabiting extreme ti
 mes. Preference goes to work engaging technological\, bodily\, aesthetic a
 nd theoretical forms at that intersection\, especially in unexpected or ra
 rely recognised ways. WHAT HAPPENS: theory and practice workshops from the
  co-hosts with individual and group exercises\, closed 15 to 20 minute ora
 l presentations by each participant\, a solo public performance or present
 ation (15 to 20 minutes) at a free-entry event\, inputs from specialists s
 uch as artists\, researchers and technologists\, guided individual and gro
 up sonic improvisations\, field trips and site visits\, and feedback sessi
 ons with the co-hosts and curators. AFTERWARDS: participants may propose n
 ew artistic work or research\, solo or in collaborations formed at the lab
 \, to the artistic team\; selected works are shortlisted\, and up to two a
 re commissioned and presented at CTM in January/February 2028. ELIGIBILITY
 : an existing practice tied to (re)connecting with traditional ideas and s
 ounds through a contemporary lens and how that overlaps with the extreme\,
  plus clear ideas or questions about the next step you want to take. Appro
 aches may be technological\, instrument-focused\, compositional\, aestheti
 c\, research-oriented or otherwise. Working knowledge of English is recomm
 ended\, and any concerns about that can be flagged in the form. Up to six 
 participants are selected by co-hosts Karina Utomo (extreme metal vocalist
 \, composer and collaborative artist) and Luigi Monteanni (sound researche
 r and ethnomusicologist completing a PhD at SOAS on extreme metal in Bandu
 ng). The lab is a time-intensive full-day commitment and participants must
  agree to give the short public performance. APPLY through the form with a
  portfolio link (PDF\, max 10 pages\, public access\, works from the last 
 five years\, text kept minimal) and a link to a specific audio track or se
 ction (max five minutes) that best represents your current practice. CTM a
 sks that AI support rather than replace your own voice in the application.
  TIMELINE: deadline 28 September 2026\; participants announced mid to late
  October 2026. Questions to resynth@ctm-festival.de.\n\nOrganisation: CTM 
 Festival\, co-curated by Stas Sharifulla\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany. Open
  to artists worldwide\; solo artists and projects only.\nAward: Travel bud
 get of up to EUR 300 per person\, plus accommodation and catering for the 
 duration of the lab and a festival pass to CTM 2027 events. Visa fees are 
 supported where required\, and letters of invitation are provided for visa
  or funding applications. NO artist fee or stipend for the lab itself. Aft
 erwards\, up to two new works arising from the lab may be commissioned and
  presented at CTM Festival in January/February 2028. No application fee.\n
 Duration: Seven days: arrival 24 January\, lab 26 to 31 January 2027 (10:0
 0 to 19:00 daily)\, public presentations 26 January\, departure 1 February
  2027.\n\nMore: https://www.ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/resyn
 thesising-the-traditional-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/resynthesising-the
 -traditional-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany. Open to artists worldwide\; solo artists and pro
 jects only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-global-south-civil-society-microgrants-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: Global South Civil Society Microgrants 2
 026
DESCRIPTION:The Pulitzer Center funds civil society in the Global South to 
 build civic engagement projects around Pulitzer Center-supported journalis
 m: grassroots collectives\, community-led movements\, ARTISTS and civic co
 alitions taking published reporting to the communities it concerns. This g
 rant cycle is restricted to the Center's Climate and Environment topics: R
 AINFOREST (drivers of rainforest degradation\, the role of global markets\
 , finance and policy\, centring the rights and roles of Indigenous peoples
 )\, OCEAN (degradation of marine environments\, ocean health and the clima
 te crisis\, ocean governance\, science-backed and community-led solutions)
 \, and CLIMATE AND LABOR (the intersection of climate and labour\, includi
 ng gender\, livelihoods\, adaptation and community resilience). WHAT COUNT
 S AS A PROJECT: community engagement and organising that connects reportin
 g with audiences\; creative expressions\, explicitly including digital cam
 paigns\, public performances and creative publication formats\; public dia
 logues bringing together affected communities\, journalists\, decision-mak
 ers and academia\; and other innovative uses of the reporting that build o
 n local cultural practices. Every project must use at least one published 
 Pulitzer Center-supported reporting project meaningfully\, or involve a Pu
 litzer Center-supported journalist centrally\, and must name a clear targe
 t audience. OUT OF SCOPE: journalism and reporting projects themselves (th
 ose go to the Center's reporting grants)\, anything not built on Pulitzer 
 Center-supported reporting\, direct advocacy or lobbying\, campaigns endor
 sing political candidates or parties\, and for-profit projects. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: grassroots organisations\, community educators\, civil society organi
 sations and coalitions\, youth movements and other groups working at the i
 ntersection of journalism\, civic rights and active citizenship\, with pas
 t experience running civic engagement activities in the Global South\, pro
 ven experience on the named topics\, and the operational capacity to manag
 e a microgrant from an international organisation. APPLICATION: a project 
 description of no more than 400 words\, the target audience and desired im
 pact\, your methodology\, a timeline and budget\, the lead applicant's CV 
 with a letter of recommendation\, and\, where activities involve Indigenou
 s peoples or traditional communities\, a signed or recorded statement of c
 onsent from a community member. Applications are accepted in English\, Spa
 nish\, Portuguese\, French and Indonesian through Submittable. TIMELINE: a
 pplications 15 August to 6 September 2026\, selection announced mid-Septem
 ber 2026\, onboarding October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoca
 tion: Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and South
 east Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. Ci
 vil Society Microgrant instead.\nAward: USD 2\,000 to USD 4\,000 per proje
 ct. Applicants name the amount requested and submit a draft budget. Funds 
 are transferred to the partner after the agreement is signed\; partners fi
 le a financial narrative and outcomes report at the end but are NOT requir
 ed to collect detailed receipts. No application fee.\nDuration: Up to six 
 months of implementation\, October 2026 to March 2027\, with a virtual che
 ck-in in December 2026 and closure in early March 2027.\n\nMore: https://p
 ulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/global-south-
 civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/g
 lobal-south-civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and Sou
 theast Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. 
 Civil Society Microgrant instead.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-new-york-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: New York Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Asian Cultural Council's New York Fellowship puts a cross-d
 isciplinary cohort of artists\, scholars and arts professionals from Asia 
 in the United States for six months to live with a new cultural landscape:
  attending performances\, exhibitions\, workshops and discussions\, and bu
 ilding relationships with peers and with ACC's alumni network. It is delib
 erately an extended pause from daily work with NO obligation to produce or
  present anything. Applicants must be citizens or legal permanent resident
 s of an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region: Afghanistan\, Banglade
 sh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, China (Mainland)\, East Timor\, Hong Kon
 g SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\, Korea\, Laos\, Macau SAR\, Malaysia\, 
 Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Philippines\, Singapore\, Sri Lank
 a\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. and Vietnam. ELIGIBLE DISCIPLINES (16): a
 rchitecture (design\, history and theory)\, archaeology\, art history\, ar
 ts administration\, arts criticism\, conservation\, crafts\, curation\, da
 nce\, ethnomusicology\, film\, literature\, museum studies\, music\, theat
 er and visual art\; video art and photography apply under visual art. Addi
 tional requirements: at least 21 years old by the start of the fellowship\
 , at least five years of professional experience in an eligible discipline
 \, no paid work\, university coursework or teaching (online included) duri
 ng the fellowship\, and enough confident English to handle daily life inde
 pendently\, as the J-1 visa requires. Previous New York Fellows cannot rea
 pply\, and anyone who has held an ACC grant in the last two calendar years
  is ineligible. VISA: fellows must enter the U.S. on an ACC-sponsored J-1 
 visa with no exceptions\, which bars paid work of any kind during the fell
 owship\, including work for a non-U.S. employer and participation in ticke
 ted events even unpaid. WHAT ACC WILL NOT FUND: this is strictly cultural-
 exchange and immersion funding\, not production money. Filming a documenta
 ry\, researching a planned book or film\, mounting an exhibition\, running
  a concert series\, promotional or PR travel\, equipment purchases and ful
 l-time residency programmes are all ineligible\, as is funding used only t
 o travel to a single event. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9
 :59am ET\, applications close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters 
 of recommendation are due 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs Novem
 ber 2026 to April 2027\, notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funde
 d activity must take place between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. App
 ly through the GoApply portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Ap
 plicants may apply to only one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee
 .\n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: New York City a
 nd the wider United States. Open to citizens and legal permanent residents
  of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and regions in Asia\; U.S. citize
 ns and U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.\nAward: USD 38\,000 for 
 the six-month fellowship\, including a USD 3\,000 advance for travel costs
 . ACC deducts certain pre-arrival costs from the grant: the USD 220 SEVIS 
 fee\, mandatory travel health insurance (roughly USD 675 to 800) and Inter
 national House housing if you take it (roughly USD 19\,400 to 20\,600 for 
 six months). Fellows can expect USD 10\,000 to 31\,100 in hand after arriv
 al depending on insurance and housing choices. Also included: ACC-organise
 d group activities and institutional visits\, individual programmatic and 
 logistical support\, and entry to the ACC alumni network. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Six months\, in one of two cohorts: 1 January to 30 June 20
 28\, or 1 July to 31 December 2028. ACC assigns the cohort.\n\nMore: https
 ://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York City and the wider United States. Open to citizens and le
 gal permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and region
 s in Asia\; U.S. citizens and U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-individual-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: Individual Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Asian Cultural Council's Individual Fellowship funds artist
 s\, scholars and arts professionals from Asia and the U.S. to spend one to
  six months somewhere new or less familiar\, pursuing self-designed resear
 ch and cultural immersion: meeting practitioners\, visiting studios\, muse
 ums\, galleries\, theatres and libraries\, joining workshops and symposia\
 , and doing archival research. ACC is explicit that this is not a producti
 on residency\, not a conference travel grant and not a PR tour. APPLYING J
 OINTLY: two or three people who share research goals can apply together as
  equal partners (one primary applicant\, up to two co-applicants\, no assi
 stant roles)\; the USD 35\,000 cap covers the whole team. Applicants must 
 be citizens or legal permanent residents of an ACC-eligible country\, terr
 itory or region: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, C
 hina (Mainland)\, East Timor\, Hong Kong SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\,
  Korea\, Laos\, Macau SAR\, Malaysia\, Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakist
 an\, Philippines\, Singapore\, Sri Lanka\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. an
 d Vietnam. ELIGIBLE DISCIPLINES (16): architecture (design\, history and t
 heory)\, archaeology\, art history\, arts administration\, arts criticism\
 , conservation\, crafts\, curation\, dance\, ethnomusicology\, film\, lite
 rature\, museum studies\, music\, theater and visual art\; video art and p
 hotography apply under visual art. Additional requirements: at least 21 ye
 ars old by the start\, at least five years of professional experience in a
 n eligible discipline\, no university coursework or teaching during the fe
 llowship\, and confident English if travelling to the U.S. on a J-1 visa. 
 Anyone who has held an ACC grant in the last two calendar years is ineligi
 ble. DESTINATIONS: any eligible country except the one you currently live 
 in\; a place you have lived in for over a year or hold citizenship in need
 s a strong rationale. U.S. fellowships need at least 30 consecutive days a
 nd cannot start before 1 October 2027 because of J-1 processing\; fellowsh
 ips in Asia need 30 cumulative days\, splittable into trips of at least 14
  consecutive days with no more than 14 days between them. ACC sponsors the
  J-1 for U.S. travel\; for Asia you arrange your own visas\, and the fees 
 can go in the budget. WHAT ACC WILL NOT FUND: this is strictly cultural-ex
 change and immersion funding\, not production money. Filming a documentary
 \, researching a planned book or film\, mounting an exhibition\, running a
  concert series\, promotional or PR travel\, equipment purchases and full-
 time residency programmes are all ineligible\, as is funding used only to 
 travel to a single event. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9:5
 9am ET\, applications close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters of
  recommendation are due 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs Novembe
 r 2026 to April 2027\, notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funded 
 activity must take place between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. Apply
  through the GoApply portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Appl
 icants may apply to only one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee.\
 n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: Travel between an
 d within Asia and the United States. Open to citizens and legal permanent 
 residents of ACC-eligible countries\, territories and regions\, including 
 the U.S. You cannot propose travel to the place where you currently live.\
 nAward: Up to USD 35\,000\, the same cap whether you apply alone or jointl
 y with up to two co-applicants. Covers economy airfare\, ground transport\
 , accommodation\, food and daily living costs\, visa fees\, mandatory trav
 el health insurance\, interpretation and costs tied to the exchange activi
 ties. Also included: ACC logistical and research guidance\, introductions 
 to alumni and professionals\, and visa assistance for U.S. travel. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One to six months\, at least 30 days in the destin
 ation\; ACC prefers two months or more in a single place.\n\nMore: https:/
 /www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Travel between and within Asia and the United States. Open to citi
 zens and legal permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries\, territories
  and regions\, including the U.S. You cannot propose travel to the place w
 here you currently live.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-graduate-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: Graduate Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:ACC's Graduate Fellowship covers travel and living costs for a 
 small number of graduate students from Asia who come to the U.S. because c
 omparable arts and culture programmes are unavailable or underrepresented 
 at home. The five-year professional experience requirement that applies to
  ACC's other programmes is WAIVED here. Applicants must be citizens or leg
 al permanent residents of an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region: A
 fghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, China (Mainland)\, 
 East Timor\, Hong Kong SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\, Korea\, Laos\, Ma
 cau SAR\, Malaysia\, Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Philippines\,
  Singapore\, Sri Lanka\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. and Vietnam. ELIGIBL
 E DISCIPLINES (16): architecture (design\, history and theory)\, archaeolo
 gy\, art history\, arts administration\, arts criticism\, conservation\, c
 rafts\, curation\, dance\, ethnomusicology\, film\, literature\, museum st
 udies\, music\, theater and visual art\; video art and photography apply u
 nder visual art. Additional requirements: be pursuing a graduate degree in
  one of the 16 eligible disciplines\; be enrolled full-time and in residen
 ce at a U.S. graduate programme\, or hold proof of admission by 1 April 20
 27\; be in the coursework phase\, not the research or thesis phase\; and c
 omplete at least two consecutive semesters of full-time coursework within 
 the grant period. Part-time\, summer-only\, online and non-degree programm
 es are not eligible. ACC does not sponsor student visas\, so the F-1 or eq
 uivalent is between you and your university. Renewal for a second year dep
 ends on ACC's review of your academic record and proof of continued enrolm
 ent. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9:59am ET\, applications
  close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters of recommendation are d
 ue 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs November 2026 to April 2027\
 , notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funded activity must take pl
 ace between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. Apply through the GoApply 
 portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Applicants may apply to o
 nly one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee.\n\nOrganisation: Asia
 n Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: United States\, at the applicant's own
  graduate institution. Open to citizens and legal permanent residents of A
 CC-eligible countries\, territories and regions in Asia\; U.S. citizens an
 d U.S. permanent residents are NOT eligible.\nAward: USD 15\,000 for one a
 cademic year\, renewable once for a second year at ACC's discretion. Cover
 s travel and living expenses during the coursework phase only\, NOT tuitio
 n and NOT research costs such as fieldwork or equipment. Also included: in
 troductions into ACC's global alumni network. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: One academic year of full-time coursework (Fall 2027 to Spring 2028\, o
 r Spring 2028 to Fall 2028)\, renewable for a second year.\n\nMore: https:
 //www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States\, at the applicant's own graduate institution. Open 
 to citizens and legal permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries\, terr
 itories and regions in Asia\; U.S. citizens and U.S. permanent residents a
 re NOT eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asian-cultural-council-organization-grant-2027@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council: Organization Grant 2027
DESCRIPTION:ACC's Organization Grant funds arts and culture organisations i
 n Asia and the U.S. to run cultural-exchange activities for their staff\, 
 communities and collaborators: sending an activity lead and participants a
 broad\, hosting incoming participants at home\, or both. Up to USD 50\,000
 \, set by ACC against the proposed budget\, activities\, travel duration a
 nd application quality. ELIGIBILITY: the applicant must be a nonprofit\, a
  government-affiliated or sponsored arts or cultural institution (national
  museums\, performing arts centres and the like)\, or an academic or highe
 r-education institution\; legally registered and based in an ACC-eligible 
 country\, territory or region\; and have at least five years of artistic o
 r cultural programming experience. Each application names a primary contac
 t\, an activity lead who runs the work on the ground (can be the same pers
 on)\, and activity participants who may be staff or external collaborators
 . The lead and all participants must be citizens or legal permanent reside
 nts of an eligible location\, and they do not have to share a nationality 
 with each other or with the organisation. Applicants must be citizens or l
 egal permanent residents of an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region:
  Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, Brunei\, Cambodia\, China (Mainland)\
 , East Timor\, Hong Kong SAR\, India\, Indonesia\, Japan\, Korea\, Laos\, 
 Macau SAR\, Malaysia\, Mongolia\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Philippines
 \, Singapore\, Sri Lanka\, Taiwan\, Thailand\, the U.S. and Vietnam. ELIGI
 BLE DISCIPLINES (16): architecture (design\, history and theory)\, archaeo
 logy\, art history\, arts administration\, arts criticism\, conservation\,
  crafts\, curation\, dance\, ethnomusicology\, film\, literature\, museum 
 studies\, music\, theater and visual art\; video art and photography apply
  under visual art. TRAVEL: one to six months within the grant period\, at 
 least 30 consecutive days in the U.S. or 30 cumulative days in Asia (split
 table into trips of at least 14 consecutive days\, no more than 14 days ap
 art). Travel to a place the lead or participants have spent over a year in
  needs a strong rationale\, and multi-destination or short itineraries are
  less competitive. ACC does not sponsor visas for this programme\; the org
 anisation secures them. WHAT ACC WILL NOT FUND: this is strictly cultural-
 exchange and immersion funding\, not production money. Filming a documenta
 ry\, researching a planned book or film\, mounting an exhibition\, running
  a concert series\, promotional or PR travel\, equipment purchases and ful
 l-time residency programmes are all ineligible\, as is funding used only t
 o travel to a single event. TIMELINE: the portal opens 1 October 2026 at 9
 :59am ET\, applications close 10 November 2026 at 9:59am ET\, and letters 
 of recommendation are due 16 November 2026 at 9:59am ET. Review runs Novem
 ber 2026 to April 2027\, notifications go out mid-May 2027\, and all funde
 d activity must take place between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. App
 ly through the GoApply portal\; ACC does not accept email applications. Ap
 plicants may apply to only one ACC programme per cycle. No application fee
 .\n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council (ACC)\nLocation: Asia and the Un
 ited States. The applicant organisation must be legally registered and bas
 ed in an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region\; activity leads and p
 articipants must be citizens or permanent residents of an eligible locatio
 n\, not necessarily the same one as the organisation.\nAward: Up to USD 50
 \,000. Covers economy airfare\, ground transport\, accommodation\, food an
 d daily living costs\, visa fees\, mandatory travel health insurance\, int
 erpretation and costs tied to the exchange activities. Also included: ACC 
 logistical and substantive guidance\, introductions to alumni\, arts profe
 ssionals and institutions\, and invitations to local ACC events. No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: One to six months of travel\; at least 30 consecutiv
 e days for U.S. activity\, or 30 cumulative days in Asia.\n\nMore: https:/
 /www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Asia and the United States. The applicant organisation must be leg
 ally registered and based in an ACC-eligible country\, territory or region
 \; activity leads and participants must be citizens or permanent residents
  of an eligible location\, not necessarily the same one as the organisatio
 n.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:creative-nz-international-engagement-fund-2026-27-round-1@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Creative New Zealand: International Engagement Fund 2026/
 27 (Round 1)
DESCRIPTION:Creative New Zealand's International Engagement Fund pays for N
 ew Zealand artists\, practitioners and arts organisations to take part in 
 international opportunities: touring\, showcasing\, presenting\, exhibitin
 g\, collaborating and being seen by overseas presenters. Eligible costs ar
 e international travel and freight\, marketing and promotion\, and the tra
 vel\, accommodation and hosting costs of getting international presenters 
 in front of New Zealand work off-shore. Up to NZ$25\,000 per application. 
 ELIGIBILITY: New Zealand citizens or permanent residents\, and New Zealand
 -based arts organisations and groups\, who have a current international st
 rategy and are internationally ready. International presenters and cultura
 l agencies can also apply for a confirmed opportunity involving New Zealan
 d artists\, and for requests over NZ$5\,000 must contribute towards the co
 sts themselves. For any request over NZ$5\,000 the applicant must already 
 hold an offer or invitation to take part\; smaller requests are looser. PR
 OCESS: submit an expression of interest first (applicant details\, an elig
 ibility check and declaration\, and an outline of the international opport
 unity)\, then complete a full application if invited. Two rounds in 2026/2
 7\, both opened 17 August 2026: Round 1 closes 1.00pm NZ time on 10 Septem
 ber 2026 with results by 5 November 2026\, Round 2 closes 1.00pm NZ time o
 n 3 December 2026 with results by 28 January 2027. Requests up to NZ$5\,00
 0 may be decided sooner. Apply through the Creative New Zealand portal. No
  application fee.\n\nOrganisation: Creative New Zealand (Arts Council of N
 ew Zealand Toi Aotearoa)\nLocation: Aotearoa New Zealand\, for activity th
 at takes place internationally. Applicants must be New Zealand citizens or
  permanent residents\, or New Zealand-based arts organisations and groups.
  International presenters and cultural agencies bringing New Zealand artis
 ts or work to their own country may also apply.\nAward: Up to NZ$25\,000 p
 er application. Covers international travel and freight\, marketing and pr
 omotion\, and the travel\, accommodation and hosting costs of bringing int
 ernational presenters to see New Zealand work off-shore. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: Project-based\; funds one specific international opportunity
 .\n\nMore: https://creativenz.govt.nz/funding-and-support/all-opportunitie
 s/international-engagement-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
URL:https://creativenz.govt.nz/funding-and-support/all-opportunities/intern
 ational-engagement-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
LOCATION:Aotearoa New Zealand\, for activity that takes place international
 ly. Applicants must be New Zealand citizens or permanent residents\, or Ne
 w Zealand-based arts organisations and groups. International presenters an
 d cultural agencies bringing New Zealand artists or work to their own coun
 try may also apply.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:project-100-exhibition-residencies-2027-nelson@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:Deadline: Project 100: Exhibition Residencies 2027 for Curators and
  Recent Graduates (Whakatu Nelson)
DESCRIPTION:Project 100\, an artist-run contemporary gallery and project sp
 ace in Whakatu Nelson\, is programming its 2027 exhibition residencies in 
 two streams. RECENT GRADUATES EXHIBITION RESIDENCY (May 2027): open to art
 ists who graduated from a tertiary arts programme within the past two year
 s\, preferably from a New Zealand institution\, and who can independently 
 realise an exhibition or public programme off a strong conceptual proposal
 . CURATORS EXHIBITION RESIDENCY (November 2027): open to emerging and esta
 blished curators\, independent practitioners and collectives based in Aote
 aroa New Zealand\; previous curatorial experience is welcomed but not esse
 ntial. Solo and collaborative projects are both welcome. WHAT YOU GET: exc
 lusive use of the gallery for one month\, one month of accommodation in a 
 central-city apartment\, administrative and promotional support\, up to fo
 ur hours of technician support\, and a travel and food stipend of up to NZ
 $1\,000. The gallery takes no commission on sales. WHAT YOU BRING: residen
 ts run the gallery themselves\, keep it open during opening hours\, must b
 e based in Whakatu Nelson for the month\, and return the space to its orig
 inal condition. Note this is an exhibition residency for presenting an exh
 ibition or public programme\, not a studio residency for developing new wo
 rk\, though some practices can work from the gallery during the exhibition
 . A refundable NZ$400 exhibition bond is paid once you are selected and re
 turned after the exhibition. DEADLINE CONFLICT\, CHECK BEFORE APPLYING: Pr
 oject 100's own application guide (the updated curators and grads residenc
 y PDF linked from its proposals page) states applications close 31 August\
 , while the open-call listings carrying this residency state 14 September 
 2026 at midnight. Email Project 100 to confirm which applies before assumi
 ng the call is still open. Apply via the form on the Project 100 proposals
  page.\n\nOrganisation: Project 100\nLocation: Project 100 gallery\, Whaka
 tu Nelson\, Aotearoa New Zealand. Open to curators\, recent graduates and 
 collectives based in Aotearoa New Zealand\; residents must be in Whakatu N
 elson for the full month.\nAward: Travel and food stipend of up to NZ$1\,0
 00\, plus one month of exclusive use of the Project 100 gallery\, one mont
 h of accommodation in a central-city apartment\, administrative and promot
 ional support (including national promotion through Art Now)\, and up to f
 our hours of technician support. No commission is taken on sales. No appli
 cation fee\, but a refundable NZ$400 exhibition bond is paid by the select
 ed artist and returned once the exhibition wraps up.\nDuration: One month.
  Recent Graduates residency runs May 2027\; Curators residency runs Novemb
 er 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.project100.co.nz/takeoverthespace?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.project100.co.nz/takeoverthespace?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Project 100 gallery\, Whakatu Nelson\, Aotearoa New Zealand. Open 
 to curators\, recent graduates and collectives based in Aotearoa New Zeala
 nd\; residents must be in Whakatu Nelson for the full month.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:creative-nz-creative-impact-fund-2026-round-2@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260917
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260918
SUMMARY:Deadline: Creative New Zealand: Creative Impact Fund 2026 (Round 2)
DESCRIPTION:The Creative Impact Fund is Creative New Zealand's project fund
  for artists and practitioners making and presenting work with real reach:
  creating\, developing\, presenting\, touring or publishing across any art
 form. Two funding tiers\, up to NZ$50\,000 and between NZ$50\,000 and NZ$1
 25\,000\, with the larger tier held to a higher bar on impact and delivery
 . ELIGIBILITY: artists and practitioners who are New Zealand citizens or p
 ermanent residents\, applying as individuals or as a group of collaborator
 s. Arts organisations and groups cannot apply to this fund\; they are serv
 ed by the separate organisations funds. Sole traders and people who operat
 e through a company can still apply as individuals. Separate assessor pool
 s cover general arts\, Nga toi Maori and Pacific arts\, and applications a
 re peer-assessed. Deaf and disabled applicants can claim accessibility cos
 ts on top of the grant. Round 2 of 2026 opened 17 August 2026 and closes a
 t 1.00pm NZ time on 17 September 2026\, with results due 2 December 2026. 
 Apply through the Creative New Zealand portal. No application fee.\n\nOrga
 nisation: Creative New Zealand (Arts Council of New Zealand Toi Aotearoa)\
 nLocation: Aotearoa New Zealand. Applicants must be New Zealand citizens o
 r permanent residents. Arts organisations and groups cannot apply to this 
 fund.\nAward: Two tiers: up to NZ$50\,000\, or between NZ$50\,000 and NZ$1
 25\,000. Deaf and disabled applicants can claim additional accessibility c
 osts. No application fee.\nDuration: Project-based\, across the funded act
 ivity period.\n\nMore: https://creativenz.govt.nz/funding-and-support/all-
 opportunities/creative-impact-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://creativenz.govt.nz/funding-and-support/all-opportunities/creati
 ve-impact-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Aotearoa New Zealand. Applicants must be New Zealand citizens or p
 ermanent residents. Arts organisations and groups cannot apply to this fun
 d.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:creative-nz-international-arts-impact-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260921
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260922
SUMMARY:Deadline: Creative New Zealand: International Arts Impact Fund 2026
DESCRIPTION:The International Arts Impact Fund is Creative New Zealand's mu
 lti-year fund for established New Zealand artists\, practitioners and arts
  organisations building sustained international careers rather than taking
  a single trip abroad. Up to NZ$75\,000 per year for one\, two or three ca
 lendar years\, so a three-year award can reach NZ$225\,000. ELIGIBILITY: N
 ew Zealand citizens or permanent residents\, and New Zealand-based arts or
 ganisations and groups\, who can show substantial previous international e
 xperience and evidence of international demand for their work. Artists and
  practitioners are prioritised in the 2026 round. PROCESS AND DATES: a two
 -stage application. The expression of interest opened 24 August 2026 and c
 loses at 1.00pm NZ time on 21 September 2026\; those invited through submi
 t a full application by 1.00pm NZ time on 1 October 2026\; results are ann
 ounced 9 December 2026. The EOI is the operative deadline\, so treat 21 Se
 ptember 2026 as the date to hit. Apply through the Creative New Zealand po
 rtal. No application fee.\n\nOrganisation: Creative New Zealand (Arts Coun
 cil of New Zealand Toi Aotearoa)\nLocation: Aotearoa New Zealand\, for sus
 tained international activity. Applicants must be New Zealand citizens or 
 permanent residents\, or New Zealand-based arts organisations and groups.\
 nAward: Up to NZ$75\,000 per year\, for one\, two or three calendar years 
 (so up to NZ$225\,000 over three years). No application fee.\nDuration: On
 e\, two or three calendar years of international activity.\n\nMore: https:
 //creativenz.govt.nz/funding-and-support/all-opportunities/international-a
 rts-impact-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://creativenz.govt.nz/funding-and-support/all-opportunities/intern
 ational-arts-impact-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
LOCATION:Aotearoa New Zealand\, for sustained international activity. Appli
 cants must be New Zealand citizens or permanent residents\, or New Zealand
 -based arts organisations and groups.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:creative-nz-creative-fellowship-fund-2026-round-2@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Creative New Zealand: Creative Fellowship Fund 2026 (Roun
 d 2)
DESCRIPTION:The Creative Fellowship Fund buys artists and practitioners tim
 e: paid months to develop their practice and make new work\, with no requi
 rement that anything be finished or public at the end. NZ$25\,000 funds up
  to six months\, NZ$50\,000 funds up to twelve\, and the money can go to l
 iving costs\, materials and the resources needed to develop the work. ELIG
 IBILITY: New Zealand citizens or permanent residents\, applying as individ
 uals or as a group of collaborators\; arts organisations cannot apply\, th
 ough sole traders and people operating through a company can apply as indi
 viduals. ASSESSMENT: peer assessors weigh whether the proposal encourages 
 creative risk-taking and new ideas\, supports artistic growth and experime
 ntation\, advances your artistic goals\, and is timely for your career sta
 ge. NZ$50\,000 applications are additionally judged on knowledge-sharing a
 nd what the work does for the artform or community. Separate assessor pool
 s cover general arts\, Nga toi Maori and Pacific arts. No budget is requir
 ed in the application\, and Creative New Zealand explicitly permits AI too
 ls for drafting. Round 2 of 2026 opened 17 August 2026 and closes at 1.00p
 m NZ time on 24 September 2026\, with results due 2 December 2026. Apply t
 hrough the Creative New Zealand portal. No application fee.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: Creative New Zealand (Arts Council of New Zealand Toi Aotearoa)\nLocati
 on: Aotearoa New Zealand. Applicants must be New Zealand citizens or perma
 nent residents. Arts organisations cannot apply.\nAward: NZ$25\,000 for up
  to six months of work\, or NZ$50\,000 for up to twelve months. Covers liv
 ing costs\, materials and the resources needed to develop new work. Deaf a
 nd disabled applicants can claim additional accessibility costs. No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: Up to six months (NZ$25\,000) or up to twelve months
  (NZ$50\,000).\n\nMore: https://creativenz.govt.nz/funding-and-support/all
 -opportunities/creative-fellowship-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://creativenz.govt.nz/funding-and-support/all-opportunities/creati
 ve-fellowship-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
LOCATION:Aotearoa New Zealand. Applicants must be New Zealand citizens or p
 ermanent residents. Arts organisations cannot apply.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:creative-nz-early-career-fund-2026-round-2@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Creative New Zealand: Early Career Fund (Toi Tipu Toi Rea
 ) 2026 Round 2
DESCRIPTION:Toi Tipu Toi Rea\, Creative New Zealand's Early Career Fund\, g
 ives up to NZ$10\,000 to artists and practitioners at the early stage of t
 heir career to create work\, pick up new skills\, pay a mentor and share w
 hat they make with their communities. No professional track record is requ
 ired\, which makes it one of the few substantial public grants open to peo
 ple who have not built a CV yet. ELIGIBILITY: New Zealand citizens or perm
 anent residents who are early in their career\, applying as individuals or
  as a collaborative group. Established artists\, arts organisations and gr
 oups cannot apply. PROCESS: no budget is needed up front\; applicants answ
 er four reflective questions about their artistic goals and supply support
 ing material such as an artistic CV or work samples. AI tools are permitte
 d for drafting and refining the application. Peer assessors judge whether 
 the activity fosters artistic growth at a critical career stage\, with fin
 al decisions made by Creative New Zealand's Head of Investment. Round 2 of
  2026 opened 31 August 2026 and closes at 1.00pm NZ time on 1 October 2026
 \; results are announced 9 December 2026\, and funded activity must take p
 lace between 9 December 2026 and 31 December 2027. Apply through the Creat
 ive New Zealand portal. No application fee.\n\nOrganisation: Creative New 
 Zealand (Arts Council of New Zealand Toi Aotearoa)\nLocation: Aotearoa New
  Zealand. Applicants must be New Zealand citizens or permanent residents a
 t an early stage of their career. Established artists\, organisations and 
 groups cannot apply.\nAward: Up to NZ$10\,000. Covers making work\, gainin
 g new skills and paying mentors. Deaf and disabled applicants can claim ad
 ditional accessibility costs. No application fee.\nDuration: Activity must
  take place between 9 December 2026 and 31 December 2027.\n\nMore: https:/
 /creativenz.govt.nz/funding-and-support/all-opportunities/early-career-fun
 d---toi-tipu-toi-rea?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
URL:https://creativenz.govt.nz/funding-and-support/all-opportunities/early-
 career-fund---toi-tipu-toi-rea?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Aotearoa New Zealand. Applicants must be New Zealand citizens or p
 ermanent residents at an early stage of their career. Established artists\
 , organisations and groups cannot apply.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sat-research-creation-residency-arts-technologies-2026-28@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261018
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:Deadline: SAT: Research-Creation Residency Program in Arts and Tech
 nologies 2026-28 (Montreal)
DESCRIPTION:The Societe des arts technologiques (SAT) in Montreal runs a re
 search-creation residency program in arts and technologies for artists and
  collectives working in the performing arts and/or media arts. The residen
 cy funds a research-creation stage of a project in technological arts rath
 er than the finalisation of a finished work\, so the call weighs the explo
 ration process and the innovation in it above a polished outcome. Each sel
 ected project receives a CAD 10\,000 grant\, whether the applicant is an i
 ndividual artist or a collective\, plus access to SAT spaces and equipment
 \, technical and creative support from the SAT teams\, mentoring and train
 ing activities\, and documentation and archiving. Access to spaces and equ
 ipment is assessed against the project's needs and granted subject to avai
 lability. Travel\, meal and accommodation costs beyond the grant are not c
 overed. Open to professional artists and artistic collectives worldwide\, 
 at any level of experience and from any primary discipline\, provided the 
 profile and project suit immersive technologies\; collectives must submit 
 a joint portfolio showing they can carry out the project independently\, p
 articularly the visual and sound creation inside a dome. Each application 
 must include a form and supporting documents setting out the concept\, the
  technical solutions considered\, and what will be explored during the res
 idency. One application per project and per person\; incomplete applicatio
 ns are not evaluated\; use permanent\, downloadable links rather than WeTr
 ansfer or similar temporary services. Accommodations and alternative suppo
 rt documents (large print and so on) are available on request at residence
 s@sat.qc.ca. Deadline 18 October 2026. Apply through the Google Form linke
 d from the SAT open-call page.\n\nOrganisation: Societe des arts technolog
 iques (SAT)\nLocation: Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Sa
 int-Laurent\, Montreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and col
 lectives from anywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.\n
 Award: CAD 10\,000 grant per selected project\, for individual artists and
  collectives alike. Also included: access to SAT workspaces during the res
 idency periods\, access to equipment and software resources subject to ava
 ilability\, mentoring and training activities (on site and remote)\, creat
 ive and technical support from the SAT teams\, and documentation and archi
 ving of the work. Travel\, meals and accommodation beyond the grant are NO
 T covered. No application fee.\nDuration: Two months\, extendable up to th
 ree months depending on the project\; residencies run across the 2026-28 p
 rogram.\n\nMore: https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-pr
 ogram-in-arts-technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-program-in-arts-
 technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
LOCATION:Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Saint-Laurent\, M
 ontreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and collectives from a
 nywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:uj-artists-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: University of Johannesburg Artists in Residence 2027
DESCRIPTION:UJ Arts & Culture's annual residency gives artists a monthly st
 ipend and an institutional base at the University of Johannesburg for ten 
 months\, with the option of a remote rather than physical residency. Disci
 plines covered are visual and fine arts\, music\, theatre\, performance an
 d dance\, design\, film and television\, and literary arts. A virtual info
 rmation session with Pieter Jacobs\, Head of UJ Arts & Culture\, was sched
 uled for 17 August 2026 to walk applicants through the process. ELIGIBILIT
 Y: applicants must hold a relevant post-school qualification\, with prefer
 ence for master's and PhD holders\, and must not be affiliated with anothe
 r tertiary institution in South Africa. Preference has historically gone t
 o artists with African connections. APPLICATION: via the online form linke
 d from the UJ Arts & Culture residency page\; contact kmachoga@uj.ac.za or
  +27 11 559 1476 with questions. DEADLINE: 31 August 2026. No fee.\n\nOrga
 nisation: UJ Arts & Culture\, University of Johannesburg\nLocation: Johann
 esburg\, South Africa. Both physical and remote residencies can be applied
  for\, though office\, studio\, rehearsal and performance space is limited
  and not guaranteed.\nAward: Stipend of ZAR 12\,000 per month across a 10-
 month residency\, so roughly ZAR 120\,000 in total. No travel or accommoda
 tion budget on top of the stipend\, and studio or rehearsal space is limit
 ed rather than guaranteed. No application fee.\nDuration: 10 months during
  2027\n\nMore: https://arts.uj.ac.za/artists-in-residence/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arts.uj.ac.za/artists-in-residence/?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Johannesburg\, South Africa. Both physical and remote residencies 
 can be applied for\, though office\, studio\, rehearsal and performance sp
 ace is limited and not guaranteed.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:das-minsk-culinary-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260905
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260906
SUMMARY:Deadline: DAS MINSK Culinary Residency 2027
DESCRIPTION:DAS MINSK opened in 1977 as a terrace restaurant and now runs a
 s a museum of modern and contemporary art\, and its Culinary Residency bui
 lds on that history by treating cooking as a cultural and artistic practic
 e. From April to July 2027 the selected resident develops an original proj
 ect at the intersection of art\, food and society and presents it as part 
 of the museum's public programme\, opening up new perspectives on art\, fo
 od and social questions and actively engaging visitors. The residency give
 s space to develop new formats and culinary projects across art\, cooking\
 , food\, culture\, sustainability and social issues\, with the museum unde
 rstood as a place of exchange and encounter. ELIGIBILITY: chefs\, artists\
 , food designers and interdisciplinary practitioners whose work engages qu
 estions of community\, memory\, hospitality\, ecology and the future of ea
 ting\; applicants from diverse backgrounds and perspectives are explicitly
  encouraged. SUPPORT: EUR 1\,000 per month\, furnished apartment\, travel 
 reimbursement to the start of the residency\, a Deutschlandticket\, possib
 le coverage of production costs\, museum access and team support. APPLICAT
 ION: a single PDF (max 10 pages\, 5 MB) with a short biography\, portfolio
  or work samples\, a project description (max 2 pages) and a cost estimate
  (max 1 page)\, emailed to residency@dasminsk.de. DEADLINE: 5 September 20
 26\, 11:59pm. Results expected November or December 2026. No fee.\n\nOrgan
 isation: DAS MINSK Kunsthaus in Potsdam\nLocation: Potsdam\, Germany (Max-
 Planck-Strasse 17\, 14473 Potsdam). In-person residency at the museum\; op
 en internationally.\nAward: Stipend of EUR 1\,000 per month for four month
 s (EUR 4\,000 total)\, plus a furnished apartment (accommodation for up to
  three people\, so family can join\, though their travel and living costs 
 are not covered)\, reimbursement of travel expenses to the start of the re
 sidency\, a Deutschlandticket for the duration\, and the possibility of pr
 oduction costs for the proposed project being covered. Access to the museu
 m's exhibitions and relevant work areas\, and support from the museum team
 . One residency awarded. No application fee.\nDuration: Four months\, Apri
 l to July 2027\n\nMore: https://dasminsk.de/en/residency?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://dasminsk.de/en/residency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Potsdam\, Germany (Max-Planck-Strasse 17\, 14473 Potsdam). In-pers
 on residency at the museum\; open internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:goldrausch-kuenstlerinnenprojekt-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Goldrausch Kuenstlerinnenprojekt 2027 (FLINTA Professiona
 l Development Programme)
DESCRIPTION:Run since 1989 by Goldrausch Frauennetzwerk e. V. to counteract
  the structural discrimination faced by FLINTA artists\, the Goldrausch Ku
 enstlerinnenprojekt is a year-long professional development programme for 
 15 Berlin-based visual artists. The participants' own artistic practice is
  the starting point: weekly courses on two fixed days\, individual appoint
 ments and three to four intensive weeks combine practice-oriented learning
  with insight into how the art world works\, plus continuous training in p
 resentation skills and communication strategy. The curriculum covers the d
 esign of an individual artist website\, studio talks and group critiques\,
  the fundamentals of self-employment\, public relations and project planni
 ng\, and culminates in a published catalogue and a group exhibition in the
  autumn. Seminars are led by art historians and cultural scholars\, with c
 urators\, gallerists and other arts professionals invited for peer-to-peer
  exchange. The alumnae network now runs to more than 525 graduates. ELIGIB
 ILITY: FLINTA visual artists of any age working in any field\, preferably 
 with a fine arts degree or equivalent experience\; must be based in Berlin
 \; German and English sufficient to follow group discussion is required\; 
 an active practice with clear goals and the capacity to devote time to com
 munication work alongside studio practice. APPLICATION: a written applicat
 ion in German or English\, submitted via the open call documents on the Go
 ldrausch site. A two-stage external jury of experts first shortlists on th
 e written applications\, then selects the 15 participants through small gr
 oup interviews. Selection criteria are the quality of the artistic work an
 d how meaningfully the programme complements the applicant's professional 
 practice. DEADLINE: 15 September 2026. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Goldrausch
  Frauennetzwerk e. V.\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany. Applicants must be base
 d in Berlin and able to attend in person on two fixed weekdays plus three 
 to four intensive weeks across the year.\nAward: Fully funded twelve-month
  professional development programme\, co-funded by the European Union (Eur
 opean Social Fund Plus) and the Federal State of Berlin\, so participants 
 pay nothing: there is no course fee and no application fee. 15 places are 
 awarded per year. The programme covers the production of a printed catalog
 ue\, a group exhibition\, an individually designed artist website\, weekly
  seminars\, and one-to-one appointments with art historians\, curators and
  gallerists. No cash stipend or production budget is paid to participants.
 \nDuration: 12 months\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https://goldraus
 ch.org/en/open-call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
URL:https://goldrausch.org/en/open-call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany. Applicants must be based in Berlin and able to a
 ttend in person on two fixed weekdays plus three to four intensive weeks a
 cross the year.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iac-bc-ai-builders-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260822
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Builders Fellowship 2026 (Internet Archive Canada x BC
  + AI)
DESCRIPTION:A $5\,000 fellowship to spend six weeks turning a very large Ca
 nadian public archive into something new. THE MATERIAL: Internet Archive C
 anada's Government Publications collection\, part of the Democracy's Libra
 ry project\, holding over 104\,000 text items\, roughly 22.1 million scann
 ed pages and about 48.25 TB of Canadian public-record material\, browsable
  at archive.org/details/governmentpublications. WHAT TO BUILD: deliberatel
 y open-ended. The call names a search tool that answers a real question wi
 th page-level citations\, a timeline tracing one policy across decades of 
 government language\, and a sonic or visual piece built from archival mate
 rial\, but explicitly invites work nobody has thought of yet. The requirem
 ents are that it is useful\, that it is imaginative\, and that the code is
  open source when it is done. ORIGIN: the fellowship came out of conversat
 ions between BC + AI's Kris Kruger and IAC executive director Andrea Mills
  about treating a public archive as material for new work rather than only
  something to preserve. IAC brings the archive\, BC + AI brings a communit
 y of builders used to working in public. ELIGIBILITY: anyone\, anywhere in
  the world\; one builder is selected. APPLICATION: a specific project desc
 ription grounded in the collection\, a phase-by-phase work plan for the si
 x weeks\, and a success metric\, submitted at bc-ai.ca/fellowship/apply. K
 EY DATES: applications close 21 August 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific\; fellow se
 lected by 31 August\; build runs 1 September to mid-October\; showcase at 
 Futureproof Festival in Vancouver on 28 October 2026 if the project is rea
 dy.\n\nOrganisation: Internet Archive Canada (IAC) in partnership with BC 
 + AI\nLocation: Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional 
 in-person showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 Oct
 ober 2026 if the project is ready.\nAward: $5\,000 award for one selected 
 builder. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-week build\, 1 September to mi
 d-October 2026\, remote\, with support from BC + AI throughout\n\nMore: ht
 tps://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional in-perso
 n showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 October 202
 6 if the project is ready.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:darc-transmediale-interstices-research-workshop-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: transmediale x DARC: Interstices\, Research (in) the Gaps
 \, PhD/Research Workshop
DESCRIPTION:The annual DARC PhD/Research Workshop\, organised by the Digita
 l Aesthetics Research Center at Aarhus University with transmediale\, resp
 onds to transmediale's 2027 thematic framework. THEME: 'Interstices: Resea
 rch (in) the gaps'. An interstice is the small space between objects set c
 losely together\, the cords in a rope or the atoms in a crystal lattice: a
 n emptiness produced by the structure it belongs to\, and without which th
 at structure could not be what it is. In crystallography\, interstitial si
 tes are the spaces a lattice and its basis generate but do not fill\, and 
 interstitial diffusion\, the movement of small atoms through the gaps of a
  host lattice\, is the mechanism by which materials transform. The call as
 ks what the equivalent sites are in social\, cultural and computational st
 ructures: where are the gaps these combinations generate but do not fill\,
  what moves through them\, and do they carry transformative power? It work
 s through Yancey Strickler's dark forest theory of the internet (the withd
 rawal of meaningful exchange from the open\, surveilled\, indexed web into
  private channels)\, the Dark Forest Collective's anthology and its 'cozyw
 eb' of small sheltered spaces\, dark nets\, miniverses and moving castles 
 as interstitial spaces produced by and defined against the dominant web\, 
 and Bogna Konior's extension of the dark forest metaphor to the opacity of
  AI systems\, where silence is refusal rather than absence and the forest 
 is increasingly inhabited by other-than-human intelligences that opt out o
 f engagement with people. Where big tech pursues efficiency through the au
 tomation of human perception\, cognition\, identification and aesthetics\,
  artists and researchers explore the non/more-than-human\, glitchy\, noisy
 \, machinic\, datafied and dark to build new expression and critique of da
 ta-ism and what is marketed as artificial intelligence\, including the dan
 gers and potentials inside badly curated data models\, between polluting d
 ata centres\, and in skeuomorphic interfaces. WHAT THEY WANT: proposals fr
 om artists\, researchers and practitioners for work that occupies and move
 s through gaps that are social as well as structural\, ontological and mat
 erial. The gaps are physical too: the heat of data centres and their thirs
 t for water\, latency in the cables\, unindexed nodes. The call invites sp
 eculations\, practical interventions and inferences that fill the gaps and
  expand the diversity of possible communications and communities that can 
 inhabit dark forests\, including those built on\, against and beneath the 
 infrastructures underlying them\, and welcomes work on the (dis-)continuou
 s histories of technological exploration of computational infrastructures 
 over the last 30 years or longer\, and on how current artistic\, theoretic
 al and technical explorations repeat or break from them. WHO: PhD candidat
 es and early-career researchers\, with submissions welcome from researcher
 s inside and outside the academy\, including artists\, designers and curat
 ors. PARTICIPANT EXPECTATIONS: participants write a paper and present it a
 t the workshop\, and on the basis of that peer review shorten the research
  into a newspaper-length article for distribution at the transmediale fest
 ival. The workshop is led by scholars from DARC at Aarhus University. DEAD
 LINE: 28 August 2026\, 23:59 CEST\, via the application link on the call p
 age. Queries to Pablo Velasco\, pvelasco[at]cc.au.dk.\n\nOrganisation: Dig
 ital Aesthetics Research Center (DARC)\, Aarhus University\, in collaborat
 ion with transmediale\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alon
 gside transmediale 2027 (festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to research
 ers based anywhere\, inside or outside the academy.\nAward: No submission 
 fee. The published call does not state a participation fee\, honorarium\, 
 travel or accommodation support\, so confirm with the organisers before bu
 dgeting. Accepted participants present a paper at the workshop and their c
 ondensed text is distributed as a newspaper-style article at the festival.
 \nDuration: Research workshop held around transmediale 2027 in Berlin (fes
 tival 26 to 31 January 2027)\n\nMore: https://transmediale.de/en/news/open
 -call-for-darc-phd-research-workshop-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://transmediale.de/en/news/open-call-for-darc-phd-research-worksho
 p-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alongside transmediale 2027 (
 festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to researchers based anywhere\, insi
 de or outside the academy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iclc-2027-antwerp-call-for-participation@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: ICLC 2027: International Conference on Live Coding\, Call
  for Participation (Dialogues)
DESCRIPTION:The International Conference on Live Coding is dedicated to pra
 ctices and research focused on technologies and philosophies that interpre
 t the use of computer code and the public writing of algorithms as perform
 ative gestures. The 10th edition runs 9 to 13 March 2027 in Antwerp\, host
 ed by Lambdasonic (TOPLAP Belgium) with the Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp
  and De Singel. THEME: 'Dialogues'\, framing live coding as continuous imp
 rovisational exchange between coder\, machine and audience\, and across di
 sciplines. Suggested topics include dialogues between live coding and acou
 stic musicians\, dancers\, theatre\, classically trained musicians reading
  live-coded (or graphical) scores\, band and jazz-combo settings\, live-co
 der ensembles\, ten-minute live-coded mini-operas\, DMX lighting systems\,
  graphics and shader live coding\, robotics (pen plotters\, textile-weavin
 g robots)\, design-as-performance\, community building\, diversity/equity/
 inclusion\, accessibility of live coding languages and interfaces\, educat
 ion\, and algorave. The venues will facilitate collaborations with residen
 t dancers\, a jazz combo (back-line provided: drums\, guitar amp\, bass am
 p\, piano)\, a string quartet reading scores a prima vista\, visual artist
 s\, students and researchers where possible. FORMATS: short papers (2-4 pa
 ges) and long papers (5-12 pages) using the provided template\; live perfo
 rmances (800-word abstract\, max 150-word programme note\, technical rider
 )\; workshops (short 2h or long 4h\, max 700-word abstract\, held in Engli
 sh or Dutch)\; installations (max 700-word abstract\, technical questionna
 ire plus a measured plan or sketch\, with the interactive aspect clearly d
 efined)\; and satellite events. All contributors submit a max 150-word bio
 graphy. Peer review is single-blind with at least two reviewers per submis
 sion. LANGUAGE: submissions must be in English\, except that live performa
 nce\, installation and workshop proposals may also be submitted in another
  language (workshops: English\, Dutch or French) with a machine-translated
  English version\, stated as such. Substantial use of generative AI in wri
 ting papers and performance abstracts must be disclosed\; proofreading and
  translation are exempt. In-person presentation is encouraged\, with remot
 e proposals considered and sessions live-streamed where possible\; the org
 anisers ask attendees to minimise travel impact and to link up with nearby
  satellite events to turn a long trip into a mini-tour. The call notes att
 ention to access needs\, care responsibilities and other barriers to parti
 cipation. SATELLITE EVENTS: live coding nodes\, art schools and other orga
 nisations worldwide are invited to host their own events before or after I
 CLC 2027\; submit a declaration of interest via Fourwaves before 1 Februar
 y 2027 with a principal contact\, city and any confirmed details\, and joi
 n #iclc-satellite-events on the TOPLAP Discord. Hosts are asked to seek lo
 cal funding for accommodation\, travel and artist fees\; the committee can
 not fund or organise these but will coordinate dates and publish your even
 t. TO APPLY: submit via Fourwaves at https://event.fourwaves.com/iclc2027/
 pages using the templates at https://codeberg.org/iclc/iclc-templates (rea
 d the README for full proposal instructions). Submission deadline extended
  to 1 September 2026\; acceptance notification 1 November 2026. CALL FOR R
 EVIEWERS: anyone active in a live-coding-related field can volunteer by em
 ailing a short description and keywords of their expertise to iclc@lambdas
 onic.be\, the same address for general questions.\n\nOrganisation: Lambdas
 onic (TOPLAP Belgium)\, Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp and De Singel\nLoca
 tion: Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp and De Singel International Arts Cent
 er\, Antwerp\, Belgium. Open to anyone engaged with live coding\; remote p
 articipation considered.\nAward: NO submission fee. The call does not stat
 e artist fees\, travel support or accommodation for accepted contributors\
 , so budget for self-funded attendance unless the organisers announce othe
 rwise. Proceedings are published with a DOI per article. Satellite-event h
 osts can request a Letter of Intent to support their own local funding app
 lications.\nDuration: Five-day conference\, 9 to 13 March 2027. Workshops 
 are 2 hours (short) or 4 hours (long).\n\nMore: https://iclc.toplap.org/20
 27/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://iclc.toplap.org/2027/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
LOCATION:Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp and De Singel International Arts Ce
 nter\, Antwerp\, Belgium. Open to anyone engaged with live coding\; remote
  participation considered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-voucher-art-event-mexico-art-week-2027@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Voucher Art Event\, Mexico Art Week 2027
DESCRIPTION:A Mondriaan Fund voucher sending visual artists to Mexico Art W
 eek 2027 in Mexico City\, built around the Zona Maco art fair. The voucher
  covers admission to the Zona Maco preview days\, a curated programme duri
 ng the week\, a networking event with the Dutch Embassy and hotel accommod
 ation from 2 to 7 February 2027\, plus a EUR 1\,300 contribution to travel
  costs. Fifteen vouchers are available. ELIGIBILITY: visual artists from t
 he Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom with at least one year
  of professional practice. Applicants may not be enrolled in a bachelor's\
 , master's or PhD programme\, with an exception for part-time programmes u
 nder 20 hours per week. CONDITIONS: the artist must be available to stay i
 n Mexico City from 2 to 7 February 2027 and take part in all scheduled act
 ivities\, and must submit a written evaluation after the visit. APPLY thro
 ugh the Mondriaan Fund online system with a CV (maximum three pages)\, a m
 otivation statement addressing your international ambitions\, the relevanc
 e of Mexico Art Week to your practice and your networking strategy\, bank 
 details and\, if applicable\, a BRP extract. Deadline 10 September 2026 be
 fore 16:00 Dutch time / 10:00 Caribbean time.\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan F
 und\nLocation: Applicants must be based in the Netherlands or the Caribbea
 n part of the Kingdom. The event takes place in Mexico City\, Mexico.\nAwa
 rd: A voucher covering admission to the Zona Maco preview days\, a curated
  programme during Mexico Art Week 2027\, a networking event with the Dutch
  Embassy\, and hotel accommodation from 2 to 7 February 2027\, plus a EUR 
 1\,300 contribution towards travel costs. 15 vouchers are available. No ap
 plication fee.\nDuration: Mexico Art Week 2027 runs 3 to 7 February 2027\;
  the artist must be available to stay in Mexico City from 2 to 7 February 
 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/v
 oucher-art-event-mexico-art-week-2027/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/voucher-art-e
 vent-mexico-art-week-2027/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
LOCATION:Applicants must be based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part 
 of the Kingdom. The event takes place in Mexico City\, Mexico.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-international-collaborative-projects-heritage-institutes-2026
 @artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: International Collaborative Projects with
  Heritage Institutes (September 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:This scheme funds cultural heritage institutions in the Netherl
 ands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom that want to collaborate with f
 oreign heritage institutions on research projects in shared collection are
 as\, including art historical and provenance research. A development phase
  can also be funded\, covering travel\, accommodation and presentation cos
 ts while the collaboration is being shaped. CONDITIONS: the applicant inst
 itution must be publicly accessible and manage nationally significant cult
 ural heritage\; it must partner with comparable institutions abroad\; the 
 research must be presented by both the Dutch and the international partner
 s\; the applicant must contribute significantly to financing the project\;
  and the international partners' share of costs must be proportional to th
 e Mondriaan Fund grant. AMOUNTS: up to 40% of variable project costs for t
 he main project\, or up to 70% of variable costs capped at EUR 15\,000 for
  development research. APPLY through the Mondriaan Fund online application
  system after registering an account. Deadline 10 September 2026 before 16
 :00 Dutch time / 10:00 Caribbean time (an earlier 28 May 2026 round has pa
 ssed).\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund\nLocation: Applicant institutions m
 ust be based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom\; the
  partner institutions are abroad.\nAward: Determined per application. Up t
 o 40% of variable project costs. For a development research phase\, up to 
 70% of variable costs capped at EUR 15\,000\, covering travel\, accommodat
 ion and presentation costs. The applicant must contribute significantly to
  the project financing\, and the international partners' cost share must b
 e proportional to the Mondriaan Fund grant. No application fee.\nDuration:
  Project-based research collaboration. Two annual rounds\; this card cover
 s the 10 September 2026 deadline.\n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/e
 n/apply-for-a-grant/grants/international-collaborative-projects-with-herit
 age-institutes/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/international
 -collaborative-projects-with-heritage-institutes/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicant institutions must be based in the Netherlands or the Car
 ibbean part of the Kingdom\; the partner institutions are abroad.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-heritage-innovation-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260917
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260918
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Heritage Innovation (September 2026 round
 )
DESCRIPTION:Heritage Innovation funds projects and research tracks that con
 tribute to substantive innovation in the heritage sector. Examples given b
 y the fund include developing new narratives about cultural heritage\, str
 engthening regional collaboration across the sector\, redesigning how a co
 llection is presented\, and feasibility studies on technological applicati
 ons such as AI or gamification\, which makes this one of the more tech-adj
 acent Mondriaan schemes. WHO CAN APPLY: museums and collection-holding cul
 tural heritage institutions\, including archives\, in the Netherlands and 
 the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. Cultural organisations in the heritage 
 sector may also apply if they collaborate with one or more collection-hold
 ing institutions. All applicants must be non-profit\, not-for-profit entit
 ies. NOT FUNDED: fixed costs\, restoration\, building museums\, or regular
  operations. CONDITIONS: institutions must subscribe to the Fair Practice 
 Code\, the Culture Governance Code and the Diversity and Inclusion Code\, 
 and must pay visual artists fairly where artists are involved. AMOUNTS: pr
 ojects get up to 40% of variable project-related costs on a minimum projec
 t budget of EUR 25\,000\; research tracks get up to 70% of costs capped at
  EUR 15\,000 with no minimum budget. APPLY through the Mondriaan Fund onli
 ne system after registering an account. Deadline 17 September 2026 before 
 16:00 Dutch time / 10:00 Caribbean time.\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund\n
 Location: Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Neth
 erlands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.\nAward: Projects: up to 40%
  of variable project-related costs\, with a minimum project budget of EUR 
 25\,000. Research tracks: up to 70% of costs\, capped at EUR 15\,000\, wit
 h no minimum budget. No application fee.\nDuration: Project or research tr
 ack. This card covers the 17 September 2026 deadline.\n\nMore: https://www
 .mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-innovation/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-inno
 vation/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Nether
 lands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tokyo-biennale-2027-social-dive@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tokyo Biennale 2027: SOCIAL DIVE open call for artists an
 d curators
DESCRIPTION:SOCIAL DIVE is Tokyo Biennale's open call\, now in its third ed
 ition and\, for 2027\, open to both international and domestic applicants 
 (the 2019 round drew roughly 1\,500 overseas applications and the 2025 int
 ernational-only round roughly 1\,400). Artists and curators propose projec
 ts to be presented as part of Tokyo Biennale 2027\, whose theme is 'From M
 e to Us'. THEME: general producer Masato Nakamura frames the biennale agai
 nst an age of conflict and division\, arguing that to avoid repeating memo
 ries of war\, violence and exclusion we must reexamine the wisdom and cult
 ural resources passed down across generations. Tokyo carries the memories 
 of neighbourhoods dating back to the Edo period\, twice suffered great los
 s through the Great Kanto Earthquake and the Second World War\, and each t
 ime was rebuilt through the strength of its citizens\; it is also an inter
 national city where different languages\, memories\, beliefs\, wounds and 
 hopes intersect. Quoting Kenzaburo Oe\, 'I cannot live my life over again.
  But we can live our lives over again'\, the call proposes that when a sin
 gle 'me' encounters others and reconnects relationships\, a new consciousn
 ess of 'us' emerges. Art projects are treated as a form of cultural prescr
 iption\, reconnecting people with people\, people with places and people w
 ith memories. ELIGIBILITY: open to artists\, curators\, researchers\, coll
 ectives and practitioners in related fields active in Japan and abroad\; a
 pplicants must be 18 or older at the time of application\; there are no re
 strictions on nationality\, place of residence or base of operations\; par
 ticipants must be able to work in Tokyo during the designated period. ACCE
 PTED FORMATS: artwork creation\, exhibitions\, performances\, video works\
 , tours\, workshops\, participatory projects\, research projects and curat
 orial projects. Applications cover both artwork production by artists and 
 art project proposals by curators. APPLY via the Google Form linked from s
 ocialdive.jp/en (a Google account is required) during the application peri
 od 17 July to 30 September 2026\, 23:59 JST. Read the official call docume
 ntation for full eligibility requirements and guidelines.\n\nOrganisation:
  Tokyo Biennale\nLocation: Tokyo\, Japan. Selected participants carry out 
 residency-based production\, research\, meetings\, preparation and impleme
 ntation in Tokyo during a designated period. No restrictions on nationalit
 y\, place of residence or base of operations.\nAward: Travel up to JPY 300
 \,000 (economy class\, one person maximum)\, accommodation in Tokyo\, a pr
 oject development budget of up to JPY 200\,000\, and a JPY 100\,000 honora
 rium. Additional support includes venue coordination\, interpretation\, tr
 anslation and an invitation letter. No application fee.\nDuration: Residen
 cy-based work in Tokyo during a designated period\, with the project prese
 nted as part of Tokyo Biennale 2027.\n\nMore: https://socialdive.jp/en/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://socialdive.jp/en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Tokyo\, Japan. Selected participants carry out residency-based pro
 duction\, research\, meetings\, preparation and implementation in Tokyo du
 ring a designated period. No restrictions on nationality\, place of reside
 nce or base of operations.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surels-place-residency-2027-winter@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Surel's Place: Month-Long Artist Residency (September 202
 6 deadline)
DESCRIPTION:Surel's Place is a residency in Garden City\, Idaho with a two-
 fold mission: to give artists the time\, shelter and professional support 
 their work deserves\, and to give the public accessible art experiences in
 cluding performances\, readings\, exhibits and workshops. In exchange for 
 the residency\, artists offer art experiences back to the community. OPEN 
 TO professional visual\, literary and performance artists: painters\, writ
 ers\, musicians\, architects\, filmmakers\, choreographers\, any artist wh
 o needs a place to focus. LIMITS: because of the property's constraints an
 d proximity to neighbours\, it cannot accommodate loud or very messy proce
 sses such as welding or blacksmithing. The organisers also note that predo
 minantly conceptual work\, work that must be explained to attract a viewer
 \, may not be met with as much enthusiasm by the jurors\, and that they we
 lcome artists who want to connect with an audience. Each cycle\, one of th
 e five residencies includes outreach to local schools through the PARTNER 
 Schools Program. REQUIREMENTS: maintain residence at the home and studio (
 no studio-use-only residencies)\; use the residency in a focused way towar
 ds a stated goal with no other employment or work happening simultaneously
 \; visual artists give a show of and/or lecture about their residency work
 \, writers give a reading and discussion session\; all artists offer at le
 ast one public workshop (2 to 3 hours\, minimal lecturing)\; visual artist
 s donate one piece of art to Surel's Place for future fundraising\; writer
 s allow Surel's Place to publish a free-standing excerpt of their residenc
 y work in books\, broadsides\, posters or similar. SALES COMMISSION applie
 s as set out in the funding notes. HOUSE RULES: the non-smoking requiremen
 t is non-negotiable and any evidence of smoking is grounds for early termi
 nation. Partners are welcome but children and pets cannot be accommodated\
 , no more than two adults may live there during a residency\, and partners
  wanting concurrent residencies must apply and be accepted separately. ELI
 GIBILITY: applicants must be 25 or older and non-smokers. APPLY by email t
 o info@surelsplace.org with the online Artist in Residence application plu
 s\, for VISUAL ARTISTS\, a cover letter introducing yourself and saying wh
 y you want to be at Surel's Place specifically\, a proposal including a wo
 rkshop outline\, a CV\, electronic images of 4 to 6 pieces of work and an 
 image list (title\, size\, medium\, year)\; for LITERARY applicants\, cove
 r letter\, proposal with workshop outline\, CV and PDF work samples with y
 our name and page number in the top right corner (fiction\, prose or scrip
 t no more than 10 typed double-spaced pages\; poetry no more than six poem
 s and no more than 10 pages)\; for OTHER MEDIUMS\, cover letter\, proposal
  with workshop outline\, CV and electronic samples (contact Surel's Place 
 to discuss sample requirements for your medium). Applications are reviewed
  by juries of accomplished artists\, writers and fine arts professionals\,
  and all applicants are notified by email.\n\nOrganisation: Surel's Place\
 nLocation: In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents 
 must live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.\nAward: Fre
 e rent\, utilities and wi-fi\, a living stipend of USD 100 per week\, a US
 D 300 travel stipend\, use of well-maintained bicycles\, free or discounte
 d tickets to art events and movies\, marketing through Surel's Place chann
 els\, and event hosting including logistics and installation help. Each re
 sident also gets a local host to orient them and host their public events.
  No application fee. NOTE: Surel's Place takes a commission on sales arisi
 ng from the residency\, 35% of retail price on artwork\, 45% on ticketed e
 vents that are offsite or need contracted services (calculated after admin
 istrative expenses)\, and 15% on books\, CDs or other merchandise tangenti
 al to the artist's medium. A surcharge may apply to cover additional utili
 ty costs if a partner joins.\nDuration: One month (longer may be possible 
 if justified in the application). The 1 September deadline covers residenc
 ies in January to May of the following year\, beginning four months after 
 the deadline\; the 1 March deadline covers July to November of the same ye
 ar.\n\nMore: https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents mus
 t live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ottawa-school-of-art-exhibition-proposals-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ottawa School of Art: Call for Exhibition Proposals 2027-
 2028
DESCRIPTION:The Ottawa School of Art invites artists and collectives to sub
 mit exhibition proposals for its 2027-2028 season\, welcoming current and 
 proposed work across all media including painting\, sculpture\, photograph
 y\, installation and video. Group exhibition proposals are accepted. Succe
 ssful applicants exhibit at ONE campus gallery\, but proposals may be subm
 itted for either or both venues\; an application to both must demonstrate 
 a clear understanding of how the exhibition would work best within each ga
 llery's dimensions (floor plans are published with the call). VENUES: the 
 J.W. Stellick Gallery at the ByWard Market campus\, dedicated to giving OS
 A students and the community a wide variety of contemporary art\, and the 
 OSA Orleans Gallery on the Centrum Level of the Shenkman Arts Centre. Arti
 sts receive an Ontario CARFAC exhibition fee. SELECTION: a jury of three a
 rt professionals selects exhibitions on the quality of the work\, the inte
 grity of the proposal\, and its relation to OSA's programs. SUBMISSION: al
 l six components are required\, incomplete proposals are withdrawn without
  notification\, and poorly organised proposals will not be reviewed. (1) C
 ompleted application cover form with contact information and exhibition de
 tails. (2) A written exhibition proposal outlining the concept and intent 
 of the proposed or current body of work. (3) One artist statement per prop
 osal\; group proposals submit a single collective statement\, not individu
 al statements. (4) An up-to-date CV for the exhibiting artist\, or a CV fo
 r each participating artist in a group proposal. (5) Up to 10 visual files
  of work completed within the last two years. An artwork and a detail of t
 he same artwork count as two images\; images must be JPG only\, 300 dpi\, 
 no larger than 2MB each\, RGB\, maximum 1240 pixels on the longest side. E
 ach video counts as one file\, total video time per proposal must not exce
 ed 15 minutes\, and videos must be AVI\, MOV or MP4 and no larger than 500
 MB each. Precede each file name with a zero-padded number and the title\, 
 for example 01Artwork title. (6) A corresponding numbered visual file list
  with title\, year\, medium and dimensions (H x W x D)\, specifying inches
  or centimetres. FILES MUST BE SENT VIA WETRANSFER to boutique@artottawa.c
 a\; proposals submitted by post\, hand delivery or email will not be accep
 ted. Deadline 4:30 pm ET\, Friday 4 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Ottaw
 a School of Art (OSA)\nLocation: Ottawa\, Ontario\, Canada. Two venues: th
 e J.W. Stellick Gallery at the ByWard Market campus\, and the OSA Orleans 
 Gallery on the Centrum Level of the Shenkman Arts Centre.\nAward: Artists 
 receive an Ontario CARFAC exhibition fee (the amount is not stated in the 
 call and follows the CARFAC schedule). No application fee.\nDuration: One 
 exhibition slot in the 2027-2028 exhibition season at one campus gallery.\
 n\nMore: https://www.artottawa.ca/call-for-exhibition-proposals?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.artottawa.ca/call-for-exhibition-proposals?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ottawa\, Ontario\, Canada. Two venues: the J.W. Stellick Gallery a
 t the ByWard Market campus\, and the OSA Orleans Gallery on the Centrum Le
 vel of the Shenkman Arts Centre.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:erie-canal-museum-artist-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Erie Canal Museum: Erie Canal Artist-in-Residence 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Erie Canal Museum\, in partnership with the New York State 
 Canal Corporation\, is appointing two Erie Canal Artists-in-Residence for 
 a one-year\, year-long creative inquiry into the New York State Canal Syst
 em\, including the historic Erie Canal: reflecting on its past\, contempla
 ting its current condition\, and considering its future. The 2027 residenc
 ies are dedicated to projects that reflect upon the Canal's physical\, env
 ironmental and social condition and history\, its materiality and its cont
 ext\, and the project must directly address the Canal. Applicants propose 
 a specific creative visual project that must be exhibitable in a gallery s
 etting\, identify their target audiences and explain how the work will be 
 shared with them and with the public at large. Studies of Canal communitie
 s are encouraged\, but the Canal and its context must be central. Projects
  may focus on key areas or on the entire system\, and applicants are told 
 to weigh that scale: 524 miles of navigable waterways and 365 miles of mul
 ti-use trails. ELIGIBILITY: all artists are encouraged to apply\, and spec
 ial consideration goes to artists with ancestral ties to the Haudenosaunee
  Confederacy and/or the Mohican Nation and to artists who have lived or wo
 rked in Canal communities. Only individual artists or artist collectives m
 ay apply\; artists representing nonprofit or for-profit organisations may 
 not. Artists must be 18 or older and may not be currently enrolled in scho
 ol full time. For collectives\, only one USD 10\,000 stipend is awarded to
  the group. APPLICATION: a statement of interest and proposal (up to 500 w
 ords) covering why the residency interests you\, your proposed project and
  how it meets the three residency goals (engage with the Canal\, adjacent 
 Canalway Trails\, Canal Corp. and Museum staff\, Canal facilities\, commun
 ities and visitors\; reflect directly on the Canal's physical\, environmen
 tal and social condition and history\; consider a fuller\, more complete a
 ccount of the Canal's past\, present and future)\, which geographic sectio
 n you will focus on\, how you would share work publicly during the residen
 cy\, your proposed final project\, and how your previous work and public e
 ngagement relate\; an artist's statement (up to 300 words)\; a resume or C
 V (max three pages)\; two professional references with phone and email\; a
 nd ten examples of recent work (written work limited to three pages each) 
 with a list giving title\, date\, materials and size plus short descriptio
 ns. EVALUATION covers clarity and relevance of the proposal\, the scope\, 
 feasibility and clarity of the plan to reach the intended audience\, the p
 lan for public engagement including at least one public-facing event durin
 g the residency\, a final project usable in an exhibit and catalogue\, and
  the quality and relevance of prior work. Applications are reviewed by a p
 anel of Erie Canal Museum and Canal Corporation representatives plus invit
 ed arts professionals (the 2026 panel included Elizabeth Dubben of Collar 
 Works\, artist and educator Dennis RedMoon Darkeem\, and Garth Johnson\, C
 urator of Ceramics at the Everson Museum of Art)\; finalists interview wit
 h the panel. SUBMIT application packages as PDFs by email to Natalie Stets
 on\, Executive Director\, at employment@eriecanalmuseum.org with 'Erie Can
 al Artist-in-Residence' in the subject line\, by 11:59 pm on Sunday 6 Sept
 ember 2026. The RFP is at eriecanalmuseum.org/about/careers/.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Erie Canal Museum (with the New York State Canal Corporation)\nLocati
 on: New York State\, USA. Non-residential: there is no live-in accommodati
 on\, and the work is fieldwork along the New York State Canal System (524 
 miles of navigable waterways and 365 miles of multi-use trails) and its co
 mmunities\, with engagement with Canal Corporation and Museum staff and fa
 cilities. The Museum is in the 1850 Syracuse Weighlock Building\, Syracuse
 \, NY. Finalist interviews are conducted virtually.\nAward: USD 10\,000 ar
 tist stipend per residency\, for two Artists-in-Residence. Collectives app
 lying as a group receive one USD 10\,000 stipend between them. No applicat
 ion fee. The final project feeds into an exhibit and catalogue\, with an e
 xhibit opening in the first half of 2028.\nDuration: One year: 1 January t
 o 31 December 2027. Finalist interviews 28 September to 23 October 2026 (v
 irtual)\; artist notification by 13 November 2026\; exhibit opening in the
  first half of 2028.\n\nMore: https://www.nyfa.org/opportunities/opportuni
 ty-info/?title=erie-canal-artist-in-residence&id=d469ab60-b5fd-41aa-ad27-a
 9fe0e841674&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.nyfa.org/opportunities/opportunity-info/?title=erie-canal-a
 rtist-in-residence&id=d469ab60-b5fd-41aa-ad27-a9fe0e841674&utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York State\, USA. Non-residential: there is no live-in accommo
 dation\, and the work is fieldwork along the New York State Canal System (
 524 miles of navigable waterways and 365 miles of multi-use trails) and it
 s communities\, with engagement with Canal Corporation and Museum staff an
 d facilities. The Museum is in the 1850 Syracuse Weighlock Building\, Syra
 cuse\, NY. Finalist interviews are conducted virtually.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:princeton-arts-fellowship-2027-2029@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: Princeton Arts Fellowship 2027-2029 (Lewis Center for the
  Arts)
DESCRIPTION:The Princeton Arts Fellowship places early-career artists at th
 e Lewis Center for the Arts for two academic years on a full-time salaried
  appointment\, on the conviction that exposure to the arts helps us make s
 ense of our lives and the lives of our neighbours. ELIGIBLE APPLICANTS are
  early-career composers\, conductors\, musicians\, choreographers\, playwr
 ights\, designers\, directors and performance artists. IMPORTANT FOR THIS 
 CYCLE: applications are accepted only for the Program in Dance\, the Progr
 am in Theater and Music Theater\, and the Department of Music. Fellows typ
 ically teach one undergraduate course per semester (subject to approval by
  the Office of the Dean of the Faculty)\, or in some cases take on an arti
 stic assignment with students in lieu of a class\; prior teaching experien
 ce is NOT required. Beyond that\, fellows are expected to engage with the 
 Princeton community through advising students\, talks\, workshops\, exhibi
 tions or other events\, and have time to pursue their own artistic practic
 e and scholarly research. NOT ELIGIBLE: the appointment cannot be used to 
 fund work leading to a PhD or any advanced degree\; holders of Princeton P
 hDs cannot apply\; past recipients of the Hodder Fellowship cannot apply\;
  and anyone with a sustained and continuous relationship with Princeton is
  excluded (an occasional and sporadic relationship is fine). Fellows may n
 ot hold concurrent teaching positions at other institutions during the app
 ointment. You may apply once per application cycle. APPLICATION via Interf
 olio: a CV\; a 750-word proposal broken into how you would use the fellows
 hip to develop your work (250 words)\, what you might teach or pursue with
  undergraduates (250 words)\, and your approach to building community with
  people of different backgrounds\, experiences\, perspectives and artistic
  practices through your practice\, teaching and/or research (250 words)\; 
 contact information for one reference\; and work samples with a 150-word s
 tatement on how they relate to your proposal\, submitted as links in a PDF
 . WORK SAMPLE SPECS: Dance (choreography)\, one to three links to performa
 nces\, rehearsals or research\, continuous footage only\, no trailers\, ph
 otographs or promotional material\, with cue points of no more than 3 to 4
  minutes per link and no more than 10 minutes total. Music\, one to two sc
 ores and/or links to performance examples totalling 10 minutes\, video opt
 ional. Theater\, up to 15 pages of writing sample and/or up to 10 design i
 mages (photos or renderings) and/or a video or audio reel with cue points\
 , no more than 10 minutes to watch. The deadline is 8 September 2026 at 11
 :59 pm EDT\; receipt of applications cannot be confirmed and late applicat
 ions are not accepted. FAQ and examples of building community in artistic 
 practice\, teaching and research: arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/faq.\n\nO
 rganisation: Princeton University\, Lewis Center for the Arts\nLocation: I
 n person on campus at Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA. Appointees are expecte
 d to be full and active members of the community with frequent\, engaged i
 nteraction with students. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship
  of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.\nAward: USD 93\,000 salary
  per year for each of two academic years\, as a full-time 24-month appoint
 ment at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Two academic years\, 2027-2028 and 2028-2029. Applications ope
 n 1 July 2026 and close 8 September 2026\; finalists attend an on-campus i
 nterview during the week of 7 December 2026.\n\nMore: https://apply.interf
 olio.com/187043?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://apply.interfolio.com/187043?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person on campus at Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA. Appointees ar
 e expected to be full and active members of the community with frequent\, 
 engaged interaction with students. These positions are not eligible for sp
 onsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:collar-works-elizabeth-murray-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Collar Works: Elizabeth Murray Artist Residency (EMAR) 20
 27
DESCRIPTION:The Elizabeth Murray Artist Residency gives emerging\, underrep
 resented and established artists an immersive\, communal atmosphere for ar
 t-making and dialogue on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, New York\, the clas
 sic farmhouse where the late Elizabeth Murray spent more than two decades 
 of summers and kept her studio in the large\, cathedral-like dairy barn. M
 urray\, who died in 2007\, won a Skowhegan Medal in Painting in 1986\, a M
 acArthur grant in 1999 and a MoMA career retrospective in 2005\; her husba
 nd Bob Holman is a poet and arts activist who founded the Bowery Poetry Cl
 ub. In 2017 the Murray-Holman family partnered with Collar Works\, a nonpr
 ofit contemporary arts organisation in Troy\, New York\, to build a summer
  residency carrying the legacy forward. EMAR is explicitly designed around
  the critical needs of emerging and underrepresented artists and of artist
 s with children\, with an active commitment to diversity\, equity\, access
 ibility and inclusion\, and describes itself as a safe space for creative 
 risk-taking\, personal growth and respite. Work is primarily in visual and
  literary arts\, though the programme supports new contemporary work acros
 s many art forms. TWO TRACKS: the INDIVIDUAL RESIDENCY takes up to five ar
 tists per session (including onsite artist-staff) for two weeks\, with 24-
 hour access to semi-private studios in the barn\, a private bedroom in the
  farmhouse\, shared bathrooms\, communal living room\, kitchen and a libra
 ry holding Elizabeth Murray's personal art book collection. The FAMILY RES
 IDENCY invites three to four artists and their families for one week\, wit
 h a private bedroom (families may need to room together)\, a semi-private 
 studio in the same barn\, and full-day childcare running as a parallel cre
 ative residency for children aged 5 to 12\, focused on art and nature prog
 ramming: while parent artists get uninterrupted studio time\, children wor
 k in their own EMAR Kids Art Studio\, explore outside through guided activ
 ities\, play\, swim\, read and take guided field trips to local farms and 
 museums. Partners are welcome to join and help with childcare\, but the pr
 ogramme is committed to fully supporting artists who want or need to atten
 d solo with their children\, and Collar Works works case by case with each
  artist to put the necessary support and equipment in place before arrival
  given the rural setting. APPLICATION requires artist information\, a stat
 ement of intent (250 to 500 words)\, an artist biography (250 words)\, ten
  examples of recent work (visual arts as JPEGs up to 2MB each\, with a PDF
  of links or a curated URL page for video and media works including any pa
 sswords\; literary arts as up to three poems totalling max six pages\, or 
 max ten pages of prose) and a list of works with title\, medium\, date and
  size. Applications go through Submittable and close 1 October 2026 (the S
 ubmittable listing shows a 2 October 2026 06:00 close in its own timezone)
 . Questions and technical assistance: info@collarworks.org.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: Collar Works\nLocation: In person on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, Wash
 ington County\, New York\, USA (the former summer home of Elizabeth Murray
  and Bob Holman). Collar Works itself is based in Troy\, New York.\nAward:
  Fully supported: there is no cost to participate for accepted artists. Pr
 ivate bedroom accommodation with shared bathrooms in the farmhouse\, 24-ho
 ur access to a semi-private studio in the barn that housed Elizabeth Murra
 y's studio\, all food provided (dinner cooked and eaten communally)\, and 
 use of the property including a swimming pool\, pond and 77 acres of field
 s and woods. Accepted artists also receive a USD 500 stipend to use at the
 ir discretion towards travel\, materials or other associated costs. The Fa
 mily Residency additionally provides free full-day (9am to 3pm) childcare 
 as a parallel creative residency for children aged 5 to 12\, plus a dedica
 ted Kids Art Studio. APPLICATION FEE: USD 35 (the first month of the appli
 cation period was free\; the fee applies from 1 August 2026).\nDuration: T
 wo weeks for individual artists\, one week for families. 2027 individual s
 essions: Session 1\, 15 to 29 May 2027\; Session 2\, 1 to 15 June 2027. 20
 27 family session: Session 3\, 19 to 26 June 2027. Finalist interviews Nov
 ember 2026\; final notification December 2026.\n\nMore: https://collarwork
 s.submittable.com/submit/360246/elizabeth-murray-artist-residency-2027?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://collarworks.submittable.com/submit/360246/elizabeth-murray-arti
 st-residency-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
LOCATION:In person on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, Washington County\, New
  York\, USA (the former summer home of Elizabeth Murray and Bob Holman). C
 ollar Works itself is based in Troy\, New York.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:video-pool-matr-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Video Pool: Media Art + Technology Residency (MATR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:Video Pool's Media Art + Technology Residency supports experime
 ntal\, independent\, technology-based work. The selected artist or artists
  develop a new project (or adapt and transform an existing one) in direct 
 response to the intimate scale and specific character of the Poolside Gall
 ery\, culminating in an exhibition there. The call is deliberately open on
  medium: video\, sound\, installation\, digital media\, performance\, inte
 ractive technologies\, expanded cinema and interdisciplinary practices are
  all welcome. VP asks proposals to speak clearly to your practice\, the qu
 estions\, materials or ideas that drive the work\, and how that work might
  grow\, shift or take new form inside the gallery. It is explicitly intere
 sted in expansive proposals\, work that pushes past the gallery's four wal
 ls to engage its surrounding spaces (hallways\, windows\, projection wall)
  or that reimagines what the gallery itself can hold\, and in projects tha
 t engage technology as a creative\, critical\, aesthetic or socio-politica
 l tool. Priority is given to artists from equity-seeking communities whose
  practices contribute to the advancement of experimental media arts. Resid
 ents get full access to VP's available equipment\, facilities and technica
 l resources throughout the residency\, and applicants are encouraged to re
 view the equipment and facilities list before proposing. ELIGIBILITY: open
  to VP members (this is a member submission call)\; no nationality restric
 tion is stated\, but the residency and exhibition are in person in Winnipe
 g. APPLICATION: artist bio (1\,500 characters)\, headshot\, description of
  your artistic practice (3\,000 characters)\, project proposal for MATR (3
 \,000 characters)\, equipment and facility needs (750 characters)\, descri
 ption of how you would use the space (750 characters)\, CV\, up to five im
 ages (optional) and up to two video or documentation links. Submit via the
  online form at videopool.org/matr-2027-application/ by 30 August 2026\; a
 lternative submission formats can be arranged by contacting Emma (emma@vid
 eopool.org) or Iyunade (iyunade@videopool.org). General enquiries: info@vi
 deopool.org\, 204-949-9134.\n\nOrganisation: Video Pool Media Arts Centre\
 nLocation: In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gall
 ery\, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.\nAward
 : CAD 2\,250 artist fee\, covering both the residency period and the subse
 quent Poolside Gallery exhibition. Residents also get full access to VP's 
 equipment\, facilities and technical resources\; the Poolside Gallery itse
 lf is equipped with two projectors and four speakers. Applicants must be V
 P members\, so a membership is purchased separately\, but there is no sepa
 rate application fee.\nDuration: Up to two months to develop and install t
 he project\, including one month of dedicated access to the Poolside Galle
 ry. Residency takes place in Winter 2027\; exhibition dates TBD.\n\nMore: 
 https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-applic
 ations/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-app
 lications/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gallery
 \, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:inato-gallery-touch-some-grass-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Inato Gallery: Touch Some Grass (open-call poster exhibit
 ion)
DESCRIPTION:Touch Some Grass is an open-call poster exhibition responding t
 o the growing presence of AI-generated imagery and its impact on creative 
 labour\, authorship and visual culture. At a time when images can be produ
 ced instantly and consumed just as quickly\, the exhibition reclaims the p
 oster as a space for ideas\, intention and public conversation\, inviting 
 artists to make work that questions\, challenges or reflects on this chang
 ing landscape. As the organisers put it\, the question is no longer whethe
 r machines can make images\, they can\; the question is what kind of world
  we build when speed matters more than thought. NOTE: this call offers exh
 ibition and visibility only\, with no artist fee or stipend. SUBMISSION GU
 IDELINES: artwork size A3 (297 x 420 mm)\, portrait orientation\, 300 DPI\
 , CMYK recommended\, print-quality PDF preferred\, maximum file size 1 GB\
 , one submission per artist. CAP: participation is limited to 60 artists a
 nd the submission form closes automatically once all slots are filled\, so
  the effective deadline may fall well before 31 August 2026 (10 of 60 slot
 s were taken at the time of listing). Inato Gallery is an artist-run space
  in Iligan City\, Philippines\, which began as a solo street exhibition by
  KaraTula and now runs concept-driven solo and group shows\, open mics\, t
 alks and workshops built on openness\, participation and the belief that a
 rt should exist where people already are.\n\nOrganisation: Inato Gallery\n
 Location: Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or tr
 avel is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-
 run space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stat
 ed.\nAward: No artist fee\, stipend or production budget. This is an exhib
 ition opportunity: selected posters are shown in Inato Gallery's Touch Som
 e Grass exhibition. No submission fee is stated in the call.\nDuration: On
 e-off poster exhibition. Exhibition dates are not announced yet.\n\nMore: 
 https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or trav
 el is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-ru
 n space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stated
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bica-generator-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: BICA: Generator Fund 2026 (Kinetic and Potential Grants)
DESCRIPTION:The Generator Fund is BICA's regranting programme for visual ar
 tists in Buffalo and the surrounding two counties\, supported by the Andy 
 Warhol Foundation's Regional Regranting Program. It makes direct project g
 rants to artists rather than to institutions\, and welcomes professional\,
  emerging and experimental practitioners alike. CHOOSING A TRACK: Kinetic 
 Grants (up to USD 5\,000) suit a project with a clear beginning and end\, 
 built around a specific event\, release or presentation\, usually made alo
 ne or with a small team. Potential Grants (up to USD 10\,000) suit an ongo
 ing space\, system or collaborative structure that involves many people an
 d is meant to evolve over time\; these applications also need a sustainabi
 lity plan. ELIGIBILITY: you must live and work in Erie or Niagara County\,
  New York. You can apply as an individual artist\, an informal group or an
  unincorporated collective. NOT ELIGIBLE: 501(c)(3) organisations\; anyone
  receiving a solo exhibition honorarium from BICA in the same fiscal year\
 ; BICA employees\; and anyone who received a Potential Grant in the previo
 us round (they can still apply for a Kinetic Grant). The fund supports vis
 ual arts based projects\, including painting\, sculpture\, installation\, 
 photography\, social practice\, performance art\, video and zines\; projec
 ts rooted primarily in music\, theatre\, dance or literary arts without a 
 strong visual component are not eligible. APPLICATION runs through Submitt
 able and asks for a project title\, a 150-character tagline\, artistic con
 text (up to 250 words)\, a full project description (up to 500 words\, the
  most heavily weighted part\, expected to cover where the project happens 
 or how the public accesses it\, a timeline with key milestones\, the audie
 nce and how you will reach them\, and the people involved)\, a budget on B
 ICA's project budget sheet covering expenses\, in-kind support and income\
 , work samples (images\, video or PDFs of past work and a sense of what is
  to come) and a CV. SUPPORT before the deadline includes a virtual info se
 ssion on 12 August 2026 at 5:30 pm\, an in-person info session at BICA on 
 5 September 2026 at 3:30 pm (followed by a budget and CV workshop led by N
 ando Alvarez-Perez)\, one-on-one office hours with the Generator Fund coor
 dinator on Thursdays\, Fridays and Saturdays through August and September\
 , and group work sessions at BICA School Lab (30B Essex St) on 19 and 26 S
 eptember 2026\, 12 to 5 pm. Applications submitted a week or more early ma
 y receive feedback and be reopened for edits.\n\nOrganisation: Buffalo Ins
 titute for Contemporary Art (BICA)\nLocation: Applicants must live and wor
 k in Erie or Niagara County\, New York\, USA. BICA is at 30D Essex Street\
 , Buffalo\, NY 14213.\nAward: Two grant types: Kinetic Grants of up to USD
  5\,000 for short-term\, public-facing visual arts projects (exhibitions\,
  performances\, installations\, workshops\, publications)\, and Potential 
 Grants of up to USD 10\,000 for long-term\, collaborative or platform-buil
 ding projects (artist-run spaces\, recurring events\, media platforms and 
 other structures that extend beyond a single outcome). No application fee.
  The fund is part of the Andy Warhol Foundation's Regional Regranting Prog
 ram.\nDuration: Applications open 1 August 2026 and close 30 September 202
 6\; jurors review in October and meet in November\; grantees are notified 
 in December 2026\; cheques are handed over in January 2027 at the Meet the
  Grantees event\; proposed projects are to be completed by 31 December 202
 7.\n\nMore: https://www.generatorfund.org/apply?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.generatorfund.org/apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicants must live and work in Erie or Niagara County\, New York
 \, USA. BICA is at 30D Essex Street\, Buffalo\, NY 14213.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jugaad-absolute-improvisation-sac-mama-magnet-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260905
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260906
SUMMARY:Deadline: JUGAAD: Absolute Improvisation\, open call for printed ma
 tter and small-format works (Amsterdam)
DESCRIPTION:Open call from SAC Art Amsterdam and Mama Magnet for a printed 
 matter fair themed around jugaad: improvised\, resourceful solutions built
  from what is on hand (from the Sanskrit yuj\, 'to join'\, via Hindi jugar
 \, famously embodied by post-independence Punjabi vehicles pieced together
  from scrap metal and pump engines). The curators want work that engages t
 he spirit of jugaad either as subject (everyday intelligences of getting b
 y\, lateral logics of adaptation\, unlikely workarounds\, reinvention born
  of necessity) or in how it was made (found or repurposed materials\, adap
 ted processes\, structures built to solve a specific constraint). Work tha
 t came from adjustment rather than access\, problem-solving rather than bu
 dget. WHAT: zines\, artist books\, comics\, risograph or photocopied print
 s\, posters\, foldables\, instruction manuals\, packaging and other small-
 format works\; if it can travel in a suitcase it is probably in scope. No 
 framed work or anything needing gallery-standard installation. Gen-AI imag
 es and text are not accepted. WHO: artists\, designers\, writers and colle
 ctives whose practice genuinely engages with the theme\; if unsure\, reach
  out and explain in a line or two. Selected work must reach the Netherland
 s by end of September 2026. TERMS: selections made on a rolling basis 5 Au
 gust to 5 September\; selected work is sold at the fair (9-10 October\, Am
 sterdam) on mutually agreeable commission terms\, with payment within 30 d
 ays of each sale and unsold work returned after the consignment period. HO
 W TO APPLY: email info@mamamagnet.org with a CC to art@sacartamsterdam.com
 \, subject line 'JUGAAD - [Your Contribution]'\, by 5 September 2026\; ear
 lier is better given rolling selection.\n\nOrganisation: SAC Art Amsterdam
  and Mama Magnet\nLocation: Fair takes place 9-10 October 2026 in Amsterda
 m. Open internationally\, but selected work must be shipped to the Netherl
 ands by the end of September 2026 at the artist's expense.\nAward: No appl
 ication fee. Selected work is sold at the printed matter fair on mutually 
 agreeable terms regarding commission\, duration and number of editions\; a
 rtists are remunerated within 30 days of each sale. Unsold works are retur
 ned at the end of the agreed consignment period.\nDuration: Rolling select
 ion 5 August to 5 September 2026\; fair 9-10 October 2026\n\nMore: https:/
 /sacartamsterdam.com/upcoming-programmes/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sacartamsterdam.com/upcoming-programmes/?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fair takes place 9-10 October 2026 in Amsterdam. Open internationa
 lly\, but selected work must be shipped to the Netherlands by the end of S
 eptember 2026 at the artist's expense.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ars-biologica-artxscience-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ars Biologica ArtXScience Residency 2027-2028 (Ars Electr
 onica x Ceske Budejovice ECoC 2028)
DESCRIPTION:A fully funded art-science residency from Ars Electronica and C
 eske Budejovice\, European Capital of Culture 2028\, exploring how art and
  science can collaborate on urgent environmental challenges: climate emerg
 ence\, sustainable agriculture\, geoengineering\, permaculture\, water res
 ource management\, and the broader ecological impacts on local communities
 . Selected residents work in direct collaboration with local scientists in
  Ceske Budejovice. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, designers\, creatives and arti
 st collectives with more than 5 years of professional creative practice an
 d documented interdisciplinary work\; experience in community engagement a
 nd science collaborations is essential\; applicants must be 18+\, of any n
 ationality\, and legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. WHAT YOU G
 ET: a EUR 24\,000 package (EUR 6\,000 artist fee\, up to EUR 15\,000 produ
 ction\, up to EUR 3\,000 travel and subsistence) plus one month of housing
  covered separately\; two winners are selected. STRUCTURE: the residency r
 uns January 2027 to April 2028\, with a required on-site stay of up to one
  month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/September 2027\
 , and a March 2028 stay for festival installation\, exhibition and deinsta
 llation. HOW TO APPLY: online form with project title\, a written proposal
  of max 250 words\, a scientific direction statement (max 100 words)\, art
 istic vision (max 100 words)\, community connection plan (max 100 words)\,
  a super short pitch (3 to 30 words)\, 3 to 7 images\, an optional 1-minut
 e video\, a portfolio link and contact information. Two-round selection (o
 nline application then interview)\; winners notified by November 2026. Dea
 dline 28 September 2026 at midnight CET.\n\nOrganisation: Ars Electronica 
 and Ceske Budejovice - European Capital of Culture 2028\nLocation: Ceske B
 udejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any nationality aged 18+
  who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Required on-site s
 tays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/
 September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibition and deinstal
 lation.\nAward: EUR 24\,000 total package per winner: EUR 6\,000 artist fe
 e\, up to EUR 15\,000 production budget\, and up to EUR 3\,000 for travel\
 , accommodation and subsistence\, plus a separate budget covering one mont
 h of housing in Ceske Budejovice. Two positions available. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: January 2027 to April 2028\, with on-site blocks in early 2027 
 and March 2028\n\nMore: https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars
 -biologica-art-science-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars-biologica-art-scienc
 e-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ceske Budejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any natio
 nality aged 18+ who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Req
 uired on-site stays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional 
 stay in August/September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibiti
 on and deinstallation.
END:VEVENT
