BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:freelands-learning-through-making-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: Freelands Foundation: Learning Through Making Fund
DESCRIPTION:The Learning Through Making fund supports UK-based visual art o
 rganisations to bring opportunities for making and material exploration in
 to their art education work. Freelands Foundation is looking for explorato
 ry projects that embrace openness\, collaboration\, and iteration -- enabl
 ing organisations to work with materials in an expansive way\, unpicking p
 reconceptions about making and material literacy without predefined outcom
 es. ELIGIBLE ACTIVITIES: providing materials\, transport\, or access to ex
 pertise and facilities\; action-research trials such as a new series of wo
 rkshops or events developing material literacy\; adding a making-focused e
 lement to existing activity. Projects may range from a few days to several
  months\, but must take place between 1 January and 31 December 2027. AMOU
 NT: up to £25\,000\; funding provided no earlier than December 2026. ELIG
 IBILITY: UK-based visual art organisations run with a charitable purpose a
 nd for public benefit\; eligible types include charities registered in Eng
 land and Wales\, Scotland\, or Northern Ireland\; exempt charities\; galle
 ries\, museums\, or organisations within a Local Authority or larger chari
 table body (e.g. universities)\; and Community Interest Companies or Commu
 nity Benefit Societies meeting specific criteria (charitable purposes\, as
 set lock\, no dividend distribution\, ability to segregate restricted fund
 s). Organisations with a prior Freelands Foundation grant relationship are
  eligible\; you may not apply for activity already funded by Freelands Fou
 ndation. Individuals are not eligible. HOW TO APPLY: download the Applicat
 ion Form\, Application Guidance\, and optional Access Form from freelandsf
 oundation.co.uk\; email completed form to grants@freelandsfoundation.co.uk
  with subject line 'LTM Fund - Application'\; one additional page of image
 s or diagrams may be included. APPLICATION SUPPORT SESSIONS (online): 9 Ju
 ly 2026\, 10:30am-12pm\; 12 August 2026\, 10:30am-12pm. KEY DATES: applica
 tions open 17 June 2026\; close 11 September 2026 at 12pm\; outcomes share
 d 23 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Freelands Foundation\nLocation: UK (E
 ngland\, Northern Ireland\, Scotland\, Wales)\; activity must occur within
  the United Kingdom.\nAward: Up to £25\,000. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Projects of any length\, taking place between 1 January and 31 December
  2027. Funding provided no earlier than December 2026.\n\nMore: https://fr
 eelandsfoundation.co.uk/engage/apply/grants/learning-through-making-fund?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://freelandsfoundation.co.uk/engage/apply/grants/learning-through-
 making-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:UK (England\, Northern Ireland\, Scotland\, Wales)\; activity must
  occur within the United Kingdom.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucross-fellowship-native-american-visual-artists-spring-2027@artificial
 nouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ucross Fellowship for Native American Visual Artists: Spr
 ing 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Ucross Fellowship for Native American Visual Artists is a d
 edicated fellowship within Ucross Foundation's residency program\, offerin
 g fully funded time and space to make work on a working cattle ranch in th
 e foothills of Wyoming's Bighorn Mountains. The fellowship provides living
  accommodations\, meals\, and work space at no charge\, with no applicatio
 n fee. One Fellowship winner is selected per session\; all applicants also
  have the option of being considered for a general Ucross residency. ELIGI
 BILITY: open to Native American visual artists who are practicing contempo
 rary artists currently producing work in any visual medium\, including but
  not limited to painting\, drawing\, mixed media\, sculpture\, printmaking
 \, photography\, video\, animation\, performance art\, installation\, and 
 ceramics\; and who are enrolled members of a state-recognized or federally
 -recognized Tribe\, Pueblo\, Nation\, Native Community\, Political Entity\
 , or Alaskan Native Village. The work sample and project description are t
 he most significant features of the application. Unless the work is interd
 isciplinary\, applicants are encouraged to apply in a single primary disci
 pline and submit a work sample and project description emphasizing that di
 scipline. Work-sample requirements: VISUAL ARTWORK\, submit 10 images (JPE
 G format\, no PDFs) with file names including title and size\, plus a data
  sheet listing title\, medium\, size\, and year of completion for each ima
 ge\; FILM/VIDEO/ANIMATION/PERFORMANCE\, submit up to 3 video files (each u
 nder 8 minutes and smaller than 200 MB) or up to 3 Vimeo or YouTube links.
  The open call for the Spring 2027 session closes July 15\, 2026 (apply by
  11:59 p.m. MT). A separate Ucross Fellowship for Native American Writers 
 is also offered.\n\nOrganisation: Ucross Foundation\nLocation: Sheridan\, 
 Wyoming\, USA\nAward: Fully funded fellowship residency\; living accommoda
 tions\, meals\, and work space provided. No application fee.\nDuration: Sp
 ring session (February through early June 2027)\; residencies range from t
 wo to six weeks\n\nMore: https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-progra
 m.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-program.html?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Sheridan\, Wyoming\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stiftung-kuenstlerdorf-schoeppingen-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Stiftung Künstler*dorf Schöppingen: Residency Fellowshi
 p 2027 Open Call
DESCRIPTION:Stiftung Künstler*dorf Schöppingen offers residency fellowshi
 ps for professional visual artists\, authors and composers\; since 2023 ap
 plications from collectives are also accepted. The call is open to people 
 of all nationalities and ages\, and accommodation for partners\, family me
 mbers or assistance can be arranged upon agreement and availability (for p
 hysical\, cognitive or sensory disabilities\, contact the foundation to ex
 plain any barriers). Fellows reside and work at the Künstler*dorf for a p
 eriod of their choosing between two and six months\, using the premises an
 d infrastructure on-site. The stipend is EUR 1\,500 per month\, minus roug
 hly EUR 100-200 for rent and operating expenses\; for collectives it is EU
 R 1\,000 per person up to a maximum of EUR 3\,000 per month\, minus rent a
 nd operating costs. Travel\, insurance or material costs are covered only 
 in exceptional cases on request. The fellowship is not designed as a produ
 ction residency and there is no obligation to present results\, though exh
 ibition and presentation facilities are available and the voluntary co-org
 anization of events (lectures\, readings\, workshops or similar) is welcom
 ed. A willingness to participate in events and to engage in exchange with 
 other fellows is a prerequisite. Selection is made by expert juries on the
  quality of previous work\, the quality and plausibility of the project fo
 r Schöppingen\, and program contributions (interaction / community buildi
 ng). Applications for 2027 can be submitted from 27 June to 31 August 2026
  (midnight CET) via the online application portal.\n\nOrganisation: Stiftu
 ng Künstler*dorf Schöppingen\nLocation: Schöppingen\, Germany (residenc
 y requires living and working on-site)\nAward: Stipend of EUR 1\,500 per m
 onth\, minus roughly EUR 100-200 for rent and operating expenses. For coll
 ectives\, EUR 1\,000 per person up to a maximum of EUR 3\,000 per month\, 
 minus rent and operating costs. Travel\, insurance and material costs are 
 covered only in exceptional cases\, on request.\nDuration: Residency of tw
 o to six months during 2027\; period chosen by agreement and availability\
 n\nMore: https://stiftung-kuenstlerdorf.de/en/application_process?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://stiftung-kuenstlerdorf.de/en/application_process?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Schöppingen\, Germany (residency requires living and working on-s
 ite)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:phenomenon-grant-kerenidis-pepe-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Phenomenon Grant 2026 (Second Edition)
DESCRIPTION:Created in 2025\, the Phenomenon Grant is a yearly program that
  supports art projects in Greece with grants of EUR 5\,000 to EUR 10\,000 
 for individuals\, collectives\, and non-profit organizations engaged with 
 queer\, feminist\, anti-colonial\, and other emancipatory art practices. T
 he grant aims to support critical phases of artistic development\, enablin
 g experimentation\, research\, collaboration\, and the realization of new 
 and ongoing projects. In a context marked by rising authoritarianism\, gen
 ocidal forces and the dismantling of social justice advancements\, the pro
 gram seeks to support voices that critically and ethically engage with the
 se issues\, create spaces of resistance\, reimagine futures beyond exclusi
 on\, persecution\, and domination\, and open pathways toward collective li
 beration. Particular attention is paid to collaborative endeavours rooted 
 in Greece that are built on connections with the Global Majority\, promoti
 ng networks of translocal solidarity and interconnected struggles. Organiz
 ed and supported by Kerenidis Pepe\, which has pledged a total budget of E
 UR 60\,000 over 2025-2027\, the amount awarded is based on the application
 's actual funding needs. In 2025 the program received 193 applications and
  awarded three grants of EUR 8\,500 each to Athens Museum of Queer Arts (A
 MOQA)\, Forensic Architecture Initiative Athens (FAIA) and Theo Panagopoul
 os. The 2026 selection committee is composed of Athena Athanasiou\, Dora G
 arcia\, Christina Li\, VASKOS\, and chaired by Aggelika Mitsiou. The submi
 ssion period runs from 1 September to 30 September 2026\, with selected pr
 ojects announced in November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Phenomenon Association
 \, supported by Kerenidis Pepe\nLocation: Greece (projects rooted in Greec
 e)\nAward: Grants ranging from EUR 5\,000 to EUR 10\,000\, based on the ap
 plication's actual funding needs. Each edition awards a minimum of EUR 20\
 ,000 to two to four grantees (in 2025\, three grants of EUR 8\,500 each we
 re awarded).\nDuration: One- or two-year initiatives\; selected projects a
 nnounced November 2026\n\nMore: https://kerenidispepe.art/projects/grants/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://kerenidispepe.art/projects/grants/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Greece (projects rooted in Greece)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pee-workshop-matter-out-of-place-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Open Call: Pee Workshop (Matter Out of Place)
DESCRIPTION:Pee Workshop is looking for 16 performers to take part in a two
 -day research practice led by Nica Roses and Eris Kyriakides as part of th
 e dance and performance event Matter Out of Place. The workshop uses drink
 ing\, holding\, and releasing liquid as a starting point for choreographic
  research\, paying attention to the body's internal timing: what we consum
 e\, how liquid moves through the body\, what the bladder signals\, and how
  the organs produce movements that often go unnoticed. This cycle becomes 
 the structure of the research\, opening into dance\, somatic exercises\, c
 horeographic tasks\, group compositions\, and experiments with collective 
 energy. The material develops through urination but also through the share
 d experience of moving\, waiting\, responding\, and performing together. P
 erformers\, dancers\, movement practitioners\, and artists interested in s
 omatic research\, experimental choreography\, and collective performance-m
 aking are welcome\; professional dance training is not required\, but appl
 icants should feel comfortable working physically and collaboratively. All
  exercises are approached through clear consent\, respect for personal bou
 ndaries\, and collective care. The workshop takes place Monday 17 and Tues
 day 18 August 2026 from 10:00 to 17:00 at The Dirty Art Foundation\, culmi
 nating in an optional 60-minute public showing on Wednesday 19 August. Eac
 h performer receives a fee of EUR 200. To apply\, send a short introductio
 n about yourself\, your movement or performance experience\, and why you a
 re interested in the workshop to nicaxroses@gmail.com by 10 August 2026.\n
 \nOrganisation: Nica Roses & Eris Kyriakides\, at The Dirty Art Foundation
 \nLocation: The Dirty Art Foundation (Amsterdam\, Netherlands)\nAward: Per
 former fee of EUR 200. Participation in the optional public showing is not
  required to receive the fee.\nDuration: Two-day workshop\, Monday 17 and 
 Tuesday 18 August 2026\, 10:00-17:00\, with an optional 60-minute public s
 howing on Wednesday 19 August\n\nMore: https://www.instagram.com/p/DacdGWT
 jTSL/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.instagram.com/p/DacdGWTjTSL/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:The Dirty Art Foundation (Amsterdam\, Netherlands)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:princeton-hodder-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Hodder Fellowship 2027-2028 (Princeton University)
DESCRIPTION:The Hodder Fellowship is given to artists and writers of except
 ional promise to pursue independent projects at Princeton University durin
 g the academic year. Potential Hodder Fellows are composers\, choreographe
 rs\, performance artists\, visual artists\, writers\, translators\, or oth
 er kinds of artists or humanists who have 'much more than ordinary intelle
 ctual and literary gifts'\; they are selected more 'for promise than for p
 erformance.' Given the strength of the applicant pool\, most successful Fe
 llows have published a first book or have similar achievements in their ow
 n fields\; the Hodder is designed to provide Fellows with the 'studious le
 isure' to undertake significant new work. Fellows spend an academic year a
 t Princeton with no formal teaching\, supported by a USD 93\,000 stipend f
 or the 10-month Visiting Fellow appointment. IMPORTANT: for this cycle\, a
 pplications are accepted only for the Program in Dance\, the Program in Th
 eater and Music Theater\, the Program in Visual Arts\, and the Department 
 of Music. Fellowships are not intended to fund work leading to an advanced
  degree\; one need not be a U.S. citizen to apply\; previous recipients of
  the Princeton Arts Fellowship are not eligible. To apply\, submit a resum
 e\, a 500-word project proposal\, and samples of your work (writing sample
 \, images\, video links to performances\, etc.). Applicants may apply for 
 the Hodder Fellowship only twice in a lifetime. Applications are submitted
  via Interfolio. The application cycle closes 8 September 2026 at 11:59 p.
 m. EDT.\n\nOrganisation: Princeton University\, Lewis Center for the Arts\
 nLocation: Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA\; academic-year appointment as a V
 isiting Fellow (no U.S. citizenship required)\nAward: USD 93\,000 stipend 
 for the 10-month appointment as a Visiting Fellow. No formal teaching is i
 nvolved. No application fee.\nDuration: One academic year (10-month appoin
 tment)\, 2027-2028\n\nMore: https://arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/hodder-
 fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/hodder-fellowship/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA\; academic-year appointment as a Visi
 ting Fellow (no U.S. citizenship required)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:accent-sisters-work-study-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260801
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260802
SUMMARY:Deadline: Accent Sisters Work-Study Fellowship Program 2026
DESCRIPTION:Accent Sisters offers a work-study fellowship for individuals p
 assionate about literature\, visual arts\, and community engagement. The f
 ellowship provides hands-on experience in a bookstore and a hybrid event s
 pace where exhibitions\, book launches\, film screenings\, educational pro
 grams\, and other public programming take place. It is ideal for someone l
 ooking to build a career in cultural institutions\, independent bookstores
 \, or art galleries. Fellows work closely with both the bookstore and gall
 ery teams\, gaining skills in book curation\, exhibit planning\, marketing
 \, and community outreach\, while developing a personal project that merge
 s these disciplines. WHAT YOU GET: opportunities to work with writers\, ar
 tists\, and creators\; a chance to pitch projects\, events\, and programs 
 for a diverse community\; free passes to all in-person events and classes\
 ; fellow discounts\; a showcase of your personal project toward the end of
  the fellowship\; one-on-one mentorship with the Accent team\; and a USD 6
 00 stipend. Open to upcoming seniors\, upcoming second-year graduate stude
 nts\, and recent graduates of undergraduate/graduate programs. Four tracks
  are offered and applicants generally choose one: Program Coordination & C
 ommunity Engagement\; Curation & Administration\; Graphic Design & Digital
  Asset Management\; or Marketing & Business Development. The fellowship ru
 ns 20 August to 20 December 2026 (part-time\, 3 days per week\, on-site re
 quired). Applications are reviewed from 15 July to 1 August 2026.\n\nOrgan
 isation: Accent Sisters\nLocation: 89 5th Ave\, Suite 702\, New York\, NY 
 10003 (hybrid\; part-time\, 3 days/week\, on-site work required)\nAward: S
 tipend of USD 600 (paid in two installments)\, plus free passes to all in-
 person events and classes\, fellow discounts\, and one-on-one mentorship.\
 nDuration: 20 August to 20 December 2026\; part-time\, 3 days per week\n\n
 More: https://www.accentaccent.com/page/fellowship?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.accentaccent.com/page/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:89 5th Ave\, Suite 702\, New York\, NY 10003 (hybrid\; part-time\,
  3 days/week\, on-site work required)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:web-residencies-no-22-ignore-all-previous-instructions-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260807
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260808
SUMMARY:Deadline: Akademie Schloss Solitude: Web Residencies No. 22 - Ignor
 e All Previous Instructions
DESCRIPTION:Web Residencies No. 22\, 'Ignore All Previous Instructions\,' i
 s curated by !Mediengruppe Bitnik as part of Akademie Schloss Solitude's W
 eb Residencies program\, which since 2016 has supported young talents from
  the international digital scene as well as artists of all disciplines eng
 aging with web-based practices. For the tenth anniversary of the program\,
  this edition invites artists to create works that challenge extractive di
 gital platforms and AI systems\, exploring how to interrupt dominant techn
 ological logics\, from ad-tech surveillance to prompt injection vulnerabil
 ities\, and imagine alternative structures and communication methods. The 
 open call seeks proposals that hijack\, glitch\, and disrupt rather than c
 omply with existing systems\, drawing on strategies like Situationist deto
 urnement to map routes through and around the grids we are trapped within.
  Four to six proposals will be selected. BENEFITS: a four-week online resi
 dency\; a grant of EUR 1\,200\; a web space (microsite) on which the proje
 ct can be developed and published online\; support from a web/UX designer 
 and a programmer (in a supportive capacity only\, not to build an entire w
 ebsite)\; and participation in individual and group online events with the
  curators and Solitude fellows. Offline or hybrid formats (a reading sessi
 on\, panel\, exhibition\, or curated program) may follow. ELIGIBILITY: art
 ists of all disciplines may apply\, with no age limit\; submission is free
  of charge. The residency takes place exclusively online\, and selected fe
 llows agree to be available online during the four weeks. TIMELINE: the op
 en call runs from July 7 to August 7\, 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Akademie Sch
 loss Solitude\nLocation: Online (fully remote). Awarded by Akademie Schlos
 s Solitude\, Stuttgart\, Germany.\nAward: EUR 1\,200 grant for a four-week
  online residency\, plus a microsite to develop and publish the project\, 
 and support from a web/UX designer and a programmer in a supportive capaci
 ty.\nDuration: Four-week online residency.\n\nMore: https://application.ak
 ademie-solitude.de/fellowship-information/web-residencies?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://application.akademie-solitude.de/fellowship-information/web-res
 idencies?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online (fully remote). Awarded by Akademie Schloss Solitude\, Stut
 tgart\, Germany.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-publication-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260730
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260731
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Publication
DESCRIPTION:The Mondriaan Fund's Publication grant supports the production 
 of special publications about living visual artists from the Netherlands o
 r the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. It funds books and other publications
  that give substantial\, high-quality attention to an artist's work and pr
 actice. WHO CAN APPLY: visual artists and publishers\, individually or joi
 ntly\; the featured artist(s) must be professional and have been active fo
 r at least one year\, based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of th
 e Kingdom. AMOUNT: the Mondriaan Fund contributes a maximum of 35% of the 
 eligible expenses\; the available budget for this round is EUR 205\,000. A
 PPLYING: submitted through the Mondriaan Fund's online application portal\
 , which requires a pre-created account (allow several working days to set 
 up). DEADLINE: 30 July 2026\, 16:00 Dutch time (10:00 Caribbean time).\n\n
 Organisation: Mondriaan Fund (Mondriaan Fonds)\nLocation: Netherlands. Fea
 tured artist(s) must be professional visual artists based in the Netherlan
 ds or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom\; publishers may co-apply.\nAward:
  Contribution of up to 35% of eligible expenses. Total budget for this rou
 nd is EUR 205\,000.\nDuration: Project-based (production of a publication)
 .\n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/publi
 cation-2/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/publication-2
 /?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands. Featured artist(s) must be professional visual artist
 s based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom\; publishe
 rs may co-apply.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260827
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260828
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Residency
DESCRIPTION:The Mondriaan Fund's Residency grant supports professional visu
 al artists\, curators\, and art observers who want to deepen their practic
 e through a residency in the Netherlands or abroad. It contributes to the 
 costs of a self-arranged residency that offers meaningful development for 
 the applicant's work. WHO CAN APPLY: visual artists\, curators\, and art o
 bservers who have been professionally active for at least one year and are
  based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. AMOUNT: th
 e grant amount varies depending on the residency's location and duration\;
  applicants must contribute at least 10% of the costs themselves\, through
  personal investment\, third-party contributions\, an interest-free loan\,
  or crowdfunding. APPLYING: submitted through the Mondriaan Fund's online 
 application portal\, which requires a pre-created account (allow several w
 orking days). DEADLINE: 27 August 2026\, 16:00 Dutch time (11:00 Caribbean
  time).\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund (Mondriaan Fonds)\nLocation: Nethe
 rlands. Applicants must be based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part 
 of the Kingdom\; residencies may take place in the Netherlands or abroad.\
 nAward: Grant amount varies depending on the location and duration of the 
 residency. Applicants must contribute at least 10% themselves (personal in
 vestment\, third-party contributions\, an interest-free loan\, or crowdfun
 ding).\nDuration: Varies by residency.\n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds
 .nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/residency/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands. Applicants must be based in the Netherlands or the Ca
 ribbean part of the Kingdom\; residencies may take place in the Netherland
 s or abroad.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:prospect-art-new-work-we-come-in-pieces-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260816
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260817
SUMMARY:Deadline: Prospect Art: NEW WORK Commission 2027 - "We Come in Piec
 es"
DESCRIPTION:Prospect Art invites visual artists worldwide to apply for the 
 2027 NEW WORK commission\, a USD 1\,500 project grant supporting the creat
 ion of a new\, conceptually driven work responding to the curatorial theme
  "We Come in Pieces\," developed by 2026-2027 Curatorial Fellow Alma Samme
 l. Through a single application\, artists are also considered for ONE WORK
  (an online series featuring an in-depth conversation centered on a single
  completed artwork) and BROADCAST (a curated online program of screenings\
 , artist talks\, and discussions). THEME: "We Come in Pieces" proposes the
  Alien as a method for shifting perspective\, approaching alienness as a p
 ractice of estrangement to encounter the world beyond familiar human-cente
 red narratives. It explores relationships between human and more-than-huma
 n worlds\, speculative cosmologies\, ecological systems\, technologies\, l
 andscapes\, histories\, infrastructures\, and nonhuman forms of intelligen
 ce (satellites and orbital debris\, fungal networks\, oceans\, atmospheric
  systems\, algorithmic intelligences)\, inviting works that displace the p
 oint of listening\, seeing\, sensing\, or knowing. Research-based and conc
 eptually driven practices are especially encouraged. WHAT'S OFFERED: NEW W
 ORK\, USD 1\,500 to one artist plus a featured website section and social 
 promotion (project completed within 12 months)\; ONE WORK\, a published ar
 ticle and social promotion\; BROADCAST\, a USD 125 screening or speaking f
 ee and inclusion in a themed program. ELIGIBILITY: professional visual art
 ists working in any medium at any career stage\, except those currently en
 rolled in undergraduate or graduate studio art programs\; collectives may 
 apply. Media welcomed include moving image\, installation\, sculpture\, ph
 otography\, performance\, sound\, digital media\, social practice\, and in
 terdisciplinary forms. APPLICATION: submit via the official Google Form wi
 th contact information\, a project description (250 words)\, a sketch/visu
 alization (1-3 images)\, up to 3 links to completed work samples\, a state
 ment relating the project to the theme (200 words)\, a short bio and exhib
 ition history (500 words)\, and a budget breakdown of the USD 1\,500. A US
 D 10 administrative fee is paid via PayPal (include PayPal name and transa
 ction ID)\; a fee waiver is available with no explanation by emailing subm
 issions@prospectart.org before the deadline. Writers may email gioj@prospe
 ctart.org with samples. DEADLINE: 16 August 2026\; results for the NEW WOR
 K grant notified by end of September. INFO SESSION: 5 August 2026 at noon 
 PST.\n\nOrganisation: Prospect Art\nLocation: Remote / online. Open to vis
 ual artists worldwide\; programs (ONE WORK\, BROADCAST) run online.\nAward
 : NEW WORK: USD 1\,500 commission for one artist\, plus a featured website
  section and social promotion. BROADCAST: USD 125 screening or speaking fe
 e if selected. ONE WORK: a published article (exposure). NOTE: a USD 10 ad
 ministrative fee applies to apply\, with a no-questions-asked waiver avail
 able on request before the deadline. PayPal account required to receive fu
 nds.\nDuration: The commissioned NEW WORK project should be completed with
 in twelve months of selection.\n\nMore: https://www.prospectart.org/new-wo
 rk-grant?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.prospectart.org/new-work-grant?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote / online. Open to visual artists worldwide\; programs (ONE 
 WORK\, BROADCAST) run online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:black-arts-centre-call-for-submissions-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
SUMMARY:Deadline: Black Arts Centre: Call for Exhibition and Programming Pr
 oposals
DESCRIPTION:The Black Arts Centre is calling for proposals from artists for
  exhibitions and public programming. Exhibition proposals are considered f
 or Spring 2027 and beyond\, while programming proposals are considered for
  projects taking place from September 2026 onward. The submission portal i
 s open from 1 June to 8 August 2026. Proposals are reviewed by the Black A
 rts Centre team and a revolving committee of external community members\, 
 evaluated using an internal rubric\; applicants begin to hear back in Sept
 ember. SUPPORT: successful proposals receive financial\, budgeting\, and c
 uratorial support from the Black Arts Centre\, including project execution
 \, production\, logistics\, and marketing. ELIGIBILITY AND PRIORITY: appli
 cants do not need to identify as Black to submit\, but priority is given t
 o Black artists\, curators\, and art organizers\, and to artists and proje
 cts that align with the gallery's mandate (BIPOC\, Disabled\, Queer\, Immi
 grant/Refugee/Newcomer perspectives). Submission does not guarantee approv
 al for this cycle\, but all proposals are kept on file for future consider
 ation\, and updated or alternate proposals are accepted. Questions and PDF
  proposals/portfolios: info@theblackartscentre.ca. Deadline 8 August 2026.
 \n\nOrganisation: Black Arts Centre\nLocation: Surrey\, British Columbia\,
  Canada. Priority to Black artists\, curators\, and art organizers\, and t
 o projects aligned with the gallery's mandate (BIPOC\, Disabled\, Queer\, 
 Immigrant/Refugee/Newcomer perspectives).\nAward: Successful proposals rec
 eive financial\, budgeting\, and curatorial support from the Black Arts Ce
 ntre\, including project execution\, production\, logistics\, and marketin
 g. No submission fee.\nDuration: Exhibition proposals considered for Sprin
 g 2027 and beyond\; programming proposals for projects taking place from S
 eptember 2026 onward. Submission portal open 1 June to 8 August 2026\; app
 licants begin to hear back in September.\n\nMore: https://theblackartscent
 re.ca/submissions/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://theblackartscentre.ca/submissions/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Surrey\, British Columbia\, Canada. Priority to Black artists\, cu
 rators\, and art organizers\, and to projects aligned with the gallery's m
 andate (BIPOC\, Disabled\, Queer\, Immigrant/Refugee/Newcomer perspectives
 ).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gallery-penn-college-exhibitions-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Gallery at Penn College: 2027-28 Call for Exhibitions
DESCRIPTION:The Gallery at Penn College (Pennsylvania College of Technology
 \, Williamsport\, PA) invites artists to apply for solo\, two-person\, or 
 group exhibitions for its 2027-28 season. All entries are juried through a
  blind selection process by a gallery committee of faculty\, staff\, K-12 
 art educators\, and community artists\; review takes place during the Fall
  semester and artists receive notification in January. PROVIDED: housing\,
  meals\, a solo exhibition honorarium\, and a shipping and travel stipend.
  Promotional support includes an annual gallery catalog\, postcards\, pres
 s releases\, and web and social media. THE SPACE: the Gallery is 3\,000 sq
 uare feet with 82-250 running feet of linear wall space and an 11'4" ceili
 ng. All exhibitions are installed by Gallery staff\; site-specific install
 ations requiring artist installation need a planning document approved bef
 ore scheduling. Artists must have enough work for a solo exhibition unless
  applying for a two-person show. ENGAGEMENT: campus visits are an importan
 t part of programming (some exhibitions are scheduled without a visit). Pe
 nn College offers a B.S. in Graphic Design with foundation courses in draw
 ing\, 2D and 3D design\, visual literacy\, painting\, ceramics\, wood scul
 pture\, printmaking\, and computer graphics\; artists are encouraged to pr
 opose class-specific programs and optional community workshops. Visiting a
 rtists give a 20-30 minute gallery talk during the public reception (held 
 Tuesdays through Thursdays). TO APPLY: 10-15 digital images of work (JPG/J
 PEG only\, max 4 MB each)\, an image list with dimensions and medium\, a C
 V\, and an artist statement. For two-person or group proposals\, provide d
 etails in the comments\; for video work\, upload a PDF with video links an
 d details. Deadline 31 August 2026 for the 2027-28 season.\n\nOrganisation
 : The Gallery at Penn College (Pennsylvania College of Technology)\nLocati
 on: Williamsport\, Pennsylvania\, United States (campus of Pennsylvania Co
 llege of Technology). Williamsport Regional Airport connects to Washington
 -Dulles\; no other direct flights or trains.\nAward: Housing\, meals\, a s
 olo exhibition honorarium\, and a shipping and travel stipend are provided
 . No application fee.\nDuration: Exhibitions scheduled during the 2027-28 
 season. Jurying takes place in the Fall 2026 semester\; artists are notifi
 ed in January 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.pct.edu/gallery/exhibitions-entry
 -form?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.pct.edu/gallery/exhibitions-entry-form?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Williamsport\, Pennsylvania\, United States (campus of Pennsylvani
 a College of Technology). Williamsport Regional Airport connects to Washin
 gton-Dulles\; no other direct flights or trains.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:railyard-art-project-santa-fe-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260805
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:Deadline: Railyard Art Project: Call for Temporary Public Art (Sant
 a Fe)
DESCRIPTION:The Railyard Art Project (RAP) facilitates temporary public art
  installations by established and emerging artists and arts collectives in
  Santa Fe's Railyard Park + Plaza. RAP is accepting proposals for temporar
 y public art installations and creative projects\; thanks to funding from 
 Santa Fe's Arts and Culture Commission\, two projects will be hosted betwe
 en October 2026 and June 2027\, each receiving a USD 3\,500 honorarium plu
 s marketing support and liability insurance. THEMES: the RAP Committee enc
 ourages collaboration across disciplines (art\, science\, history) and wel
 comes thoughtful\, challenging\, playful\, and edifying art in the context
  of social and cultural expression and environmental sustainability. Propo
 sed projects must physically withstand outdoor public display and engage t
 he community by responding to the natural and social landscape of the park
 . SELECTION CRITERIA: overall artistic merit\; context and familiarity wit
 h the proposed site\; practical viability\; and potential for community en
 gagement and cultural/educational enrichment. Artists should select an eli
 gible area of the park's open space and describe the project in detail\; p
 hotos or renderings may be uploaded with the application (a map of Railyar
 d Art Locations is linked from the program page). The RAP Committee\, made
  up of community members and Park + Plaza stakeholders\, selects and sched
 ules the exhibitions\; a formal in-person presentation may be requested de
 pending on complexity. CONDITIONS: the park operates under a Conservation 
 Easement\, so installations cannot be located on planted areas\, no stakin
 g of any kind is permitted (including during install/de-install)\, and not
 hing may be hung from trees. Artists lend artwork at their own risk (no pr
 otection against vandalism or weather damage\, and artists are responsible
  for repairs). Installs and de-installs must be coordinated in advance wit
 h the RAP Committee or Railyard Park Conservancy staff\, with a Committee 
 or staff member on site throughout. Application deadline 5 August 2026.\n\
 nOrganisation: Railyard Art Project (Railyard Park + Plaza)\nLocation: San
 ta Fe\, New Mexico\, United States (Railyard Park + Plaza\, City-owned pro
 perty under a Conservation Easement).\nAward: USD 3\,500 honorarium per pr
 oject (two projects selected)\, plus marketing support and liability insur
 ance. Funded by Santa Fe's Arts and Culture Commission. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Two projects hosted between October 2026 and June 2027.\n\nMo
 re: https://railyardpark.org/public-art-program/?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://railyardpark.org/public-art-program/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Santa Fe\, New Mexico\, United States (Railyard Park + Plaza\, Cit
 y-owned property under a Conservation Easement).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fundacion-ama-amoedo-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: Fundacion Ama Amoedo Grants 2026 (4th edition)
DESCRIPTION:The Fundacion Ama Amoedo Grants provide resources and opportuni
 ties to strengthen the visual arts ecosystem and its institutions. Through
  an open call\, ten grants of USD 10\,000 are awarded annually across four
  categories: Artists\, Art and Social Engagement\, Organizations\, and Pub
 lications. The program supports the realization of projects by artists\, o
 rganizations\, and institutions with a meaningful connection to Latin Amer
 ica. This is the 4th edition of the grants\; the Foundation receives appli
 cations via its online platform from 7 July to 9 September 2026. ELIGIBILI
 TY: artists\, collectives\, art professionals\, non-profit organizations\,
  institutions\, and foundations with a significant connection to Latin Ame
 rica\, whether through nationality\, cultural heritage\, and/or the locati
 on where the project will take place. Refer to the Terms and Conditions do
 cument on the website for full details. Inquiries: info@fundacionamaamoedo
 .org. Application deadline: Wednesday 9 September 2026\, 11:59 pm.\n\nOrga
 nisation: Fundacion Ama Amoedo\nLocation: Open to applicants with a signif
 icant connection to Latin America\, through nationality\, cultural heritag
 e\, and/or the location where the project takes place.\nAward: Ten grants 
 of USD 10\,000 each\, awarded annually across four categories: Artists\, A
 rt and Social Engagement\, Organizations\, and Publications. No applicatio
 n fee mentioned.\nDuration: Applications accepted via the online platform 
 from 7 July to 9 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://www.fundacionamaamoedo.o
 rg/programas/becas?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://www.fundacionamaamoedo.org/programas/becas?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open to applicants with a significant connection to Latin America\
 , through nationality\, cultural heritage\, and/or the location where the 
 project takes place.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:target-margin-tmt-institute-fellowship-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260722
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260723
SUMMARY:Deadline: Target Margin Theater: TMT Institute Fellowship 2026-27
DESCRIPTION:Target Margin Theater invites applications for its next cohort 
 of TMT Institute Fellows for the 2026/2027 season. The Institute is a coho
 rt of artists who seek to radically disrupt and re-examine their existing 
 practice without any goal or expected result: pure experimentation with no
  product-oriented end goal. Fellows pursue no project\, no developmental s
 tage\, no deliverable\; misfires\, dead ends\, and bad ideas are considere
 d a fruitful part of the work. SUPPORT: TMT provides space\, material reso
 urces\, collaborators\, and a USD 2\,000 stipend. STRUCTURE: three short i
 ntensive sessions at Target Margin Theater in Brooklyn (5-8 November 2026\
 , 8-10 January 2027\, and 19-21 March 2027\, all day with some evening hou
 rs)\, monthly breakfasts at Junior's in Brooklyn (20 October\, 30 November
 \, 4 January\, 1 February\, 8 March\, 5 April\, 3 May)\, and occasional ex
 cursions. After the second intensive\, each Fellow commits to an explorati
 on pursued outside group gatherings\, supported by TMT resources. The seas
 on ends with an Open Studio weekend in June 2027 to share explorations wit
 h the community\; this is not a performance and should not be an audience-
 attended\, time-bounded show. WHO CAN APPLY: artists from any discipline w
 ithin or outside theater\, as well as production\, technical\, and adminis
 trative workers in the arts. Applicants must have an established\, existin
 g body of work and practice they are committed to changing\; do not apply 
 with a project. Recent graduates of training programs are unlikely to be a
 ppropriate candidates\; no formal education level or degree is required. S
 ELECTION: TMT staff and artistic community review applications and invite 
 a handful of candidates to interview\; all applicants are notified by Octo
 ber. Deadline: Wednesday 22 July 2026\, 11:59 pm EST.\n\nOrganisation: Tar
 get Margin Theater\nLocation: Brooklyn\, New York\, United States (Sunset 
 Park). Fellows must be available in person for intensives\, monthly breakf
 asts\, and excursions.\nAward: USD 2\,000 stipend\, plus space\, material 
 resources\, and collaborators to support each Fellow's experiments. No app
 lication fee.\nDuration: 2026-27 season: three intensive sessions in Brook
 lyn (5-8 November 2026\, 8-10 January 2027\, 19-21 March 2027)\, monthly b
 reakfasts October 2026 through May 2027\, and an Open Studio weekend in Ju
 ne 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.targetmargin.org/institute/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.targetmargin.org/institute/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Brooklyn\, New York\, United States (Sunset Park). Fellows must be
  available in person for intensives\, monthly breakfasts\, and excursions.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:arts-council-ireland-visual-arts-project-award-2027@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260723
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260724
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arts Council of Ireland: Visual Arts Project Award 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Arts Council of Ireland's Visual Arts Project Award funds p
 rofessional visual artists\, curators\, and producers to develop and deliv
 er visual arts projects\, in three strands: planning and pre-production (u
 p to EUR 12\,000)\, production and engagement (up to EUR 60\,000)\, and pa
 rtnership production (up to EUR 150\,000). Primarily aimed at applicants b
 ased in the Republic of Ireland\, but the Arts Council's standard eligibil
 ity clause allows applicants based elsewhere where the application explici
 tly demonstrates how the outcomes will benefit the arts in Ireland. The wi
 der Project Award 2027 covers nine disciplines including film and music on
  a similar timeline. Deadline 23 July 2026\, 17:30 Irish time.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Arts Council of Ireland / An Chomhairle Ealaion\nLocation: Republic 
 of Ireland. Applicants based outside Ireland may apply if the application 
 clearly shows how outcomes benefit the arts in the Republic of Ireland.\nA
 ward: Three strands: up to EUR 12\,000 (planning and pre-production)\, up 
 to EUR 60\,000 (production and engagement)\, up to EUR 150\,000 (partnersh
 ip production). No application fee.\nDuration: Projects delivered in 2027.
 \n\nMore: https://artscouncil.ie/funding-opportunities/project-award/visua
 l-arts-project-award/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=ref
 erral
URL:https://artscouncil.ie/funding-opportunities/project-award/visual-arts-
 project-award/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Republic of Ireland. Applicants based outside Ireland may apply if
  the application clearly shows how outcomes benefit the arts in the Republ
 ic of Ireland.
END:VEVENT
