BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:youth-music-trailblazer-fund-round-13-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Youth Music: Trailblazer Fund (Round 13)
DESCRIPTION:Youth Music's Trailblazer Fund gives GBP 2\,000 to GBP 30\,000 
 to organisations in England running projects that let children and young p
 eople aged 25 or under make\, learn and earn in music. A project should do
  at least one of three things: trial work or test a new way of working\, s
 ustain a grassroots programme\, or disrupt the status quo. Doing all three
  is welcome. THEMES: your work must meet one of these: early years\; Disab
 led\, d/Deaf and neurodivergent young people\; the youth justice system\; 
 young people facing barriers\; young adults\; or organisations and the wor
 kforce. WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE IN PRACTICE\, using Youth Music's own examples:
  a new CIC testing ways to help nurseries embed music-making into the day\
 ; a small grassroots music organisation sustaining a programme that alread
 y works\; a Music Education Hub setting up a Youth Board with real budget 
 and decision-making power over how the hub is run\; or an established yout
 h centre trying a music programme for the first time. ROUND 13: opened 31 
 July 2026\, deadline 28 August 2026 at 5pm\, notification 11 December 2026
 \, for projects starting between January and March 2027. ROUND 14: opens 2
 3 October 2026\, deadline 20 November 2026 at 5pm\, notification 19 March 
 2027\, for projects starting between April and June 2027. All deadlines ar
 e 5pm. NOTE: this is a grant to ORGANISATIONS\, not to individual musician
 s or composers.\n\nOrganisation: Youth Music\nLocation: England only. Proj
 ects for children and young people aged 25 or under.\nAward: GBP 2\,000 to
  GBP 30\,000 for organisations in England. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 Round 13 projects start between January and March 2027. Round opened 31 Ju
 ly 2026\; deadline 28 August 2026 at 5pm\; notification 11 December 2026.\
 n\nMore: https://www.youthmusic.org.uk/funding/i-need-funding/trailblazer-
 fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.youthmusic.org.uk/funding/i-need-funding/trailblazer-fund?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England only. Projects for children and young people aged 25 or un
 der.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:screen-argyll-sea-change-animated-women-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Screen Argyll: Sea Change - Animated Women 2026-27 (envir
 onmental animation development)
DESCRIPTION:Sea Change is a seven-month professional development programme 
 from Screen Argyll for six UK-based women and marginalised gender animator
 s developing animated feature film projects\, with environmental storytell
 ing at the centre of the brief. It runs a project from concept toward scre
 en: online workshops in early September 2026\, a residential lab on the Is
 le of Tiree from 15 to 22 September 2026\, monthly mentoring from October 
 2026 to February 2027\, a project showcase at MOVE Summit in February 2027
 \, and final submissions in March 2027. WHAT PARTICIPANTS GET: GBP 150 per
  day for 20 days of participation\, which comes to GBP 3\,000\; accommodat
 ion covered during the residential lab\; travel support\; and industry pas
 ses for MOVE Summit and Glasgow Film Festival 2027. ELIGIBILITY: UK reside
 nt\, over 18\, a woman or marginalised gender animator\, not in full-time 
 education when the programme begins\, with prior experience making short-f
 orm or longer animation\, and a feature project at early-to-mid developmen
 t stage. NOTE THE TIMING: the deadline is 10 August 2026 and selected proj
 ects are notified on 17 August 2026\, so this is a short-turnaround applic
 ation.\n\nOrganisation: Screen Argyll\nLocation: UK-based applicants. Onli
 ne workshops plus a residential lab on the Isle of Tiree\, Scotland\, and 
 a showcase at MOVE Summit\, Edinburgh.\nAward: GBP 150 per day for 20 days
  of participation (GBP 3\,000 total)\, plus covered accommodation during t
 he residential lab\, travel support\, and industry passes for MOVE Summit 
 and Glasgow Film Festival 2027. No application fee.\nDuration: Seven month
 s\, September 2026 to March 2027. Deadline 10 August 2026\; selections ann
 ounced 17 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://screenargyll.co.uk/sea-change-anim
 ated-women/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://screenargyll.co.uk/sea-change-animated-women/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:UK-based applicants. Online workshops plus a residential lab on th
 e Isle of Tiree\, Scotland\, and a showcase at MOVE Summit\, Edinburgh.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dot-line-space-water-bodies-ecology-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dot Line Space Art Foundation: Water Bodies & Ecology 202
 6 (Mumbai)
DESCRIPTION:Water Bodies & Ecology 2026 is a 40-day multidisciplinary initi
 ative from the Dot Line Space Art Foundation in Mumbai\, bringing artists\
 , researchers\, collectives and communities together across research activ
 ities\, installations and exhibitions at two venues. The premise is artist
 ic engagement with water\, ecology\, climate\, memory and environmental co
 nsciousness\, and the programme is explicitly after research-based and sit
 e-responsive approaches rather than finished gallery objects\, aiming for 
 dialogue between contemporary practice and ecological concerns. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: individual artists\, artist groups\, interdisciplinary practitioners\
 , community-based initiatives\, and collectives and collaborative practice
 s\, working across all mediums and disciplines. WHAT IS OFFERED: selected 
 artists receive grants towards travel and production expenses. Be aware th
 at the published call does not give amounts\, does not mention an artist f
 ee\, and does not mention accommodation\, so confirm the terms with the fo
 undation (info@dotlinespace.co.in) before committing to travel. Applicatio
 ns go through a Google Form linked from the project page. ABOUT THE HOST: 
 Dot Line Space was co-founded in 2015 by Gourmoni Das\, Piyush Bavalia and
  Shruti Bhosle\, all graduates of Sir J.J. School of Art in Mumbai.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Dot Line Space Art Foundation\nLocation: Mumbai\, India. Presen
 ted across two venues\; the foundation operates out of the New Great Easte
 rn Mills in Byculla\, where it runs Nine Fish Art Gallery. Open to artists
  internationally.\nAward: Grants towards travel and production expenses fo
 r selected artists. The call does not publish fixed amounts and does not s
 tate an artist fee or accommodation. No application fee.\nDuration: A 40-d
 ay multidisciplinary programme in 2026 across two Mumbai venues. Deadline 
 30 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://www.dotlinespace.co.in/water-bodies-and-e
 cology-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.dotlinespace.co.in/water-bodies-and-ecology-2026/?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Mumbai\, India. Presented across two venues\; the foundation opera
 tes out of the New Great Eastern Mills in Byculla\, where it runs Nine Fis
 h Art Gallery. Open to artists internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:julies-bicycle-iter-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: ITER 2026: International Touring and Environmental Respon
 sibility Programme
DESCRIPTION:ITER is now in its fifth year and supports people in the perfor
 ming arts to build environmentally responsible\, inclusive and equitable w
 ays of touring and collaborating internationally. It is initiated and fina
 nced by Arts Council England\, Arts Council Norway\, Creative Scotland and
  the Danish Arts Foundation\, and designed and delivered by Julie's Bicycl
 e and in futurum. Creative Scotland joined as a partner for 2026. TWO TRAC
 KS\, up to 60 places in total: PATHFINDERS\, ten sessions introducing the 
 principles of sustainable touring for people new to the subject\; and CHAN
 GEMAKERS\, nine sessions for those further along\, on leadership and syste
 mic change. Seminars run weekly from 1 October to 10 December 2026\, Pathf
 inders on Tuesdays and Changemakers on Thursdays\, 10:00 to 12:00 UK time\
 , alongside networking and a new alumni network. THE MONEY COMES IN PHASE 
 TWO: after the seminars\, groups of three or more participants can apply f
 or up to GBP 30\,000 to implement collaborative projects that cut environm
 ental impacts and advance climate justice. Funding applications open 6 Nov
 ember 2026 and close 11 January 2027\; awarded projects begin in spring 20
 27. ELIGIBILITY: based in England\, Scotland\, Norway or Denmark\, working
  in dance\, music or theatre. That covers music and theatre venues receivi
 ng touring work\, festivals\, orchestras\, theatre companies\, solo artist
 s\, performers and musicians\, performing arts producers\, and multidiscip
 linary arts organisations. Applications are made through the relevant nati
 onal funder (Arts Council England open funds\, Creative Scotland targeted 
 funding\, Arts Council Norway\, Danish Arts Foundation). Questions go to e
 nvironmentaltouring@artscouncil.org.uk.\n\nOrganisation: Julie's Bicycle a
 nd in futurum\, funded by Arts Council England\, Arts Council Norway\, Cre
 ative Scotland and the Danish Arts Foundation\nLocation: England\, Scotlan
 d\, Norway and Denmark. Seminars run online.\nAward: The seminar programme
  is free to join. Groups of three or more participants can then apply for 
 up to GBP 30\,000 for collaborative projects\, with funding applications o
 pen 6 November 2026 to 11 January 2027 and projects starting spring 2027. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: Seminars run weekly from 1 October to 10 De
 cember 2026\, Tuesdays for Pathfinders and Thursdays for Changemakers\, 10
 :00 to 12:00 UK time. Deadline to join 1 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://
 juliesbicycle.org/news/iter-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://juliesbicycle.org/news/iter-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England\, Scotland\, Norway and Denmark. Seminars run online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:earth-partner-prize-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261007
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261008
SUMMARY:Deadline: Earth Partner Prize 2026 (climate and ecological justice\
 , ages 14 to 30)
DESCRIPTION:The Earth Partner Prize is a global open call for young creativ
 es making work about climate action and ecological justice\, run by Earth 
 Partner with the Global Environment Facility and Re:wild\, and timed to am
 plify youth voices ahead of the UN climate conference in November 2026. IT
  IS NOT A PHOTOGRAPHY PRIZE: the call takes work across disciplines\, and 
 the organisers explicitly list photography projects\, docu-style and exper
 imental film\, performance art\, app development\, spoken word\, musical c
 ompositions\, dance\, fashion design\, new media and social media projects
 . PRIZES: USD 10\,000 for first prize\, USD 5\,000 each for two runners-up
 \, USD 2\,000 each for five finalists\, plus an Impact Award and twenty ho
 nourable mentions. ELIGIBILITY: aged 14 to 30 at the time of submission\, 
 any nationality\, race\, gender or faith\; applicants under 18 need parent
 al consent. SUBMISSION NOTES: entry is free\, and entrants are asked to di
 sclose any use of AI tools and to mention prior awards or press coverage i
 n the project description. Submissions are handled on Picter\; deadline 7 
 October 2026 at 21:59.\n\nOrganisation: Earth Partner\, with the Global En
 vironment Facility (GEF) and Re:wild\nLocation: Worldwide\, any nationalit
 y. Entrants must be aged 14 to 30 at the time of submission\; under-18s ne
 ed parental consent.\nAward: First prize USD 10\,000\; two runner-up award
 s of USD 5\,000\; five finalist awards of USD 2\,000\; plus one Impact Awa
 rd and twenty honourable mentions. Free to enter.\nDuration: Deadline 7 Oc
 tober 2026\, 21:59. Timed to feed into the UN climate conference (COP) in 
 November 2026.\n\nMore: https://site.picter.com/earth-partner-prize-2026?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://site.picter.com/earth-partner-prize-2026?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\, any nationality. Entrants must be aged 14 to 30 at the
  time of submission\; under-18s need parental consent.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:connect-and-create-circulation-performing-arts-music-2026@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Connect and Create: Circulation of Performing Arts and Mu
 sic (Africa-Europe) - 2nd Call
DESCRIPTION:The Circulation strand of Connect and Create funds the touring 
 and dissemination of African artistic creations across Africa and Europe. 
 It is part of the Africa-Europe Partnerships for Culture programme (EUR 8M
 \, led by the Goethe-Institut and Expertise France in partnership with Ins
 titut francais\, supported by the European Union)\, and is a different str
 and from the Professional Mobilities call also on the desk. THIS CARD IS T
 HE SECOND CALL\, deadline 30 September 2026 (the first call closed 30 Nove
 mber 2025). AMOUNT: EUR 15\,000 to EUR 39\,500 per project. WHAT IS FUNDED
 : mobility costs for artistic teams\, including transport\, visas\, insura
 nce\, lodging\, per diems\, local transport\, and shipping of scenographic
  and technical elements. WHO CAN APPLY: artistic teams (performing arts co
 mpanies\, music ensembles\, collectives) based in sub-Saharan Africa\; dis
 semination structures (production houses\, artist managers\, theatres\, la
 bels\, venues) based in Africa or Europe\; and event organisers (festivals
 \, markets\, professional gatherings) in Africa or Europe. Applicants must
  be legally established in one of the 47 eligible sub-Saharan African coun
 tries\, Kenya among them\, or one of the 27 EU Member States. INDIVIDUALS 
 CANNOT APPLY. ELIGIBLE PROJECTS\, two formats: (1) TOURS\, requiring a min
 imum of 5 pre-booked dates across at least 3 different African countries\,
  or at least 3 pre-booked dates in one or more European countries\; (2) PR
 OGRAMMING WITHIN MAJOR EVENTS\, requiring a festival\, market or professio
 nal gathering in Africa or Europe to host at least 3 artistic teams from d
 ifferent African countries. TIMELINE: activities must take place between F
 ebruary and December 2027\; selection committee December 2026\, results an
 nounced January 2027. PRIORITY is given to projects that build on existing
  tandems already collaborating\, feature emerging artists\, use eco-respon
 sible practices\, and address inclusion. APPLY via the Institut francais e
 mundus portal. Questions: connectandcreate@expertisefrance.fr.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Goethe-Institut and Expertise France\, in collaboration with Institu
 t francais (Africa-Europe Partnerships for Culture consortium)\nLocation: 
 Applicants based in one of the 47 eligible sub-Saharan African countries (
 Kenya included) or one of the 27 EU Member States. Touring in Africa and/o
 r Europe.\nAward: EUR 15\,000 to EUR 39\,500 per project\, covering travel
 \, visas\, insurance\, accommodation\, per diems\, local transport and shi
 pping of scenographic and technical elements. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Projects carried out between February and December 2027\n\nMore: https:
 //ifprog.emundus.fr/en/campagne-info?view=programme&cid=353&utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://ifprog.emundus.fr/en/campagne-info?view=programme&cid=353&utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicants based in one of the 47 eligible sub-Saharan African cou
 ntries (Kenya included) or one of the 27 EU Member States. Touring in Afri
 ca and/or Europe.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:titanik-mcult-residing-non-linear-technologies-sonic-worldings-2027@art
 ificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Residing Non-linear Technologies and Sonic Worldings (M-C
 ult x Titanik\, Helsinki and Turku)
DESCRIPTION:A new pilot residency from Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult 
 in Helsinki and Arte artists' association / Titanik gallery in Turku\, sit
 ting at the intersection of art\, media and sonic practice\, funded by the
  Nordic-Baltic Mobility Programme for Culture from Nordic Culture Point. T
 WO ARTISTS will be selected. THE PREMISE: the programme treats technology 
 as a situated practice and deliberately shifts attention away from the pro
 gress narratives promoted by large global technology companies\, toward lo
 cal\, indigenous and community-oriented perspectives. It focuses on making
  vernacular and non-linear technological and sonic environments visible\, 
 foregrounding vibrant local histories and traditions across the Nordic and
  Baltic region\, and asks what non-linear sonic and media practice might l
 ook like in the current social\, political and cultural landscape. WHAT EA
 CH ARTIST RECEIVES: EUR 3\,500 artist fee\; EUR 2\,000 production budget\;
  accommodation for two months divided between Helsinki and Turku\, one mon
 th in each\; travel costs for one return ticket from a Nordic-Baltic count
 ry\; access to Titanik's residency studio space and a working desk at M-Cu
 lt's office during each stay\; mentoring and curatorial support from both 
 organisations' staff\; and networking into the Finnish media art and conte
 mporary art scenes. In return the artist develops in-depth or focused rese
 arch and presents their practice and research at two public events\, one i
 n each city. WHO CAN APPLY: individual artists only\, explicitly NOT colle
 ctives this round\; artists from or based in Finland\, Sweden\, Norway\, D
 enmark\, Iceland\, Aland\, the Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Estonia\, Latvi
 a or Lithuania\; working in sound\, media and/or digital arts\, or the exp
 anded field of vernacular technologies\, all defined broadly by the organi
 sers. Applicants must be available to travel to Finland for the full two-m
 onth period across February and March 2027. The organisers welcome applica
 nts at all career stages and from all backgrounds. One submission per arti
 st: if two are sent\, only the later one is reviewed. HOW TO APPLY: submit
  via the form linked from the open call page with a brief letter of motiva
 tion including a research plan for the residency\, how it connects with th
 e programme's themes and what you would focus on (max 1-2 pages)\, your CV
 \, and a link to your portfolio. Applications in English only. DEADLINE: 3
 0 August 2026 at 23:59 UTC+3. Online interviews with shortlisted artists t
 ake place before 19 September 2026\, and all applicants are notified by em
 ail by the end of September 2026. JURY: Jussi Koitela\, Director of M-Cult
 \, Helsinki\, and Farbod Fakharzadeh\, Director of Arte ry and Titanik\, T
 urku. ACCOMMODATION AND ACCESSIBILITY: in Helsinki\, the Vallila Residency
  hosted by M-Cult is a 30 sqm apartment in a small wooden house with one r
 oom\, kitchen and bathroom with shower\; it is NOT fully accessible\, sitt
 ing on the first floor up stairs with no elevator (details from info@m-cul
 t.org). In Turku\, the Titanik A.i.R. apartment is 27 sqm on the top floor
  of a building with an elevator\, but the space including the bathroom is 
 not fully accessible (details from info@titanik.fi). A partner\, friend or
  child may join\, but their expenses are not covered. THE PARTNERS: M-Cult
 \, founded in 2000\, develops and presents art and media culture with a fo
 cus on the social and political dimensions of media and technology\, worki
 ng through art commissions\, residencies and learning programmes. Titanik\
 , established in Turku in 1988 by the artist-run organization Arte Ry\, ha
 s since 2006 run Titanik A.i.R.\, the first artist residency in Finland wi
 th a focus on sound art and sonic practices. CONTACT: Jussi Koitela (jussi
 .koitela@m-cult.org) or Farbod Fakharzadeh (director@arte.fi).\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult (Helsinki) and Arte artists' ass
 ociation / Titanik gallery (Turku)\nLocation: Finland\; one month in Helsi
 nki and one month in Turku. Open to artists from or based in Nordic and Ba
 ltic countries.\nAward: EUR 3\,500 artist fee plus EUR 2\,000 production b
 udget\, two months' accommodation split between Helsinki and Turku\, and o
 ne return travel ticket from a Nordic-Baltic country. No application fee s
 tated.\nDuration: Two months\, February to March 2027 (one month in each c
 ity)\n\nMore: https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-line
 ar-technologies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-linear-technolo
 gies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
LOCATION:Finland\; one month in Helsinki and one month in Turku. Open to ar
 tists from or based in Nordic and Baltic countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:spazioserra-open-call-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Open Call 2026/2027 'Where is the tomorrow we saw yesterd
 ay?' (spazioSERRA)
DESCRIPTION:spazioSERRA turns ten in 2027\, and this open call is framed as
  a deliberate break from how independent art usually asks artists to arriv
 e: with deadlines met\, ideas already defined\, and a project ready to be 
 exhibited. The call asks 'where is the tomorrow we saw yesterday?' and sta
 tes plainly that it is not looking for completed projects or stabilised vi
 sions\, but for practices\, processes\, obsessions and still-open question
 s. Site-specific installations\, performances\, editorial productions\, vi
 deos\, urban interventions\, hybrid collaborations and forms that are stil
 l undefined are all welcome\; the point is not the format but the urgency 
 running through it\, and the willingness to enter a shared research proces
 s that can slow down\, change direction and stay incomplete long enough to
  transform. WHAT IT LEADS TO: the call identifies artists\, collectives an
 d research practices with whom spazioSERRA may develop collaborations duri
 ng the 2027 exhibition and project season\, which may take the form of sol
 o or group exhibitions\, site-specific installations\, performances\, edit
 orial productions\, video projects\, collaborations with institutions or e
 xternal organisations\, and further projects developed together. If select
 ed\, the artist or collective begins a curatorial dialogue with spazioSERR
 A aimed at developing an exhibition and/or additional projects\, jointly d
 efined during the collaboration. FUNDING: each selected project receives a
  financial contribution of EUR 500 to EUR 2\,000\, determined by the proje
 ct's characteristics and available resources\, covering production costs o
 f the work (which remains the property of the artist or collective)\, trav
 el\, food and accommodation\, and communication and promotion materials. T
 he contribution is paid at the end of the collaboration unless otherwise a
 greed\, and spazioSERRA reimburses only documented expenses paid by tracea
 ble methods with the relevant tax documentation. It is explicitly a contri
 bution towards the realisation of the project and/or the practice\, not ne
 cessarily full coverage of production costs\; if the project is developed 
 with external partners bringing additional resources\, the budget may be s
 upplemented by mutual agreement. Use of the exhibition space and the work 
 of the spazioSERRA team cost the artist nothing. SELECTION: no exhibition 
 project or defined proposal is required\; the jury assesses the artistic r
 esearch as a whole\, including the applicant's path\, the languages used\,
  the questions explored\, the potential for development and compatibility 
 with spazioSERRA's curatorial vision. The selecting curatorial collective 
 is Pietro Agostini\, Lidia Barca\, Tracy Bassil\, Virginia Dal Magro\, Mas
 similiano Fanto\, Camilla Gurgone\, Giulia Maffioli Brigatti\, Cristiano R
 izzo\, Victoria Tincati and Valentina Toccaceli. First selection in Novemb
 er 2026\, interviews November to December 2026\, final notification by Dec
 ember 2026. APPLICATION: online form open 15 July to 6 September 2026\, wi
 th a single PDF containing statement\, artistic CV and a selection of work
 s\, shared via a stable online link viewable without download\, authorisat
 ion or password\; collectives list all members. FEE: EUR 12 membership fee
  per person\, multiplied by the number of members for a collective. Contac
 t: info.spazioserra@gmail.com.\n\nOrganisation: spazioSERRA\nLocation: Mil
 an\, Italy. spazioSERRA operates inside the Milano Lancetti passante railw
 ay station\; registered office Via Maloia 1\, 20158 Milan. Open to artists
  and collectives of any nationality\, aged 18 or over.\nAward: EUR 500 to 
 EUR 2\,000 financial contribution per selected project\, set according to 
 the project's characteristics and available resources\, paid at the end of
  the collaboration against documented expenses with traceable payment meth
 ods. No cost to the artist for use of the exhibition space or for the work
  of the spazioSERRA team. Application fee: EUR 12 membership fee per perso
 n (multiplied by the number of members for collectives).\nDuration: Collab
 orations developed across the 2027 exhibition and project season\n\nMore: 
 https://www.spazioserra.org/en/exhibitions/open-call-2026-2027?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.spazioserra.org/en/exhibitions/open-call-2026-2027?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Milan\, Italy. spazioSERRA operates inside the Milano Lancetti pas
 sante railway station\; registered office Via Maloia 1\, 20158 Milan. Open
  to artists and collectives of any nationality\, aged 18 or over.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nordic-currents-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Nordic Currents 2027-2028 Open Call (Nordic Council of Mi
 nisters)
DESCRIPTION:Nordic Currents is a new international culture initiative from 
 the Nordic Council of Ministers\, running 2027-2028\, that invites cultura
 l organisations in European countries neighbouring the Nordic region to jo
 in forces with Nordic partners on projects that move audiences\, deepen re
 lationships and create lasting connections. Trust\, openness\, artistic fr
 eedom and democratic values are named as its core. The stated aims are to 
 showcase the richness and quality of contemporary Nordic culture and art i
 n neighbouring European countries and to build long-term collaborations be
 tween Nordic and European actors. WHO CAN APPLY: established organisations
  based in European countries neighbouring the Nordic region\, primarily Be
 lgium\, Estonia\, France\, Germany\, Ireland\, Lithuania\, Latvia\, the Ne
 therlands\, Poland and the United Kingdom. Eligible applicant types are cu
 ltural organisations\, institutions\, promoters\, festivals and producers\
 , as well as embassies and diplomatic missions working in cooperation with
  arts and culture stakeholders. Every application must have at least two p
 artners from different Nordic countries who stay active throughout the pro
 ject. WHERE: in a single country or as a cooperation across several countr
 ies\, but activities must primarily take place in the country where the ap
 plying organisation is based\; if several European partners outside the No
 rdic region are involved\, activities may also be organised in their count
 ries. Emphasis goes to projects in regions and countries with existing loc
 al interest in Nordic art and culture\, where Nordic and local partners ca
 n build long-term\, mutually beneficial collaborations. WHAT THE PROJECT M
 UST INCLUDE: both artistic programmes and events accessible to local audie
 nces\, and platforms for professional exchange and sector development. Pub
 lic cultural events can be festivals\, programmes\, series or other curate
 d formats\, standalone or attached to existing structures. Projects must a
 lso include network-building\, knowledge sharing and other forms of sector
  development. Programming must include representation from all the Nordic 
 countries\, with artists and cultural actors from Denmark\, the Faroe Isla
 nds\, Finland\, Greenland (Kalaallit Nunaat)\, Iceland\, Norway\, Sweden a
 nd Aland. Projects must take place between 1 June 2027 and 1 June 2028. FU
 NDING: applications may request EUR 100\,000 to EUR 800\,000\; the total p
 ool is EUR 800\,000\, so between one and eight proposals will be successfu
 l. Co-financing through local\, national or other funding sources is a pre
 requisite for eligibility. HOW TO APPLY: proposals are submitted in Englis
 h through an online portal open only between 1 October and 1 November 2026
 . ASSESSMENT: all eligible proposals go to a professional committee and ex
 pert reviewers in the corresponding art fields\, judged on artistic qualit
 y\, connection to the Nordic region\, relevance to the artistic fields cov
 ered\, how the concept incorporates democratic values such as openness\, t
 rust and artistic freedom\, capacity to implement the collaboration\, publ
 ic appeal for the intended audiences\, public accessibility\, potential fo
 r long-term impact and future collaboration\, and organisational and finan
 cial capacity. Selected projects are publicly announced in May 2027.\n\nOr
 ganisation: Nordic Council of Ministers\nLocation: Projects take place pri
 marily in the applicant organisation's home country\, in European countrie
 s neighbouring the Nordic region: primarily Belgium\, Estonia\, France\, G
 ermany\, Ireland\, Lithuania\, Latvia\, the Netherlands\, Poland and the U
 nited Kingdom.\nAward: EUR 100\,000 to EUR 800\,000 per project. The total
  pool for the initiative is EUR 800\,000\, so between one and eight propos
 als will be funded. Co-financing from local\, national or other sources is
  a prerequisite for eligibility. No application fee.\nDuration: Projects m
 ust take place between 1 June 2027 and 1 June 2028\n\nMore: https://www.no
 rden.org/en/nordiccurrents?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
URL:https://www.norden.org/en/nordiccurrents?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Projects take place primarily in the applicant organisation's home
  country\, in European countries neighbouring the Nordic region: primarily
  Belgium\, Estonia\, France\, Germany\, Ireland\, Lithuania\, Latvia\, the
  Netherlands\, Poland and the United Kingdom.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:foodculture-days-kitchen-central-location-2027@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Kitchen / Central Location Open Call (foodculture day
 s Biennale\, Vevey 2027)
DESCRIPTION:foodculture days is opening up The Kitchen / Central Location\,
  the living heart of its biennale in Vevey\, to an outside collective for 
 the festival's 6th edition. The Kitchen is at once an open-air kitchen\, a
  gathering space and an artistic installation\, sited by the lake on the E
 splanade de la Paix along a busy promenade\, hosting multidisciplinary pro
 jects\, concerts\, themed meals\, public discussions\, citizens' assemblie
 s and moments of collective cooking. The call asks for new ways of inhabit
 ing it. WHO THEY WANT: a transdisciplinary collective of scientists\, arti
 sts\, designers\, craftspeople\, cooks\, activists and architects\, either
  newly formed or already active\; collaboration across generations\, cultu
 res and levels of expertise is encouraged. WHAT IS OFFERED: an open-air ci
 tizen experimentation laboratory in Vevey's public space\, framed around r
 ethinking ways of living and the relationship to time and to creation\, an
 d thinking together about the conditions under which multidisciplinary pra
 ctices can flourish. THE BRIEF: kitchen and hospitality\, lunch and dinner
  service\, vegetarian meals with a vegan option. Meals should be affordabl
 e\, seasonal where fresh produce is involved\, and conceived in the spirit
  of a communal canteen: simple\, generous\, nourishing and full of flavour
 . Expected covers are roughly 100 guests Wednesday to Thursday and roughly
  150 Friday to Sunday. WHAT THE FESTIVAL PROVIDES: curatorial support to d
 evelop the proposal\, with particular attention to the themed meals\; intr
 oductions to local producers\, growers and artisans including farm visits 
 and foraging sites\; production support and volunteers throughout\; and st
 andard professional kitchen equipment and cooking facilities. BUDGET: CHF 
 15\,000 all-inclusive (VAT included) covering travel\, visas\, equipment r
 ental\, any additional kitchen equipment\, per diems\, materials and artis
 t fees\; food costs and accommodation in Vevey are covered separately. THE
  SPACE: the pavilion\, called Cuisine\, is a modular metal structure assem
 bled from repurposed agricultural greenhouse components on a 3.1 metre bid
 irectional grid\, with pillars and arches forming ridge vaults\, a textile
  covering naturally dyed yellow with reseda\, and a terracotta brick heart
 h for wood-fired cooking laid directly on the floor. It comes with dining 
 tables\, low tables\, a reception table\, a shelf\, a bar\, pendant lights
 \, 30 cushions\, removable curtains\, flags\, blackboards and signage pane
 ls\, and can expand\, contract or be reconfigured. TIMELINE: Phase 1 deadl
 ine 31 August 2026 at midnight CET\; three shortlisted proposals selected 
 in late September\, each invited to an online meeting and paid a CHF 250 r
 esearch fee for Phase 2\; final project selected and announced in early Oc
 tober 2026\; collaborative development with the foodculture days architect
 ural team October to November\; production December to February\; build an
 d installation in Vevey 1 to 16 June 2027\, with the collective present th
 roughout those two weeks\; 6th edition of the biennale 16 to 20 June 2027.
  PHASE 1 QUESTIONS: tell us about your collective (origins\, intention\, w
 hat makes you special)\; member biographies with collective or individual 
 portfolios\, website\, Instagram\, city of residence and practices\; what 
 the concept consists of in terms of food and cuisine\, relationships\, and
  scenography and spatial research\; what you want to put into practice in 
 the central pavilion\, your vision and the kind of experience you want to 
 create\; any special requirements\; and references\, including a quote rep
 resenting the collective\, two reference texts\, three images and optional
 ly video. An Open Call Information Kit\, a recording of the online informa
 tion session and a Q&A are published on the open call site and are worth r
 eading before applying.\n\nOrganisation: foodculture days\nLocation: Vevey
 \, Switzerland. The Kitchen is built on the Esplanade de la Paix by the la
 keside. The selected collective must be present in Vevey for the two-week 
 build and installation\, 1 to 16 June 2027\, and through the biennale 16 t
 o 20 June 2027.\nAward: CHF 15\,000 all-inclusive project budget (VAT incl
 uded)\, covering travel\, visas\, equipment and additional kitchen equipme
 nt rental\, per diems\, materials and artist fees. Food costs and accommod
 ation in Vevey are covered separately by foodculture days. Three shortlist
 ed collectives each receive a CHF 250 research fee to develop their propos
 al in Phase 2. One collective is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 October 2026 to June 2027: collaborative development October-November\, pr
 oduction December-February\, on-site build 1-16 June 2027\, biennale 16-20
  June 2027\n\nMore: https://opencall.foodculturedays.com/en?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://opencall.foodculturedays.com/en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Vevey\, Switzerland. The Kitchen is built on the Esplanade de la P
 aix by the lakeside. The selected collective must be present in Vevey for 
 the two-week build and installation\, 1 to 16 June 2027\, and through the 
 biennale 16 to 20 June 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:impart-nalli-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Impart & Nalli Fellowships 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:A research fellowship from Impart\, an online platform building
  accessible resources on South Asia's art histories\, in collaboration wit
 h Nalli Silks\, a heritage textile brand established in 1928. The programm
 e has supported two research projects a year since 2024-25\; for the 2027-
 28 cycle two fellowships are awarded\, each carrying a grant of up to INR 
 5.5 lakhs for one year of independent research and project development. AR
 EAS OF FOCUS: underexplored textile-related histories\, practices and trad
 itions across South Asia. Any textile-centric subject may be proposed\; th
 e call particularly encourages textiles in religious\, literary\, liturgic
 al and mythological contexts\; the environmental impact of textile product
 ion and consumption\; endangered or disappearing textile traditions\; crit
 ical perspectives on textile revivalism\, digitisation\, archives and mate
 rial afterlives\; labour in textile production with attention to caste\, c
 lass and gender\; relationships between textiles and other cultural forms 
 including storytelling\, poetry\, music\, architecture and material cultur
 e\; techniques\, technologies and tools tied to specific traditions\; Sout
 h Asian textiles within the contemporary global design economy\; intellect
 ual property\, ownership and cultural rights\; and socio-cultural perspect
 ives on the relationship between handmade and industrial textiles. ELIGIBI
 LITY: open to anyone aged 18 or over residing anywhere in the world\, prov
 ided the project engages with textile practices in the wider South Asia re
 gion (Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\,
  Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka\, Tibet). Early- and mid-career researchers\
 , designers\, archivists\, journalists\, writers\, educators\, practitione
 rs\, collectives and small organisations are all welcome\, and collaborati
 ve projects are permitted provided co-creators are credited and listed in 
 the application. Both new and ongoing projects are eligible\, but applican
 ts must specify which part of the project the grant will develop and discl
 ose any current or previous funding from other organisations. SUPPORTED FO
 RMATS: journalistic inquiry\, documentary film\, archive activation\, prac
 tice-based research\, archive generation\, digitisation and documentation 
 initiatives\, and exhibition or book projects. DELIVERABLES: a comprehensi
 ve project report at the end of the grant period\; a research dossier deve
 loped from that report in collaboration with Impart\, presenting key quest
 ions and findings and hosted on Impart's website for public benefit\; and 
 a set of final project outputs\, which may be audio-visual (interviews\, s
 hort films)\, visual essays (photographic or process-based documentation)\
 , written pieces (essays\, features\, interviews) or curatorial work (exhi
 bition or digital curation). Fellows also take part in periodic check-ins 
 and submit progress reports. Copyright is settled case by case\, but all f
 inal outputs must be made openly and freely accessible to the public. APPL
 ICATION: a Google Form structured as a full project proposal\, plus a proj
 ect budget (percentage-based allocation is acceptable\, but be as specific
  as possible)\, a project timeline for the one-year period with key phases
 \, activities and milestones\, and a single supporting PDF containing an u
 p-to-date CV\, selected work samples\, and up to two letters of support or
  recommendation. The form is in English\; applicants who would prefer anot
 her language are invited to write to Impart to arrange an alternative. NOT
 E: the fellowship has a strict no-tolerance policy on the use of AI or LLM
 s in the application form or any submitted materials\, and any violation r
 esults in rejection. KEY DATES: applications close 31 August 2026\; awarde
 es notified December 2026\; fellowships run January to December 2027. No i
 ndividual feedback is given on unsuccessful applications.\n\nOrganisation:
  Impart in collaboration with Nalli Silks\nLocation: Remote and self-direc
 ted\; applicants may reside anywhere in the world. Projects must engage wi
 th textile practices across South Asia: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\
 , India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
 \nAward: Up to INR 5.5 lakhs (INR 550\,000) per fellow towards project exe
 cution\, covering research-related expenses including travel\, fieldwork a
 nd documentation subject to approval. Two fellowships are awarded. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https
 ://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and self-directed\; applicants may reside anywhere in the w
 orld. Projects must engage with textile practices across South Asia: Afgha
 nistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pa
 kistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:teatri-riflessi-2027-short-performance-competition@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:Deadline: Teatri Riflessi 2027: Short Performance Competition (12th
  edition)
DESCRIPTION:An open call for at least 10 short performances of maximum 15 m
 inutes for the 12th edition of Teatri Riflessi in Zafferana Etnea\, Sicily
 . THE FORMAT: the competition champions the short performance in its own r
 ight\, not as a reduction of a longer work nor as a sketch for a full-even
 ing adaptation\; its strength is the artistic immediacy and urgency it car
 ries. The framing connects ancient Greek theatre\, conceived as an outdoor
  competition for a large audience\, to the more provocative tendencies of 
 happenings and performance art that lean on the beholder's desire to parti
 cipate. STRUCTURE: semi-finalists perform on the evenings of Thursday 15 a
 nd Friday 16 July 2027\; the Programmers' Panel and the Scholars' & Critic
 s' Panel determine the finalists\, who perform again in the final on Sunda
 y 18 July. The panels bring together around 30 Italian and international a
 rtistic directors\, curators\, festival and theatre programmers\, journali
 sts\, critics and performing arts scholars\, many from the Teatri Riflessi
  Network and Anticorpi XL Network\; the full list is published in June 202
 7. Past partner organisations include Antistatic Festival (Sofia)\, ATER F
 ondazione\, BIG Festival\, Cantieri Danza\, Circuito CLAPS\, COORPI\, Danc
 e ICONS (Washington DC)\, Danza Urbana\, Derida Dance Centre\, EN-KNAP and
  Spanski Borci (Ljubljana)\, Fabbrica Europa\, Festival Ammutinamenti\, Cl
 uny Danse (France)\, Danza Canarias dentro y fuera (Tenerife)\, FIND Festi
 val\, Fondazione Armunia and Festival Inequilibrio\, Fondazione Claudia Lo
 mbardi per il teatro (Lugano)\, Fondazione Sipario Toscana\, Fondazione To
 scana Spettacolo\, Galway Dance (Ireland)\, Interplay Festival (Turin)\, L
 'Arboreto Teatro Dimora\, La Piccionaia\, Movimento Danza\, New Dance For 
 Asia International Festival (Daegu)\, Puglia Culture\, Toplocentrala (Sofi
 a) and Umbria Danza Festival. MONEY: each selected performance receives a 
 gross fee of EUR 500 to EUR 1\,100 depending on cast size\, plus a possibl
 e travel contribution of up to EUR 250. Awards are Best Performance EUR 1\
 ,200 (from the combined weighted scores of both panels\, the Jury of the F
 inal\, the Youth Jury and the Citizens' Jury)\, Best Dramaturgy EUR 500 an
 d the Scholars' and Critics' Award EUR 500 (both from the Scholars' & Crit
 ics' Panel)\, Best Direction EUR 500 and Best Performer EUR 500 (both from
  the Programmers' Panel)\, the 'Valentina Nicosia' Fund of EUR 500 (joint 
 decision of both panels)\, the 'Sandra Bressan' research fund of EUR 1\,80
 0 (awarded by the Citizens' Jury to reinvite a company to Sicily to work w
 ith the community)\, and the Youth Jury Award of EUR 300 to a semi-finalis
 t\, alongside the Audience Special Mention\, the Companies' Choice Award a
 nd the Technical Crew Award. Fees\, reimbursements and prizes are paid aft
 er the festival\, provided all regulations and deadlines are met. FEES TO 
 APPLY: none. Only selected companies pay a EUR 50 deposit on selection\, f
 ully refunded after the festival. RULES: performances should aim for 15 mi
 nutes\, with total stage occupancy including set-up and dismantling capped
  at 20 minutes. Multiple entries are allowed but need a separate form each
 . No major changes are permitted after selection\; minor adjustments\, esp
 ecially cast substitutions\, must be communicated in advance and authorise
 d by IterCulture APS. Works in languages other than Italian are welcome an
 d are presented with Italian and English surtitles prepared by IterCulture
  APS. APPLICATION: a digital form linked in Article 5 of the open call reg
 ulations\, with Annex A for dramaturgy (the complete script where one exis
 ts\, or for dance\, physical theatre and non-text work a short document on
  the dramaturgical research and choreographic structure) and a full video 
 recording of the complete work\; excerpts and teasers are not accepted\, a
 nd for rehearsed readings\, directorial notes on costumes\, lighting and s
 et design are mandatory. COMMITMENT: rehearsal attendance is compulsory\, 
 with the day assigned by the Competition Manager after selection\; compani
 es must arrive in Zafferana Etnea by their rehearsal day (rehearsals run 1
 3 and 14 July 2027) and stay until the festival closes at 23:30 on Sunday 
 18 July. Rehearsals and public events run mainly in afternoons and evening
 s\; the only compulsory morning commitment is the panel feedback session o
 n Sunday 18 July. Online information sessions are held on Google Meet on T
 uesday 22 September 2026\, with the English session at 19:30 CEST. Deadlin
 e 15 October 2026 at 5pm CET.\n\nOrganisation: IterCulture APS\nLocation: 
 Zafferana Etnea\, Sicily\, Italy. Open to Italian and international compan
 ies and artists. Semi-finals 15 and 16 July 2027\, final 18 July 2027\; re
 hearsals 13 and 14 July.\nAward: Gross performance fee of EUR 500 to EUR 1
 \,100 (VAT included) per selected short performance\, scaled by the number
  of performers on stage\, plus a possible travel contribution of up to EUR
  250 subject to available funding\, place of origin\, cast size and absenc
 e of external touring grants. At least 10 performances are selected. Compe
 tition prizes: Best Performance EUR 1\,200\; Best Dramaturgy\, Best Direct
 ion\, Best Performer and the Scholars' and Critics' Award EUR 500 each\; '
 Valentina Nicosia' Fund EUR 500\; 'Sandra Bressan' research fund EUR 1\,80
 0\; Youth Jury Award EUR 300\; plus non-cash awards (Audience Special Ment
 ion\, Companies' Choice\, Technical Crew). Panel members and Teatri Rifles
 si Network partners may also award special prizes in the form of artist re
 sidencies and touring opportunities. Application is free\; selected compan
 ies pay a EUR 50 deposit that is fully refunded after the festival.\nDurat
 ion: Festival participation 13 to 18 July 2027\, with mandatory rehearsals
  and presence until 23:30 on 18 July\n\nMore: https://www.iterculture.eu/e
 n/teatri-riflessi/tr2027/tr12/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.iterculture.eu/en/teatri-riflessi/tr2027/tr12/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Zafferana Etnea\, Sicily\, Italy. Open to Italian and internationa
 l companies and artists. Semi-finals 15 and 16 July 2027\, final 18 July 2
 027\; rehearsals 13 and 14 July.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dp-curator-support-open-call-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: 2027 d/p Curator Support Open Call (Seoul)
DESCRIPTION:d/p is an independent alternative exhibition space in the Nakwo
 n Arcade\, the historic mixed-use commercial and residential complex in Jo
 ngno-gu\, Seoul built in 1968. Its Curator Support programme\, sponsored b
 y NakwonSangga\, has selected two to three independent curators every year
  since 2018\, giving each an exhibition venue\, curatorial collaboration w
 ith the d/p team\, and a modest curatorial grant to develop and realise th
 eir curatorial practice. ELIGIBILITY: individual curators or teams with at
  least one curating experience within the past three years. The applying c
 urator or team cannot participate in the exhibition as an artist\; curator
 s and artists must be clearly distinguished. SELECTION: approximately two 
 individuals or teams. WHAT IS PROVIDED: a curatorial grant of KRW 4\,000\,
 000 including tax\; use of the d/p exhibition space of approximately 187 s
 quare metres\; and a five-week exhibition slot somewhere between March and
  December 2027\, with installation and deinstallation time counted separat
 ely from the five weeks. PROCESS: first round is a document review of the 
 APPLICATION and PROPOSAL submitted in the provided format\; second round i
 s individual interviews followed by final selection. Interviews are conduc
 ted offline in principle\, but may be held online if the applicant is abro
 ad or in other exceptional circumstances. HOW TO APPLY: submit the APPLICA
 TION via the Google Form and the PROPOSAL by email to dslashp@gmail.com be
 tween 3 August and 30 September 2026. The proposal template is downloadabl
 e from the link in the @dslashp Instagram bio. File name format: '2027 d/p
  Curator Support Application_Applicant Name'. Submit as PDF\; if the propo
 sal includes video\, provide a link rather than a file. KEY DATES: submiss
 ions 3 August to 30 September 2026\; first round results announced in the 
 first week of October 2026 by individual contact\; second round interviews
  in the second week of October 2026\; final selection announced in the thi
 rd week of October 2026 by individual contact and on the @dslashp account.
  Inquiries: Minji Lee\, Curator\, dslashp@gmail.com.\n\nOrganisation: d/p\
 , sponsored by NakwonSangga\nLocation: d/p\, 417 Nakwon Arcade\, 428 Samil
 -daero\, Jongno-gu\, Seoul\, South Korea. Interviews are held offline in p
 rinciple\, with online interviews possible for applicants abroad or in oth
 er exceptional circumstances.\nAward: Curatorial grant of KRW 4\,000\,000 
 (including tax) per selected curator or team\, plus use of d/p's approxima
 tely 187 square metre exhibition space for a five-week exhibition and cura
 torial collaboration with the d/p team. Approximately two curators or team
 s are selected. No application fee.\nDuration: Five-week exhibition slot b
 etween March and December 2027\, with installation and deinstallation peri
 ods separate\n\nMore: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1iSjEuCjay7DhL1AF1NC
 ePu-5RV3t_10WT0CNcjNNJy8/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1iSjEuCjay7DhL1AF1NCePu-5RV3t_10WT0CNcj
 NNJy8/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:d/p\, 417 Nakwon Arcade\, 428 Samil-daero\, Jongno-gu\, Seoul\, So
 uth Korea. Interviews are held offline in principle\, with online intervie
 ws possible for applicants abroad or in other exceptional circumstances.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:abbey-foundation-day-use-studio-grant-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: Abbey Foundation for the Arts: Day Use Studio Grant (Jan 
 to Jun 2027)
DESCRIPTION:The Abbey Foundation for the Arts in Boonville\, California awa
 rds six-month day-use studio residencies paired with a materials stipend o
 f up to USD 5\,000 for equipment and supplies needed to realise the propos
 ed project. The studio is 850 square feet indoors with an additional 850 s
 quare foot screened outdoor area for large-scale work\; note that it may b
 e shared with one other artist during some periods. ELIGIBILITY: open to a
 rtists across all mediums. IMPORTANT: this is a day-use studio grant\, not
  a live-in residency\, and the foundation provides no support for housing\
 , transportation\, meals or travel\, so it states the grant is most approp
 riate for artists already living in the local counties. CONDITIONS: at the
  end of the residency the artist is expected to contribute one of the piec
 es made while in residence to the foundation\, and the programme may come 
 to require grant recipients to take part in community workshops as it deve
 lops. DEADLINES: 1 December 2026 for the 1 January to 30 June 2027 residen
 cy\; the foundation runs the call twice a year\, with a 1 June deadline fo
 r the following July to December period. No application fee.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Abbey Foundation for the Arts\nLocation: Boonville\, California\, Unit
 ed States (Anderson Valley\, Mendocino County). Day use of the studio only
 : there is NO support for housing\, transportation\, meals or travel\, so 
 the foundation says it suits artists already living in the surrounding cou
 nties.\nAward: Stipend of up to USD 5\,000 for the purchase of materials a
 nd additional equipment needed to complete the proposed project\, plus six
  months of day use of an 850 square foot studio and a further 850 square f
 oot screened outdoor area for large-scale work. No housing\, travel\, meal
 s or transport support. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-month residency
 \, 1 January to 30 June 2027\n\nMore: https://www.abbeyfoundationfortheart
 s.org/grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.abbeyfoundationforthearts.org/grants?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Boonville\, California\, United States (Anderson Valley\, Mendocin
 o County). Day use of the studio only: there is NO support for housing\, t
 ransportation\, meals or travel\, so the foundation says it suits artists 
 already living in the surrounding counties.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gasworks-residency-east-africa-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Gasworks Residency 2027: Artists Based in East Africa
DESCRIPTION:Gasworks residency open call for an early-career contemporary v
 isual artist based in East Africa. The eleven-week\, fully funded residenc
 y runs at Gasworks in London from 7 April to 22 June 2027. Gasworks' resid
 ency programme supports professional development\, cultural exchange and a
 rtistic experimentation\, hosting up to twenty residencies a year\; reside
 ncies are self-led\, non-prescriptive and process-based\, with artists exp
 ected to lead on the research and production of their own work while Gaswo
 rks provides administrative\, pastoral and curatorial support during offic
 e hours. The residency includes studio visits with Gasworks staff\, extern
 al curators\, artists and educational groups\, at least two individual stu
 dio visits with other artists\, curators and arts professionals\, networki
 ng opportunities\, a dedicated residency page on the Gasworks website with
  a short video interview filmed and edited by Gasworks\, and an Open Studi
 o event and presentation of the work and research developed during the res
 idency on 12 June 2027. ELIGIBILITY: early-career contemporary visual arti
 sts based in Burundi\, the Chagos Archipelago\, Comoros\, Djibouti\, Ethio
 pia\, Eritrea\, the French Southern Territories\, Kenya\, Madagascar\, Mal
 awi\, Mauritius\, Mayotte\, Mozambique\, Reunion\, Rwanda\, Seychelles\, S
 omalia\, South Sudan\, Uganda\, the United Republic of Tanzania or Zambia 
 only. Applicants should have at least a moderate level of spoken English a
 nd feel comfortable discussing their work in English. The residency is off
 ered subject to approval of the visa application. Deadline 28 September 20
 26\, 12:00 UTC. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Gasworks\nLocation: Gasworks\, Lo
 ndon\, United Kingdom. Applicants must be based in East Africa: Burundi\, 
 Chagos Archipelago\, Comoros\, Djibouti\, Ethiopia\, Eritrea\, French Sout
 hern Territories\, Kenya\, Madagascar\, Malawi\, Mauritius\, Mayotte\, Moz
 ambique\, Reunion\, Rwanda\, Seychelles\, Somalia\, South Sudan\, Uganda\,
  United Republic of Tanzania or Zambia.\nAward: Fully funded eleven-week r
 esidency: 24/7 access to a fully accessible private studio at Gasworks\; s
 ingle-room accommodation in Gasworks' Residency House (shared two-storey h
 ouse with three communal bathrooms and a kitchen)\; economy return flights
  and taxi transfers from the artist's home country to London (one cabin an
 d one hold bag)\; all visa costs and visa-application support\; weekly sti
 pend of GBP 175 for living expenses\; materials budget of up to GBP 800\; 
 prepaid TFL Zones 1+2 travel card for unlimited central London travel thro
 ughout the residency\; access to Gasworks' basic tools and AV equipment su
 bject to availability. No application fee.\nDuration: Eleven weeks\, 7 Apr
 il to 22 June 2027\n\nMore: https://www.gasworks.org.uk/opportunities/resi
 dency-open-call-for-artists-based-in-east-africa-2027?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gasworks.org.uk/opportunities/residency-open-call-for-artis
 ts-based-in-east-africa-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Gasworks\, London\, United Kingdom. Applicants must be based in Ea
 st Africa: Burundi\, Chagos Archipelago\, Comoros\, Djibouti\, Ethiopia\, 
 Eritrea\, French Southern Territories\, Kenya\, Madagascar\, Malawi\, Maur
 itius\, Mayotte\, Mozambique\, Reunion\, Rwanda\, Seychelles\, Somalia\, S
 outh Sudan\, Uganda\, United Republic of Tanzania or Zambia.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mpi-empirical-aesthetics-inhabit-16-17-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: INHABIT #16 and #17: Artist-in-Residence 2027 (Max Planck
  Institute for Empirical Aesthetics)
DESCRIPTION:INHABIT is the artist-in-residence programme of the Max Planck 
 Institute for Empirical Aesthetics in Frankfurt am Main\, which invites ar
 tists from various disciplines to collaborate with its scientists and rese
 archers\, treating artistic thinking and knowledge as reflective practices
  in their own right. The 2027 call is addressed to artists from the perfor
 ming arts and music\, who spend four months creating new work\, or develop
 ing an existing project\, inside a scientific research environment and in 
 dialogue with the institute's researchers. Two slots are open for 2027: IN
 HABIT #16 from April to July 2027 and INHABIT #17 from September to Decemb
 er 2027. Work developed during the residency is presented in cooperation w
 ith local arts institutions as an exhibition\, performance\, concert or ot
 her format\, complemented by discursive formats. WHO THEY ARE LOOKING FOR:
  artists from the performing arts and music interested in interdisciplinar
 y collaboration between artistic and scientific fields\; artists open to d
 eveloping and sharing a project as an exhibition\, performance\, concert o
 r another format\; and artists active in contemporary culture who respond 
 to the institute with innovative and challenging ideas. WHAT IS OFFERED: a
 n artist's fee of EUR 8\,000\, an accommodation fee of EUR 2\,000\, a proj
 ect-dependent production budget\, travel costs to and in Frankfurt\, reali
 sation of the final project\, collaborations with scientists\, access to t
 he library\, sound studio and scientific facilities\, and curatorial suppo
 rt during the residency. APPLY through the institute's e-recruiting portal
  by 1 September 2026\, 23:59 CET\; the jury decision follows in November 2
 026. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Max Planck Institute for Empirical Aesthetic
 s\nLocation: Frankfurt am Main\, Germany\nAward: Artist's fee of EUR 8\,00
 0 plus an accommodation fee of EUR 2\,000\, a production budget depending 
 on the project\, and travel costs to and within Frankfurt. Also included: 
 realisation of the final project\, collaborations with scientists\, access
  to the institute's library\, sound studio and scientific facilities\, and
  curatorial support throughout the residency. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Four months. Two slots: INHABIT #16 runs April to July 2027\, INHABIT #
 17 runs September to December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.aesthetics.mpg.de/
 en/research/inhabit-artist-in-residence.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.aesthetics.mpg.de/en/research/inhabit-artist-in-residence.h
 tml?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Frankfurt am Main\, Germany
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:platform-dali-residencies-2027-what-is-real@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Platform Dali Open Call for Residencies 2027: What is rea
 l?
DESCRIPTION:Platform Dali launches its first annual Open Call for Residenci
 es as part of its Research and Creation Programme\, with the inaugural 202
 7 edition built around the question 'What is real?'. Artists of any nation
 ality and any discipline are invited to propose a research and creation re
 sidency project developed through close engagement with one of Platform Da
 li's partner scientific institutions: the Barcelona Supercomputing Center 
 (BSC-CNS)\, ICFO (the Institute of Photonic Sciences)\, the Institute of M
 arine Sciences (ICM-CSIC)\, the Institute for High Energy Physics (IFAE)\,
  and the ecosystem of the Barcelona Biomedical Research Park (PRBB)\, incl
 uding the Centre for Genomic Regulation (CRG) and EMBL Barcelona. Five art
 ists are selected\, each hosted by one of the partner institutions. WHAT T
 HE PROGRAMME EXPECTS: research is treated as a creative practice in its ow
 n right\, and residents are explicitly not expected to arrive with a fully
  defined artwork. The residency is designed to support an initial phase of
  open-ended enquiry inside a scientific research environment\, letting que
 stions\, methods and ideas emerge through dialogue and experimentation\; t
 hat research phase then informs the development and production of a new ar
 twork\, so research and creation run as sequential\, interconnected stages
 . TIMELINE: open call launched 2 July 2026\; applications close 7 Septembe
 r 2026 at 23:59 CET\; selected artists notified by November 2026\; residen
 ts publicly announced 2 December 2026. Two residency periods are available
 \, February to April 2027 and September to November 2027\, with final date
 s agreed between Platform Dali\, the host scientific institution and each 
 resident. Full application criteria\, residency structure and terms and co
 nditions are on the open call page.\n\nOrganisation: Platform Dali\nLocati
 on: Barcelona\, Spain\, hosted at one of Platform Dali's partner scientifi
 c institutions. Open to artists of any nationality.\nAward: Artist fee of 
 EUR 7\,500 per selected artist\, plus access to a production budget of up 
 to EUR 25\,000 for the development of a new artwork. Five artists are sele
 cted. For artists based outside Barcelona\, Platform Dali covers travel\, 
 accommodation\, subsistence and travel insurance for both the introductory
  visit and the on-site residency\, within a predefined budget. Also provid
 ed: curatorial support from Platform Dali and the partner institution's te
 am throughout the residency\, and access to the host institution's facilit
 ies\, expertise and research communities\, subject to that institution's r
 egulations and operational requirements. For collectives\, the artist fee\
 , production budget and covered expenses are shared among members\, and a 
 maximum of two members may take part in the introductory visit and on-site
  residency.\nDuration: One of two residency periods: February to April 202
 7\, or September to November 2027. Final dates are agreed between Platform
  Dali\, the host institution and the resident.\n\nMore: https://platformda
 li.org/en/call/open-call-for-residencies-2027-what-is-real/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://platformdali.org/en/call/open-call-for-residencies-2027-what-is
 -real/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Barcelona\, Spain\, hosted at one of Platform Dali's partner scien
 tific institutions. Open to artists of any nationality.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tartu-city-of-literature-residency-autumn-2026@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tartu UNESCO City of Literature Residency for Writers and
  Translators\, Autumn 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Estonian Literary Society\, which coordinates Tartu UNESCO 
 City of Literature\, runs an international residency programme for writers
  and translators that has hosted authors from Slovenia\, Ireland\, Hungary
 \, the USA\, the UK\, Ukraine and Canada since 2017. This is the open call
  for the autumn 2026 slot: one resident stays two months in the Karl Risti
 kivi residency apartment in Tartu\, working on their own writing or transl
 ation while connecting with the local literary scene. WHAT THE RESIDENCY O
 FFERS: a EUR 600 monthly stipend\, travel reimbursement up to EUR 350\, fr
 ee accommodation\, a local support coordinator to help arrange cultural en
 gagements\, and opportunities to perform and present at literary events. T
 he autumn period covers the interdisciplinary literary festival Crazy Tart
 u on 12 to 14 November\, and the resident is offered a slot to perform the
 re alongside writers\, poets\, musicians and artists from Estonia and abro
 ad. ELIGIBILITY: open to writers\, poets and translators of Estonian liter
 ature of any nationality\; applicants need an upper-intermediate level of 
 spoken English\, at least one published book\, screenplay\, theatre script
  or translation from Estonian into another language\, a demonstrable inter
 est in Estonian culture and literature\, a willingness to take part in loc
 al literary activities\, and a commitment to working on their own literary
  project during the stay. HOW TO APPLY: complete the online application fo
 rm linked from the residency page and upload a fragment of up to five page
 s of a published text (in Estonian or English) or of a published translati
 on. SELECTION: made by a committee representing the Estonian Literary Soci
 ety\, the Estonian Writers Union\, the Estonian Literary Museum and the Ta
 rtu Literature House Foundation. Results are announced before 15 September
  2026. The programme's stated aims are to enhance international exchange\,
  diversify the local literary scene\, introduce new foreign authors and th
 eir work to Estonian readers\, and build lasting connections beyond the re
 sidency period. Questions: Marja Unt\, eks@kirjandus.ee. Deadline 1 Septem
 ber 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Estonian Literary Society (Eesti Kirjanduse Sel
 ts)\, Focal Point for Tartu UNESCO City of Literature\nLocation: Tartu\, E
 stonia. Open to writers\, poets and translators of Estonian literature wor
 ldwide.\nAward: Monthly stipend of EUR 600 for the two-month stay (EUR 1\,
 200 in total)\, plus reimbursement of travel costs up to EUR 350. Free acc
 ommodation is provided in the Karl Ristikivi residency apartment\, which h
 as a bedroom\, living room\, kitchen\, bathroom and exhibition room. One r
 esident is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: Two months\, 15 Octobe
 r to 15 December 2026\, with some flexibility on exact dates\n\nMore: http
 s://tartu.kirjandus.ee/residency-autumn-2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://tartu.kirjandus.ee/residency-autumn-2026?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Tartu\, Estonia. Open to writers\, poets and translators of Estoni
 an literature worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:this-is-gender-regional-curatorial-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260922
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260923
SUMMARY:Deadline: This is Gender Regional Curatorial Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:This is Gender\, the photography and visual culture platform ru
 n by Global 50/50\, is launching its inaugural Regional Curatorial Fellows
  Programme for early-career curators and cultural practitioners working ac
 ross photography\, visual culture and gender justice. The premise: images 
 shape how gender\, power and social realities are understood\, feeding pub
 lic debate\, cultural memory\, research\, advocacy and policy\, yet the co
 mmunities and visual practices that should inform those narratives stay un
 derrepresented in the institutions that circulate them. The programme's ar
 gument is that diversifying who makes images is not enough\, and that it a
 lso matters who selects\, contextualises\, writes about\, commissions and 
 circulates them. For the first time\, This is Gender is embedding regional
  curators directly inside its global photography competition\, which has r
 un six cycles and receives thousands of submissions a year. WHAT THE FELLO
 WSHIP INVOLVES: working inside the competition and seeing first-hand how a
  major platform discovers\, reviews and champions new work\; shaping curat
 orial decisions by bringing regional knowledge and cultural insight into t
 he selection and interpretation of work\; contributing to public-facing pr
 ojects including exhibitions\, editorial outputs and digital storytelling 
 while developing your own writing\, framing and curatorial voice\; direct 
 engagement with international judges\, curators\, artists\, editors and cu
 ltural practitioners\; and building a network across an international coho
 rt of peers. WHO SHOULD APPLY: curators\, critics\, writers\, researchers\
 , artistic directors\, photographers\, organisers and cultural practitione
 rs from global majority countries and historically underrepresented commun
 ities. Early-career practitioners and recent graduates are strongly encour
 aged\, and a formal curatorial title is not required. HOW TO APPLY: read t
 he full role description and FAQs on the fellowship page and submit the on
 line application form\; the organisers offer the application in alternativ
 e formats and are open to a conversation before applying. Applications clo
 se 22 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: This is Gender (Global 50/50)\nLoca
 tion: Remote and part-time\, working within a global photography competiti
 on. Applications are invited from practitioners in global majority countri
 es and historically underrepresented communities\, including Asia\, Africa
 \, Latin America\, the Middle East\, Oceania\, and diasporic communities w
 orldwide.\nAward: Stipend of up to GBP 3\,000 for the seven-month part-tim
 e fellowship. No application fee.\nDuration: Seven months from October 202
 6\, part-time and flexible\n\nMore: https://thisisgender.global5050.org/re
 gional-curatorial-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://thisisgender.global5050.org/regional-curatorial-fellowship/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and part-time\, working within a global photography competi
 tion. Applications are invited from practitioners in global majority count
 ries and historically underrepresented communities\, including Asia\, Afri
 ca\, Latin America\, the Middle East\, Oceania\, and diasporic communities
  worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bow-seat-true-blue-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: True Blue Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:Bow Seat's True Blue Fellowship funds youth-led projects that u
 se the creative arts as their primary method for building awareness or dri
 ving action on ocean\, waterways and climate change issues\, including env
 ironmental justice. Twelve fellows are selected each year and receive up t
 o USD 2\,500 in project funding alongside mentorship\, capacity-building p
 rogramming\, and access to a peer community that includes the Future Blue 
 Youth Council and Bow Seat staff. ELIGIBILITY: youth worldwide aged 16 to 
 24 at the start of the fellowship period in January. Individuals\, groups 
 and youth-led organisations may apply\, with one designated project leader
  for a group. Projects must be new or emerging\, meaning less than two yea
 rs old\, and should span at least one year running January to December. Ap
 plications must be submitted in English\, though translation tools are acc
 eptable\, and applicants should be comfortable with spoken English for pee
 r collaboration. NOTE: this is a youth programme with an upper age limit o
 f 24\, and the creative-arts requirement is central rather than incidental
 \, so proposals built on other methods are out of scope. Deadline 30 Augus
 t 2026 at 11:59pm ET.\n\nOrganisation: Bow Seat Ocean Awareness Programs\n
 Location: Remote\, open to youth worldwide.\nAward: Up to USD 2\,500 in pr
 oject funding per fellow\, plus mentorship and capacity-building programmi
 ng. Twelve youth are selected annually. No application fee.\nDuration: One
  year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https://bowseat.org/programs/tru
 e-blue-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://bowseat.org/programs/true-blue-fellowship/?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to youth worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:art-worker-artist-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:Deadline: Art Worker Artist Grant 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Art Worker Artist Grant is a biennial award for artists who
 se livelihoods are connected to the production\, handling\, exhibition\, c
 are\, movement or stewardship of art. Its premise is that a great many art
 ists fund their practice through labour inside the art industry itself\, a
 nd that this work rarely comes with support for their own artistic develop
 ment. WHO IS ELIGIBLE: artists who support their practice by providing any
  form of labour in any area of the art industry\, including but not limite
 d to museums\, galleries\, artist studios\, collections\, auction houses\,
  art fairs\, fabrication\, framing and mountmaking workshops\, conservatio
 n labs\, art logistics and crating companies\, and storage facilities. WHA
 T IT FUNDS: the development of artistic work in whatever form that takes\,
  with the money explicitly unrestricted\; the call lists studio time\, tra
 vel\, healthcare\, research\, equipment\, rest\, materials\, living expens
 es and childcare as legitimate uses. There are no required outcomes and no
  required acquittal\, so no reporting obligation. APPLICATION: intentional
 ly minimal\, consisting of a brief statement describing how the funds coul
 d support artistic development or production\, and a link to a website\, p
 ortfolio\, social media or other documentation of the work. SELECTION: by 
 an independent jury of Andria Hickey (curator and museum leader\, curatori
 al advisor for the Barclays Center art programme in Brooklyn\, previously 
 at The Shed\, Pace Gallery and MOCA Cleveland)\, Clynton Lowry (artist and
  publisher) and Paul Graham (photographer). IMPORTANT: applications for th
 e 2026 edition are capped at the first 500 submissions\, so the call may c
 lose before the stated deadline. Key dates in AEST: entries open 24 August
  2026\, entries close 14 September 2026\, recipient announced 2 November 2
 026. Overseen and funded by ARTIST ENGINEERING.\n\nOrganisation: ARTIST EN
 GINEERING\nLocation: Remote. No geographic restriction is stated in the ca
 ll\; key dates are given in AEST.\nAward: AUD 5\,000 to one recipient for 
 the 2026 edition. Funds are unrestricted: they may be used for studio time
 \, travel\, healthcare\, research\, equipment\, rest\, materials\, living 
 expenses\, childcare\, or something else entirely. There are no required o
 utcomes and no required grant acquittal. No application fee is mentioned.\
 nDuration: One-off unrestricted grant\, awarded biennially\n\nMore: https:
 //artistengineering.co/pages/awa?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://artistengineering.co/pages/awa?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. No geographic restriction is stated in the call\; key date
 s are given in AEST.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:toronto-arts-council-artists-in-the-library-2027@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261006
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261007
SUMMARY:Deadline: Artists in the Library 2027 (Toronto Arts Council)
DESCRIPTION:Created in 2014 as a partnership between Toronto Arts Council a
 nd Toronto Public Library\, Artists in the Library gives Toronto artists p
 aid opportunities and space while widening access to the arts in Etobicoke
 \, York\, North York\, East York and Scarborough. It funds professional ar
 tists\, arts organisations and collectives in any artistic discipline to c
 reate and deliver free community-engaged arts programming at a Toronto Pub
 lic Library branch. TAC encourages applications from its equity priority g
 roups and from artists working in their local communities. WHAT RECIPIENTS
  GET OUT OF IT: a chance to explore their practice in a community setting\
 , access to branch space and facilities\, meaningful engagement with the b
 ranch and its community\, close support from the branch team\, experience 
 navigating a multi-stakeholder environment\, and the delivery of a communi
 ty-engaged arts project from start to finish. WHAT COUNTS: community engag
 ement is required but its format can vary\, covering exhibitions\, install
 ations\, performances\, concerts\, screenings\, readings\, filmmaking\, st
 orytelling\, workshops\, artist talks\, participatory programmes\, arts ed
 ucation and mentorship. Permanent exhibitions and installations are not el
 igible. Projects are produced by the recipient\, hosted and supported by t
 he assigned branch\, and must be delivered on site within branch operating
  hours. SCALE: because host slots at TPL branches are limited\, this is no
 t for one-off workshops or small-scale events\; the offering must be subst
 antial with a budget to match. LOCATION: there is no list of eligible bran
 ches for 2026. Applicants indicate a preferred area or neighbourhood outsi
 de the downtown core and TPL staff assign the branch based on suitability 
 and availability\, so a different branch than the one requested may be ass
 igned. For Toronto and East York\, downtown is excluded\, defined as south
  of St Clair\, east of Jane and west of the Don Valley. Only one branch ma
 y be proposed. ELIGIBILITY: individuals must be Canadian citizens\, perman
 ent residents\, have a pending PR application\, or be a protected person\,
  must hold a Social Insurance Number\, and must have lived in the City of 
 Toronto for at least one year before the deadline and live and work there 
 at least eight months a year (no PO boxes). Artists in graduate study may 
 not apply for work related to their programme without a letter from the pr
 ogramme director. Organisations must be incorporated non-profit arts organ
 isations\, and collectives must operate on a non-profit basis\, be located
  in Toronto\, and have both members (for a pair) or a majority of members 
 resident in Toronto\; successful collectives must supply bank account deta
 ils in the collective's name. IMPORTANT: proposals must align with TPL's p
 rogramming policy\, and proposals that TPL staff judge unsuitable for a br
 anch or impossible in its context will not be funded\, so read the 'Workin
 g With Toronto Public Library' section of the guidelines. First-time appli
 cants are encouraged to consult the programme manager and to attend the Zo
 om office hours on eligibility and project fit (17 September) and on budge
 ts (29 September). Contact: Lena Golubtsova\, lena@torontoartscouncil.org.
  Deadline 6 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Toronto Arts Council\, in partn
 ership with Toronto Public Library\nLocation: Toronto\, Canada. Programmin
 g takes place at a Toronto Public Library branch outside the downtown core
 \, in Etobicoke York\, North York\, Toronto and East York\, or Scarborough
 . Applicants must be City of Toronto residents.\nAward: Maximum grant of C
 AD 20\,000\, which may cover up to 100 percent of project costs. Payment o
 f professional artist fees is a requirement of the programme. Recipients a
 lso get access to space and facilities at the assigned library branch for 
 arts programming and presentation\, and support from the branch team. Fund
 ed by the City of Toronto. No application fee is mentioned.\nDuration: Pro
 gramming must take place between September and December 2027\n\nMore: http
 s://torontoartscouncil.org/grants/artists-in-the-library/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://torontoartscouncil.org/grants/artists-in-the-library/?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Toronto\, Canada. Programming takes place at a Toronto Public Libr
 ary branch outside the downtown core\, in Etobicoke York\, North York\, To
 ronto and East York\, or Scarborough. Applicants must be City of Toronto r
 esidents.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mad-artist-studios-residency-cycle-46-47@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: MAD Artist Studios Residency\, Cycles 46 and 47
DESCRIPTION:Established in 2008 and the first of its kind\, the Artist Stud
 ios at MAD is a public-facing community engagement and professional develo
 pment programme on the sixth floor of the Museum\, where visitors meet art
 ists-in-residence at work. Two artists are selected per six-month session 
 (the application text elsewhere says three\, so confirm with MAD). More th
 an 200 artists and designers have come through the programme\, which MAD d
 escribes as an innovative model for museum education\, with a sustained fo
 cus on artists from historically underrepresented communities whose work p
 ushes the boundaries of contemporary craft\; alumni have exhibited at the 
 Queens Museum\, Whitney\, Brooklyn Museum and Studio Museum. WHAT MAD MEAN
 S BY CRAFT: both a discipline and a way of thinking\, covering traditional
  studio craft materials such as clay\, fiber\, wood\, glass\, metal and st
 one\, and craft-based methods used in new ways\, including handwork combin
 ed with digital tools\, performance or experimental materials. Craft here 
 is defined by deep engagement with process\, materiality and the hand rath
 er than by medium. RESIDENCY EXPECTATIONS: this is a substantial public co
 mmitment\, not quiet studio time. Residents commit to 14 or 21 public open
  studio hours a week on Thursdays\, Fridays and/or Saturdays 10:30am to 5:
 30pm and/or Wednesdays 11:00am to 7:00pm\, on a schedule fixed with MAD's 
 Education Department for the whole six months. During those hours resident
 s host K-12 school groups (in person and virtual) and public tour groups. 
 Because of the contact with schoolchildren\, all selected artists must pas
 s a background check. ELIGIBILITY: an artist or designer with an active st
 udio practice in the applied arts\; a resident of the New York City metro 
 area\, defined as the five boroughs plus nearby New York\, New Jersey and 
 Connecticut counties within reasonable commuting distance\, typically on M
 TA\, PATH or NJ Transit\; at least 21 at the time of application\; and a U
 S citizen or legal US resident with a valid SSN or EIN. The Museum does no
 t sponsor visas and states this is not subject to exception. Programme alu
 mni may not reapply\, though unsuccessful past applicants may. SELECTION: 
 the committee looks for a mature body of work engaging critically with the
  transdisciplinary fields of contemporary craft\, design\, art and technol
 ogy\, a strong conceptual framework\, deep engagement with process and mat
 erials\, a clear direction or question for the residency\, and genuine rea
 diness for the open studios format. Top applications go to interview with 
 the Deputy Director of Education and Manager of Public Programs and Studio
 s\, and finalists' references are contacted. Applicants are strongly encou
 raged to visit the studios in person first. NOT AN EXHIBITION PROGRAMME: r
 esidents may show work in progress in their studio and finished work in th
 e sixth-floor vitrines\, but the residency does not include a gallery exhi
 bition or a proposal for MAD's galleries. STUDIO RESTRICTIONS: no open fla
 mes\, all hot work approved by the Director of Facilities\, no large or un
 safe power tools such as saws\, and no toxic materials including turpentin
 e\, certain resins\, glazes\, lusters and lead-based paints. Applicants su
 bmit one application indicating their preferred schedule and which session
  or sessions they want\, and are selected for one. Initial notifications O
 ctober 2026\, finalist interviews November 2026\, offers December 2026. Qu
 estions: education@madmuseum.org. Deadline 11 September 2026.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Museum of Arts and Design (MAD)\nLocation: Sixth floor of the Museum 
 of Arts and Design\, New York City\, USA. Open to residents of the New Yor
 k City metro area only.\nAward: Stipend based on the on-site public schedu
 le: USD 15\,000 for three public-facing days per week\, or USD 10\,000 for
  two days. Also provided: a dedicated studio of roughly 200 square feet in
  the Museum with worktable\, sink\, storage and Wi-Fi\; a separate stipend
  for materials and mentorship\; professional development and studio visits
  with museum staff\; additional paid public programming opportunities\; ad
 mission to paid public programmes\; and access to MAD's galleries\, public
  programmes and exhibition openings. Shared equipment includes a Skutt 102
 7 kiln\, a Brent pottery wheel and a 22 inch floor loom. MAD does not pay 
 travel costs or provide housing. Residents are one-time independent contra
 ctors and are responsible for their own taxes. Collectives may apply but a
 re paid the individual stipend. APPLICATION FEE: USD 5 per application\, w
 ith fee waivers available on request by emailing education@madmuseum.org b
 efore submitting.\nDuration: Six months. Cycle 46 runs 15 February 2027 to
  15 August 2027\; Cycle 47 runs 16 August 2027 to 13 February 2028.\n\nMor
 e: https://madmuseum.submittable.com/submit/363177/mad-artist-studios-arti
 st-residency-cycle-46-and-47?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
URL:https://madmuseum.submittable.com/submit/363177/mad-artist-studios-arti
 st-residency-cycle-46-and-47?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Sixth floor of the Museum of Arts and Design\, New York City\, USA
 . Open to residents of the New York City metro area only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:laselle-travel-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Travel Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation established this fell
 owship in recognition of the impact travel can have on an artist's career.
  Two awards of USD 5\,000 go to artists working and residing in Texas\, en
 abling each recipient to complete a research trip that furthers their arti
 stic practice. The fellowship echoes its namesake's own history: Dorothy A
 ntoinette 'Toni' LaSelle\, a pioneer of Modernism in Texas\, travelled to 
 Europe in 1927-28 after completing her MA at the University of Chicago\, e
 xploring the art of England\, Italy and France on a trip made possible by 
 a study fund awarded for work in the First Student Art Exhibition at the R
 enaissance Society in 1926. JUROR: this year's guest juror is Lana Meador\
 , Associate Curator of Modern and Contemporary Art at the San Antonio Muse
 um of Art\, who joined SAMA's curatorial department in 2015 and has organi
 sed more than a dozen exhibitions there\, including co-curating Texas Wome
 n: A New History of Abstract Art (2020) and the ongoing Gateway series of 
 commissioned site-specific murals. She has juried artist awards for the Ci
 ty of San Antonio's Department of Arts and Culture\, the International Col
 or Awards\, Texas Sculpture Group and the Utah Artist Fellowship. ELIGIBIL
 ITY: must currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year
 \; must be a practising professional with at least three solo or group exh
 ibitions in the past three years (BFA and juried exhibitions count)\; and 
 must currently hold a degree in the arts. APPLICATION MATERIALS: image thu
 mbnails of a maximum of four works with title\, date\, medium and dimensio
 ns (JPEG or JPG only\, at least 1\,200 pixels on the longest side and unde
 r 5MB each)\; an artist CV\; a reference contact list with two names\, con
 tact details and relationship to the artist\; a project proposal of 2\,000
  characters or less covering why the fellowship matters to your practice\,
  your goals for the travel\, and when the proposed travel would take place
 \; and a copy of a valid Texas driver's licence or other proof of one year
  of Texas residency. All information and communications are in English\, w
 ith translation assistance available on request. Applications opened 3 Aug
 ust 2026\; awardees are notified 2 November 2026. Questions: info@lasellef
 oundation.org. Deadline 31 August 2026 at 11:59pm CDT.\n\nOrganisation: Do
 rothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation\nLocation: Texas\, USA. Applicants mus
 t currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year. The fu
 nded research trip itself may be anywhere.\nAward: Two awards of USD 5\,00
 0 each\, distributed in the fall of 2026\, to fund a research trip that fu
 rthers the artist's practice. No application fee.\nDuration: Travel must b
 e completed between 1 December 2026 and 31 December 2027\n\nMore: https://
 www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Texas\, USA. Applicants must currently reside in Texas and have li
 ved there for the past year. The funded research trip itself may be anywhe
 re.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:south-arts-in-these-mountains-infrastructure-2026@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: In These Mountains: Infrastructure Grants 2026
DESCRIPTION:Part of South Arts' In These Mountains initiative in Central Ap
 palachia\, these grants fund non-programming operational expenses\, the un
 glamorous infrastructure work that project grants rarely cover. Six organi
 zations receive USD 30\,000 each\, two from each eligible state\, with no 
 matching requirement. WHAT IT FUNDS: staff and volunteer professional deve
 lopment\, emergency readiness planning\, minor equipment purchases or soft
 ware upgrades\, staff travel and registration for conferences or research\
 , marketing\, and strategic planning\, among others. Up to 10 percent of t
 he grant may go to overhead and salaries. ELIGIBILITY: nonprofit organizat
 ions with 501(c)(3) status in good standing located in Appalachian Regiona
 l Commission counties in Kentucky\, North Carolina or Tennessee. Colleges 
 and universities are excluded\, as are previous and current In These Mount
 ains Partners from FY19 through FY26. Applications opened 15 July 2026. De
 adline 30 September 2026 at 11:59pm ET.\n\nOrganisation: South Arts\nLocat
 ion: Appalachian Regional Commission counties in Kentucky\, North Carolina
  and Tennessee\, USA.\nAward: USD 30\,000 per organization\, with no match
  required. Two organizations from each of the three eligible states are se
 lected\, for six recipients in total. Up to 10 percent may cover overhead 
 and salaries. No application fee.\nDuration: Organizational capacity and i
 nfrastructure support\n\nMore: https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportunit
 ies/these-mountains-infrastructure-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportunities/these-mountains-infrastr
 ucture-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Appalachian Regional Commission counties in Kentucky\, North Carol
 ina and Tennessee\, USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:south-arts-emerging-traditional-artists-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260831T203817Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Emerging Traditional Artists Program 2026
DESCRIPTION:South Arts' Emerging Traditional Artists Program supports folk 
 and traditional artists in the early stages of their careers with USD 5\,0
 00 each\, plus professional development workshops and peer mentorship at n
 o cost. The money is for learning: training\, networking and promotion. Re
 cipients also record an interview about their art practice. ELIGIBILITY: i
 ndividual practitioners early in their careers\, at least 18 by the deadli
 ne\, US citizens or lawfully authorised residents\, who have lived for at 
 least two years in designated Appalachian Regional Commission or Delta Reg
 ional Authority counties within Alabama\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\,
  Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina or Tennessee\, or who are m
 embers of state or federally recognised Native American tribes. Applicants
  must demonstrate a high level of skill in\, commitment to\, and leadershi
 p in their traditional art form. Deadline 30 September 2026 at 5:00pm ET.\
 n\nOrganisation: South Arts\nLocation: Designated Appalachian Regional Com
 mission and Delta Regional Authority counties in Alabama\, Georgia\, Kentu
 cky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennes
 see\, USA.\nAward: USD 5\,000 per awardee\, paid as a single lump sum at t
 he start of the award period\, to fund learning opportunities including tr
 aining\, networking and promotion. Recipients also take part in virtual wo
 rkshops and peer mentorship at no cost\, and record an interview about the
 ir practice. No application fee.\nDuration: Award period with accompanying
  workshops and peer mentorship\n\nMore: https://www.southarts.org/grants-o
 pportunities/emerging-traditional-artists-program?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportunities/emerging-traditional-art
 ists-program?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Designated Appalachian Regional Commission and Delta Regional Auth
 ority counties in Alabama\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\,
  North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennessee\, USA.
END:VEVENT
